arlier conversation on Ranked Choice voting and agreed with several of them that there are a great number of benefits that Ranked Choice voting can provide voters. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.101956+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9699cb48-a99b-4544-80fe-9a79400481b6", "source_url": "local://04_April/Approved April 21 2025 minutes.pdf", "title": "Approved April 21 2025 minutes", "section_hierarchy": ["04_April"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 299, "text": "Fletcher of their answer once it is available. Mr. Sheldon Fishman recognized the speakers from the earlier conversation on Ranked Choice voting and agreed with several of them that there are a great number of benefits that Ranked Choice voting can provide voters. Mr. Fishman added that he had run the 2018 Montgomery County Green Democrats endorsement selection using Ranked Choice voting, and everyone involved, including the losing candidates, had an enjoyable experience with the voting process. Website Update Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the Board of Elections’ website usage has returned to expected non-election year volume following the 2024 Election Cycle. Mr. Brajkovic explained that the high volume of page views of the Election Inclement Weather Policy was due to inclement weather in the county and that they were likely looking for the main county website or inclement weather updates from MCPS. Website Statistics can be found here. Outreach Update Staff is currently scheduling High School outreach events and other community events and attendance for the spring of 2025 through the end of the school year. Additionally, Dr. Zelaya and staff are working with local organizations who indicate that they would like the Board of Elections to be a part of their events. Mr. Naimon added that on March 20, 2025, he alongside Mr. Brajkovic, Ms. Prudenti, Ms. Mills- Pherigo, and Ms. Gonda, will be speaking at an event for Montgomery Women, a nonpartisan community organization. The list of outreach events for March 2025 through April 2025 can be found here. Future Action Items e Board staff will continue conversations about the duplication process with Mr. Daniels. Approval of Minutes Ms. Hoeber moved to approve the minutes from the Board Meeting on February 3, 2025, as amended. The motion was seconded by Ms. Dillon and passed unanimously.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.101969+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "3f4651f0-e2a4-43eb-a4bd-30340130c2c8", "source_url": "local://04_April/Approved April 21 2025 minutes.pdf", "title": "Approved April 21 2025 minutes", "section_hierarchy": ["04_April"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 125, "text": "Future Action Items e Board staff will continue conversations about the duplication process with Mr. Daniels. Approval of Minutes Ms. Hoeber moved to approve the minutes from the Board Meeting on February 3, 2025, as amended. The motion was seconded by Ms. Dillon and passed unanimously. Future Meetings All meetings begin at 3:00 P.M. unless otherwise indicated. Dates are subject to change. e = April 21, 2025 e May 19, 2025 e June 16, 2025 e = =6August 4, 2025 e September 15, 2025 e October 20, 2025 « November 17, 2025 e December 15, 2025 Adjournment Mr. Naimon adjourned the Board Meeting at 6:24 P.M. Approved by the Board, D ccc H f (| ain ON David A. Naimon Board President 4915-4917-2017, v. 2", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.101976+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a8384662-6415-4e6b-96db-4a351a3432d0", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 287, "text": "MEETING MINUTES MONDAY, MAY 19, 2025 MONTGOMERY COUNTY BOARD OF ELECTIONS/BOARD OF CANVASSERS In-Person Attendance Board Members: David A. Naimon, President Alexander Vincent, Board Member Margie C. Delao, Substitute Member Lawrence Halloran, Substitute Member Virtual Attendance Board Members: Daniel A. Koroma, Vice President Amoretta (Amie) M. Hoeber, Secretary Keyna I. Anyiam, Board Member In-Person Attendance Board Attorney: Kevin Karpinski In-Person Staff Attendance: Boris Brajkovic, Director Julia Douglass, Lead Trainer Michelle Gonda, Vote By Mail Program Manager Lisa Merino-Dean, Administrative Specialist Aishah Mills-Pherigo, Election Worker Recruitment Coordinator Sandra Prudenti, Voter Services Manager Janet Ross, IT/Training Manager Mindy Williams, Operations Manager Gilberto Zelaya, Outreach Coordinator/Public Information Officer In-Person Guest Attendance: Curtis Antonucci Anita Cox Mary Elcano Ann Hingston Lorraine Jaffe Dennis Melby Brigitta Mullican Reardon Sullivan Amy Waychoff A video of the meeting may be found on YouTube at the link below: https://youtu.be/BFhj8ehLe9s?si=O9bQumzdrgNkH1js Swearing-In of New Substitute Member of the Board of Elections (1:50) Lawrence Halloran was nominated by the Montgomery County Republican Central Committee and appointed by Governor Wes Moore to fill the vacancy of the minority party Substitute Board Member of the Montgomery County Board of Elections. The Clerk of the Court, Karen Bushell, administered the oath of office. Mr. Halloran and other Board Members’ comments can be found here. (5:06) Lawrence Halloran (8:11) David A. Naimon (11:38) Amoretta (Amie) M. Hoeber (13:50) Daniel A. Koroma (14:21) Alexander Vincent (15:09) Margie Delao (15:33) Keyna I. Anyiam Convene Board of Canvassers Meeting (17:56) Mr. Naimon called the meeting of the Board of Canvassers (which includes the six remaining Board members who were sworn in for the Board of Canvassers for the 2024 general election but does not include Mr. Halloran) to order and declared a quorum present. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351292+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9c4730d3-0416-498f-ad3a-80a4c63c9085", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 277, "text": "Anyiam Convene Board of Canvassers Meeting (17:56) Mr. Naimon called the meeting of the Board of Canvassers (which includes the six remaining Board members who were sworn in for the Board of Canvassers for the 2024 general election but does not include Mr. Halloran) to order and declared a quorum present. Mr. Brajkovic presented four untimely mail-in ballots from the 2024 Presidential General Election that were received at the Board of Elections after the mandated deadline of 10:00 A.M. on November 15, 2024. • Two ballots were sent from within the United States, were postmarked after Election Day (11/5/2024), and arrived untimely. • Two ballots were sent from within the United States, arrived untimely, and did not have a postmark with date information. Mr. Vincent moved to reject the four untimely mail-in ballots. The motion was seconded by Ms. Delao and passed unanimously. Mr. Naimon adjourned the meeting of the Board of Canvassers. Convene Board of Elections Meeting (21:08) Mr. Naimon called the meeting of the Board of Elections to order and declared a quorum present. President’s Remarks (21:11) Mr. Naimon announced that he joined Governor Wes Moore at the Tuesday, May 6, 2025 bill signing ceremony, for the signing into law of two pieces of election legislation from the 2025 Maryland General Assembly that were proposed by our Board of Elections. These pieces of legislation include the change of the Gubernatorial Primary Election date to the fourth Tuesday in June. Mr. Naimon clarified that, as a result of this legislation, the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary Election will take place on Tuesday, June 23, 2026, with Early Voting held from Thursday, June 11 through Thursday, June 18, 2026.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351314+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "423fc809-79a3-4ae6-b834-416d7d506d0c", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 298, "text": "These pieces of legislation include the change of the Gubernatorial Primary Election date to the fourth Tuesday in June. Mr. Naimon clarified that, as a result of this legislation, the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary Election will take place on Tuesday, June 23, 2026, with Early Voting held from Thursday, June 11 through Thursday, June 18, 2026. Additionally, Governor Moore signed into law legislation that requires local boards to count votes for the Board of Education contests on provisional ballots that would otherwise have been rejected due to the voters’ ballot not matching their party affiliation listed in the voter registry. Mr. Naimon congratulated Mr. Koroma for his feature in a chapter of a new book entitled, African Roots American Dreams, by author Dehab Ghebreab. Additions/Changes to the Agenda (23:41) Kevin Karpinski, Board Attorney, requested that a closed executive session be added to the end of the public meeting. Disclosure of Campaign Contributions and Fundraiser Attendance (24:42) No disclosures were made. Public Comments (24:50) No public comments were submitted. Election Director’s Report (25:22) The Election Director’s Report can be found here. Mr. Brajkovic reported that the State Board of Elections adopted two policies at its Board Meeting on April 29, 2025. The first policy pertains to voters who require assistance at polling places. Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that all required actions are already in place in Montgomery County. The second adopted policy is the updated Obituary Policy, which clarifies the sources from which obituaries may be obtained to perform Voter Registration List Maintenance. Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that hiring processes are underway for the Nursing Homes and Mail-In Ballot Specialist positions, as well as the Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager position. Onboarding for the Nursing Homes and Mail-In Ballot Specialist is expected by the end of May 2025.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351327+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "11d7e091-334f-4147-b709-3a98d5cd8dea", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 290, "text": "Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that hiring processes are underway for the Nursing Homes and Mail-In Ballot Specialist positions, as well as the Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager position. Onboarding for the Nursing Homes and Mail-In Ballot Specialist is expected by the end of May 2025. Onboarding for the Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager is expected in June 2025. Mr. Brajkovic noted that the Budget Expenditure Report that was shared with the Board includes last month’s spending. Mr. Brajkovic anticipates that the department will be in a surplus at the end of FY25, including the anticipated billings from the State Board of Elections for the third and fourth quarters. Mr. Brajkovic added that the Voter Services section is working with municipalities that are conducting elections this year by providing them with voter registration lists upon request. A full list of 2025 municipal elections and election dates can be found in the Director’s Report available on the website under the meeting documents for this Board Meeting and linked above. Mr. Naimon requested that a communication be sent to all municipalities within Montgomery County, requesting that they send their election results to the State Board of Elections for publishing on the website. Mr. Vincent asked why the State Board of Elections' final invoice for the third and fourth quarter of FY25 is not expected until after the end of FY25. Me. Brajkovic responded that the third quarter bill has been paid but was not reflected on the prepared document. Mr. Halloran asked about the status of the procurement for new Electronic Pollbooks. Mr. Brajkovic responded that the procurement process is underway at the State Board of Elections, and it is anticipated that the process will be completed for the 2028 Election Cycle.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351340+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b1d4f216-e260-43a3-8c51-aaba3f83e882", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 299, "text": "Mr. Halloran asked about the status of the procurement for new Electronic Pollbooks. Mr. Brajkovic responded that the procurement process is underway at the State Board of Elections, and it is anticipated that the process will be completed for the 2028 Election Cycle. The plan is to utilize the Legacy Electronic Pollbooks for the 2026 Election Cycle. Ms. Delao asked where the surplus of our budget goes at the end of the fiscal year. Mr. Brajkovic responded that the surplus is directed to the Montgomery County General Fund. Board Attorney’s Report (46:20) Mr. Karpinski updated the Board and Staff on the lawsuits regarding the 2020 and 2022 elections. Both the 2020 and 2022 cases have been decided by the United States Court of Appeals for the 4th Circuit, and no writ of certiorari petitions were filed before the deadline. The litigation hold on 2020 and 2022 election materials has been lifted and shredding of old materials may commence. Old Business (47:27) There was no old business discussed. Website Update (47:36) Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the Board of Elections’ website usage continues to reflect normal usage in a non-election year following the 2024 Election Cycle. Website Statistics can be found here. Outreach Update (49:13) Dr. Zelaya informed the Board that the staff continues to participate in High School outreach events through the end of the school year, as well as other community events throughout the summer of 2025. The list of outreach events for May and June 2025 can be found here. New Business – (51:55) Discussion of Election Day Polling Place Locations for the 2026 Election Cycle (52:51) Mr. Brajkovic and Ms. Williams presented the staff proposal of Election Day Polling Place location changes for the 2026 election cycle. The full presentation can be found here. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351351+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1f9be4c0-6b89-4c3c-b811-0edb95499532", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 287, "text": "The list of outreach events for May and June 2025 can be found here. New Business – (51:55) Discussion of Election Day Polling Place Locations for the 2026 Election Cycle (52:51) Mr. Brajkovic and Ms. Williams presented the staff proposal of Election Day Polling Place location changes for the 2026 election cycle. The full presentation can be found here. Mr. Brajkovic indicated that per SB259 from the 2025 General Assembly Legislative Session, local boards of elections are required to submit a comprehensive election plan to the State Board of Elections for approval. This election plan will consist of the proposed drop box locations, Election Day polling places, and early voting centers approved by the local board of elections. Mr. Brajkovic added that if approved by the Board, the presented list of Election Day polling place locations would constitute the second part of the holistic Election Plan for the 2026 Gubernatorial Election Cycle. Ms. Williams noted that local boards of elections must maintain a list of individuals and organizations to be notified about local board meetings at which proposed changes to the location of polling places will be discussed. A notification was emailed to 275 email addresses on May 5, 2025. Mr. Halloran asked how someone could sign up for this email notification. Mr. Brajkovic responded that there is a link to sign up for the polling place change notification on the Board of Elections’ website under the tab entitled ‘Board Meeting’. Mr. Naimon requested that all board members be added to the list of emails to receive the polling place change communications. Ms. Williams proposed a slate of 229 Election Day polling places serving 257 Precincts. This includes 7 polling place changes from the 2024 Election Cycle.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351362+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a7777c02-5337-4c7d-b6b8-8108594d7fa9", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 284, "text": "Mr. Naimon requested that all board members be added to the list of emails to receive the polling place change communications. Ms. Williams proposed a slate of 229 Election Day polling places serving 257 Precincts. This includes 7 polling place changes from the 2024 Election Cycle. These changes are predominantly caused by construction at locations that served as polling places in 2024. The proposed changes are as follows: 1. Precinct 01-07, previously held at Damascus Library & Senior Center, will be consolidated into Laytonsville Elementary School, currently housing precincts 01-01 and 12-06. 2. Precinct 02-06, previously held at Hallie Wells Middle School, will be moved to Snowden Farm Elementary School to be within the assigned precinct. 3. Precinct 02-12, previously held at Snowden Farm Elementary School, will be moved to Hallie Wells Middle School to be within the assigned precinct. 4. Precinct 10-09, previously held at Our Lady of Mercy Catholic School, will be temporarily consolidated into Seven Locks Elementary School, currently housing precinct 10-03, due to construction. 5. Precinct 13-07, previously held at the IMAAM Center, will be moved to Woodlin Elementary School as school construction was completed. 6. Precinct 13-50, previously held at Sligo Creek ES, will be moved to Silver Spring International Middle School, which is located in the same building, to improve accessibility and increase parking. 7. Precinct 13-13, previously held at Highland View Elementary School, will be temporarily consolidated into Silver Spring International Middle School, housing precinct 13-50. Mr. Naimon stated that this small number of proposed polling place changes is ideal and that he is grateful that legislation changing the Gubernatorial Primary Election date helped minimize the number of polling place changes by avoiding some school construction. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351372+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e92ec728-1a51-45a8-8ea2-4ffce1a6cf52", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 279, "text": "Mr. Naimon stated that this small number of proposed polling place changes is ideal and that he is grateful that legislation changing the Gubernatorial Primary Election date helped minimize the number of polling place changes by avoiding some school construction. Mr. Brajkovic added that all affected registered voters living within the precincts that will have their polling place location changed will receive a new Voter Notification Card issued and mailed to them on colored paper to indicate the change in voting location. The Board plans to vote on the proposed Election Day polling places for the 2026 Gubernatorial Election Cycle at the June 16, 2025, Board Meeting. Election Workers’ Stipends – Staff Proposal and Board Action (1:35:10) Mr. Brajkovic presented the proposed stipend amounts for each Election Worker and Election Judge service position. The list of proposed stipend amounts for the 2026 Gubernatorial Election Cycle can be found here. The proposal includes one change compared to the 2024 election cycle in the stipend amount for the Nursing Home volunteers. The proposed stipend amount for Nursing Home volunteers is raised to equal the stipend amounts for Canvass volunteers. The proposed stipend amounts for all other positions remain the same from the 2024 Presidential Election Cycle. Mr. Vincent moved to approve the list of stipend amounts for Election Workers and Election Judges, including one change, for the 2026 Gubernatorial Election Cycle. The motion was seconded by Mr. Halloran and passed unanimously. Proposal on the Creation of a Subcommittee on Web Delivered Ballots (1:38:35) Ms. Anyiam proposed to the Board the creation of a subcommittee focusing on voter outreach regarding the process by which voters can obtain and return web-delivered ballots. Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351382+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e8f0c8d6-d9e1-48e3-8f64-7af33ad3cc38", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 286, "text": "The motion was seconded by Mr. Halloran and passed unanimously. Proposal on the Creation of a Subcommittee on Web Delivered Ballots (1:38:35) Ms. Anyiam proposed to the Board the creation of a subcommittee focusing on voter outreach regarding the process by which voters can obtain and return web-delivered ballots. Ms. Anyiam added that, given the difficulties and misunderstandings regarding the full process of the use of web-delivered ballots, it would be valuable to look at the ways the Board can help educate voters more fully on how to complete the full voting process with a web-delivered ballot. Mr. Naimon agreed that this subcommittee is a valuable idea, and the creation of the subcommittee can help further the goals of the Board regarding educating voters on web- delivered ballots. Mr. Naimon appointed Ms. Anyiam and Mr. Halloran to serve on the Board subcommittee on web-delivered ballots. Mr. Naimon requested that the subcommittee present its report and suggestions at the August 4, 2025, Board Meeting. Future Action Items (1:54:54) • Staff will send a notification to the municipalities holding elections in 2025, reminding them to communicate to the State Board of Elections the 2025 election results. • Instructions for signing up to receive notifications regarding Board meetings where polling place changes will be discussed will be posted on the main page of the Board of Elections website, 777Vote.org. • All Board Members will be added to the notification distribution list for polling place change meetings. Approval of Minutes (1:56:00) Mrs. Hoeber moved to approve the minutes from the Board Meeting on April 21, 2025, as amended. The motion was seconded by Ms. Delao and passed unanimously. Future Meetings (1:57:10) All meetings begin at 3:00 P.M. unless otherwise indicated.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351392+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b6489d22-15a2-4900-b245-b2989b95a029", "source_url": "local://05_May/Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note.pdf", "title": "Approved May Minutes 2025 signature note", "section_hierarchy": ["05_May"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 10, "word_count": 176, "text": "Approval of Minutes (1:56:00) Mrs. Hoeber moved to approve the minutes from the Board Meeting on April 21, 2025, as amended. The motion was seconded by Ms. Delao and passed unanimously. Future Meetings (1:57:10) All meetings begin at 3:00 P.M. unless otherwise indicated. Dates are subject to change. • Monday, June 16, 2025 • Monday, August 4, 2025 • Monday, September 15, 2025 • Monday, October 20, 2025 Adjournment (1:58:32) Ms. Anyiam moved to adjourn the public meeting of the Board of Elections and move into an executive session to discuss a matter with the Board Attorney. The motion was seconded by Mr. Vincent and passed unanimously. The public meeting of the Board of Elections was adjourned at 5:06 P.M. Report on Executive Session The Montgomery County Board of Elections moved into an executive session at 5:06 P.M., pursuant to §3-305(b)(2), General Provisions Article, to obtain legal advice. The Executive Session was adjourned at 6:02 P.M. Approved by the Board, Board President's signature appears on the original document David A. Naimon Board President 4902-1574-3048, v. 1", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.351400+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "009e3b09-5c18-4f5e-8be0-1cda2a348eab", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 291, "text": "MEETING MINUTES MONDAY, JUNE 16, 2025 MONTGOMERY COUNTY BOARD OF ELECTIONS Virtual Attendance Board Members: David A. Naimon, President Daniel A. Koroma, Vice President Amoretta (Amie) M. Hoeber, Secretary Keyna I. Anyiam, Board Member Alexander Vincent, Board Member Margie C. Delao, Substitute Member Lawrence J. Halloran, Substitute Member Virtual Attendance Board Attorney: Kevin Karpinski In-Person Staff Attendance: Boris Brajkovic, Election Director Julia Douglass, Lead Trainer Philip Jackson, Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager Aishah Mills-Pherigo, Election Worker Recruitment Coordinator Sandra Prudenti, Voter Services Manager Janet Ross, Voting Systema and IT Manager Mindy Williams, Elections Operations Manager Gilberto Zelaya, Outreach Coordinator/Public Information Officer A video of the meeting may be found on YouTube at the link below: https://youtu.be/iCm39EdQzuI?si=_TNTQrbA01MJyfiW Convene Board of Elections Meeting (0:03) Mr. Naimon called the meeting of the Board of Elections to order and declared a quorum present at 3:00 p.m. President’s Remarks (00:22) Mr. Naimon recognized Ms. Anyiam and Mr. Vincent, who both celebrated birthdays following the May Board Meeting, as well as Mr. Koroma, who will be celebrating his birthday in the upcoming week. Mr. Naimon also noted that he anticipates that the August 4, 2025, Board Meeting will be an in-person meeting, held at the Board of Elections’ office in Gaithersburg. Additions/Changes to the Agenda (2:37) No additions or changes to the agenda were requested. Disclosure of Campaign Contributions and Fundraiser Attendance (2:46) No disclosures were made. Public Comments (3:03) No public comments were submitted. Election Director’s Report (3:55) The Election Director’s Report can be found here. Mr. Brajkovic announced and congratulated Ms. Jennifer Daley on her new position as the Nursing Homes and Vote-By-Mail Canvassing Specialist. Additionally, Mr. Brajkovic announced and welcomed Mr. Philip Jackson to his role as the Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655100+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f40ed6b1-7ff1-44b3-9f41-319681ea02d8", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 280, "text": "Public Comments (3:03) No public comments were submitted. Election Director’s Report (3:55) The Election Director’s Report can be found here. Mr. Brajkovic announced and congratulated Ms. Jennifer Daley on her new position as the Nursing Homes and Vote-By-Mail Canvassing Specialist. Additionally, Mr. Brajkovic announced and welcomed Mr. Philip Jackson to his role as the Outreach, Recruitment, and Training Manager. Mr. Brajkovic updated the Board that one of the remaining quarterly bills from the State Board of Elections has been received, processed, and is reflected in the May Expenditure Report. Additionally, Mr. Brajkovic reported that he still anticipates that the department will be in a surplus at the end of Fiscal Year 2025, including the remaining bill from the State Board of Elections for the fourth quarter. Mr. Brajkovic added that the Voter Services section is working with municipalities that are conducting elections this year by providing them with voter registration lists upon request. A full list of 2025 municipal elections and election dates can be found in the Director’s Report available on the website under the meeting documents for this Board Meeting and linked above. Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that, according to a letter from the Department of Justice, the Board of Elections and all other County Agencies are working on a website update project to meet the new ADA requirements and standards. Mr. Brajkovic reported that on June 4, 2025, a representative from a Montgomery Conty’s partner region in South Korea visited the Montgomery County Board of Elections to learn more about our election administration process. This visit included a tour of the facility and a presentation on current practices used by the Board of Elections. Additionally, Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655122+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0c42cdca-1fd1-46ce-a179-b10d6bbee49b", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 287, "text": "Mr. Brajkovic reported that on June 4, 2025, a representative from a Montgomery Conty’s partner region in South Korea visited the Montgomery County Board of Elections to learn more about our election administration process. This visit included a tour of the facility and a presentation on current practices used by the Board of Elections. Additionally, Mr. Brajkovic and the Management team visited the Baltimore County Board of Elections to participate in an Emergency Management tabletop exercise to collaborate on strategies to ensure readiness in the event of any emergencies arising during an election. Board Attorney’s Report (14:41) Kevin Karpinski, Board Attorney, reported that recently the Montgomery County Board of Canvassers, as well as several other Maryland local Boards of Canvassers, received an Open Meetings Act Complaint, on the grounds that the canvass minutes were not being posted on the Board of Elections’ website. Mr. Karpinski added that a response to the complaint, arguing that there has not been a violation, has been filed with the Open Meetings Compliance Board. Additionally, canvass minutes, as well as all livestream recordings of the 2024 elections canvass sessions, have been posted on the Board of Elections’ website in a continued effort to promote transparency in Montgomery County elections. Mr. Karpinski anticipates that a decision will be made before the August Board meeting regarding the response that was filed to the complaint. Mr. Karpinski updated the Board on the recent decision from the United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts, which blocked several provisions of President Trump’s Executive Order 14248, “Preserving and Protecting the Integrity of American Elections.” Several States, including the State of Maryland, joined the State of Massachusetts in the Lawsuit against President Trump’s Executive Order 14248. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655134+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "94c47d4c-f6de-492a-acff-43c3f5cd49ed", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 289, "text": "Mr. Karpinski updated the Board on the recent decision from the United States District Court for the District of Massachusetts, which blocked several provisions of President Trump’s Executive Order 14248, “Preserving and Protecting the Integrity of American Elections.” Several States, including the State of Maryland, joined the State of Massachusetts in the Lawsuit against President Trump’s Executive Order 14248. Mr. Karpinski anticipates that this case will reach the Supreme Court quickly, and there will be a definitive decision on which provisions of the Executive Order, if any, will apply to the State of Maryland for the 2026 Election Cycle. Website Update (21:14) Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the Board of Elections’ website usage continues to reflect normal usage in a non-election year following the 2024 Election Cycle. Website Statistics can be found here. Outreach Update (22:16) The list of outreach events for May through July 2025 can be found here. Dr. Zelaya informed the Board that following the end of the school year, staff has been busy participating in various community events and festivals for their summer outreach activities. Dr. Zelaya encourages the public to contact the Board of Elections with any inquiries requesting our participation in a community event. Mr. Naimon asked Dr. Zelaya for clarification on what constitutes a virtual outreach event. Dr. Zelaya informed the Board that a virtual outreach event is largely tailored to the request of the organization that is holding the event, including presentations on how to vote in Montgomery County, information on how to become an election worker, and information on becoming a trained voter registrar. Old Business (29:57) Proposal of Election Day Polling Place Locations for the 2026 Election Cycle – Further Discussion and Board Action Mr. Brajkovic and Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655145+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b6936475-9e26-439c-9fbe-939aa1b2dce9", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 300, "text": "Old Business (29:57) Proposal of Election Day Polling Place Locations for the 2026 Election Cycle – Further Discussion and Board Action Mr. Brajkovic and Ms. Williams' presentation of the staff proposal for Election Day Polling Places changes for the 2026 election cycle can be found here. Mr. Brajkovic indicated that per SB259 from the 2025 General Assembly Legislative Session, which will take effect on July 1, local boards of elections will be required to submit a comprehensive election plan to the State Board of Elections for approval. This election plan will consist of the proposed drop box locations, election day polling places, and early voting centers approved by the local board of elections. Mr. Brajkovic added that if approved by the Board, the presented list of election day polling place locations will constitute the second part of the holistic Election Plan for the 2026 Gubernatorial Election Cycle. Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the staff’s polling place change proposal for precinct 12-01 was no longer viable due to the proposed location no longer being able to hold Election Day activities. Mr. Brajkovic added that a new proposal for the relocation of 12-01 will be presented to the Board at the August Board Meeting. Ms. Williams reviewed all of the proposed polling place changes that were presented at the May Board Meeting. The proposed changes are as follows: 1. Precinct 01-07, previously held at Damascus Library & Senior Center, will be consolidated into Laytonsville Elementary School, currently housing precincts 01-01 and 12-06. 2. Precinct 02-06, previously held at Hallie Wells Middle School, will be moved to Snowden Farm Elementary School to be within the assigned precinct. 3. Precinct 02-12, previously held at Snowden Farm Elementary School, will be moved to Hallie Wells Middle School to be within the assigned precinct. 4.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655156+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c9a1351e-543a-40ab-b859-e2af99ee5e17", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 287, "text": "Precinct 02-06, previously held at Hallie Wells Middle School, will be moved to Snowden Farm Elementary School to be within the assigned precinct. 3. Precinct 02-12, previously held at Snowden Farm Elementary School, will be moved to Hallie Wells Middle School to be within the assigned precinct. 4. Precinct 10-09, previously held at Our Lady of Mercy Catholic School, will be temporarily consolidated into Seven Locks Elementary School, currently housing precinct 10-03, due to construction at Our Lady of Mercy. 5. Precinct 13-07, previously held at the IMAAM Center, will be moved to Woodlin Elementary School as school construction was completed. 6. Precinct 13-50, previously held at Sligo Creek ES, will be moved to Silver Spring International Middle School, which is located in the same building, to improve accessibility and increase parking. 7. Precinct 13-13, previously held at Highland View Elementary School, will be temporarily consolidated into Silver Spring International Middle School, also housing precinct 13-50. Mr. Vincent asked if the Board will need to notify the State Board of Elections and the State Administrator of Elections that precinct 12-01 will need to be relocated, despite not having a proposed polling place location for that precinct yet. Mr. Brajkovic responded that because of the staff and the Board are working far ahead of schedule for the Election Plan, prospective polling place changes can be presented up until the deadline for the Election Plan in November, without needing to inform the State now of any changes. Mr. Vincent asked for clarification on which polling place changes would be voted on today. Mr. Naimon stated that the Board would be voting on all polling place changes except the 12-01 polling place change. Ms. Anyiam congratulated Mr. Brajkovic and Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655166+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8cd1990f-4365-4dc6-b985-6b252fa05529", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 292, "text": "Mr. Vincent asked for clarification on which polling place changes would be voted on today. Mr. Naimon stated that the Board would be voting on all polling place changes except the 12-01 polling place change. Ms. Anyiam congratulated Mr. Brajkovic and Ms. Williams on planning ahead of the deadline for the Election Plan, effectively communicating with the community, and allowing the community to provide their input on the proposed polling place changes. Ms. Hoeber asked about the status of Westland Middle School as a polling place, given the recent school closure due to a tree falling on the school building. Mr. Naimon responded that since the 2026 election is still a year away, it is assumed that the school will be fixed in time. Nevertheless, the staff will monitor the progress of any repairs to ensure that the polling place is operational for Election Day. Mr. Vincent moved to accept the staff’s proposal for polling place changes for precincts 01-07, 02-06, 02- 12, 10-09, 13-07, 13-13, and 13-50. The motion was seconded by Ms. Delao and passed unanimously. Board action on the proposed polling place change of precinct 12-01 has been postponed to a future Board Meeting. New Business (46:10) Proposal of the Early Voting Sites for the 2026 Election Cycle – Discussion and Board Action (46:13) The staff proposal for Early Voting sites for the 2026 Election Cycle is available here. Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the staff proposal for Early Voting sites for the 2026 Election Cycle is the third component of the Election Plan that would be submitted to the State Board of Elections for final approval. Mr. Brajkovic added that the proposal presentation was posted on the Board of Elections’ website two weeks before the meeting. Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655176+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ac591eac-365e-4b88-a3e5-5ca2300fe9d1", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 215, "text": "Mr. Brajkovic informed the Board that the staff proposal for Early Voting sites for the 2026 Election Cycle is the third component of the Election Plan that would be submitted to the State Board of Elections for final approval. Mr. Brajkovic added that the proposal presentation was posted on the Board of Elections’ website two weeks before the meeting. Ms. Williams presented the staff proposal for Early Voting sites for the 2026 Election Cycle, which consists of the same 14 Early Voting sites and the same 3 Early Voting alternate site used during the 2024 Election Cycle. The proposed Early Voting sites for the 2026 Election Cycle are as follows: • Activity Center at Bohrer Park 506 S. Frederick Ave., Gaithersburg, MD 20877 • Bauer Drive Community Recreation Center 14625 Bauer Dr., Rockville, MD 20853 • Damascus Community Recreation Center 25520 Oak Dr., Damascus, MD 20872 • Germantown Community Recreation Center 18905 Kingsview Rd., Germantown, MD 20874 • Isiah “Ike” Leggett Executive Office Building 101 Monroe St., Rockville, MD 20850 • Jane E. Lawton Community Recreation Center 4301 Willow Ln., Chevy Chase, MD 20815 • Marilyn J. Praisner Community Recreation Center 14906 Old Columbia Pike, Burtonsville, MD 20866 • Mid-County Community Recreation Center 2004 Queensguard Rd., Silver Spring, MD 20906 • Nancy H. Dacek N.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655185+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "892f4d7d-5f65-4b7e-b73b-5fc03832eec3", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 272, "text": "Lawton Community Recreation Center 4301 Willow Ln., Chevy Chase, MD 20815 • Marilyn J. Praisner Community Recreation Center 14906 Old Columbia Pike, Burtonsville, MD 20866 • Mid-County Community Recreation Center 2004 Queensguard Rd., Silver Spring, MD 20906 • Nancy H. Dacek N. Potomac Community Recreation Center 13850 Travilah Rd., Rockville, MD 20850 • Potomac Community Recreation Center 11315 Falls Rd., Potomac, MD 20854 • Sandy Spring Volunteer Fire Department 17921 Brooke Rd., Sandy Spring, MD 20860 • Silver Spring Civic Building One Veterans Pl., Silver Spring, MD 20910 • Wheaton Library & Community Recreation Center 11701 Georgia Ave., Wheaton, MD 20902 • White Oak Community Recreation Center 1700 April Ln., Silver Spring, MD 20904 Alternate Early Voting sites: • East County Community Recreation Center 3310 Gateshead Manor Way, Silver Spring, MD 20904 • Longwood Community Recreation Center 19300 Georgia Avenue, Brookeville, MD 20833 • Upper County Community Recreation Center 8201 Emory Grove Road, Gaithersburg, MD 20877 Mr. Naimon began the Board discussion, mentioning that he appreciates the continued split use of the Social Halls and Gyms of White Oak Community Recreation Center and Wheaton Library & Community Recreation Center to be respectful of the needs of our partnering agency of the Montgomery County Department of Recreation. Mr. Naimon also appreciates the continuity of the staff proposal of the use of the same 14 Early Voting centers as used in the 2024 Election Cycle. Mr. Naimon added that Maryland Law requires Montgomery County to have 13 Early Voting locations and allows one additional Early Voting location per a mutual agreement with the Board of Elections, the County Executive, and the County Council. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655195+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8857f9c9-cd8e-44ff-8696-de790aa98c82", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 279, "text": "Mr. Naimon added that Maryland Law requires Montgomery County to have 13 Early Voting locations and allows one additional Early Voting location per a mutual agreement with the Board of Elections, the County Executive, and the County Council. Mr. Naimon thanked the County Executive and the County Council for supporting the Board of Elections’ request for the use of one additional Early Voting location during the 2022 and 2024 Election Cycles and asked for that same support in the 2026 election cycle. Ms. Hoeber emphasized the difference between Early Voting Centers and Election Day Polling Places, as Montgomery County voters can choose to vote at any Early Voting Center in the county, yet not voting at their assigned polling place on Election Day will cause the voter to need to vote a provisional ballot. Mr. Koroma added that the staff and the Board are actively trying to make the voting process more accessible by maximizing the number of locations available to Montgomery County voters. Mr. Vincent asked if the Board would need to request additional funding from the County Council for the use of a fourteenth Early Voting Center. Mr. Brajkovic responded that these funds were already planned as part of the Board of Elections’ Fiscal Year 2026 budget, and no additional funding is needed for one additional Early Voting Center. Mr. Koroma moved to accept the staff proposal of the use of the same 14 Early Voting Centers and 3 Alternate Early Voting Centers for the 2026 Election Cycle. The motion was seconded by Ms. Hoeber and passed unanimously. Request to the County Executive and County Council for the Approval of a 14th Early Voting Site (1:13:00) Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655205+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "742c5126-8b47-4d63-ae29-3ec1849d689c", "source_url": "local://06_June/Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting.pdf", "title": "Approved June 16 2025 minutes for Posting", "section_hierarchy": ["06_June"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 268, "text": "Mr. Koroma moved to accept the staff proposal of the use of the same 14 Early Voting Centers and 3 Alternate Early Voting Centers for the 2026 Election Cycle. The motion was seconded by Ms. Hoeber and passed unanimously. Request to the County Executive and County Council for the Approval of a 14th Early Voting Site (1:13:00) Ms. Hoeber moved that the Board of Elections request that the County Executive and the County Council agree to reestablish a fourteenth Early Voting site. The motion was seconded by Ms. Anyiam and passed unanimously. Future Action Items (1:13:33) • Staff will follow up with MCPS and Westland Middle School staff regarding the condition of the building. • The new staff proposal for the polling place change of precinct 12-01 is anticipated to be presented at the August Board Meeting. Approval of Minutes (1:14:42) Ms. Hoeber moved to approve the minutes from the Board Meeting on May 19, 2025, as amended. The motion was seconded by Mr. Vincent and passed unanimously. Future Meetings (1:15:35) All meetings begin at 3:00 P.M. unless otherwise indicated. Dates are subject to change. • Monday, August 4, 2025 In-person, located at the Montgomery County Board of Elections, 18753 N. Frederick Ave. Ste. 210, Gaithersburg, MD 20879. Livestream of the Board Meeting will also be available; details can be found at the Board of Elections’ website, 777Vote.org. • Monday, September 15, 2025 • Monday, October 20, 2025 Adjournment (1:16:33) Mr. Naimon adjourned the Board of Elections meeting at 4:16 p.m. Approved by the Board, Board President’s signature appears on the original document David A. Naimon Board President", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.655215+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b75af9e3-9b72-4011-b906-513c13868c7b", "source_url": "local://2026-01/01_Agenda.pdf", "title": "01_Agenda", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 400, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nState Board of Elections’ Meeting\n151 West Street, Suite 200, Annapolis, MD 21401\nJanuary 22, 2026 1:00 p.m. Virtual\n1. Declaration of Quorum Present\n2. Additions to the Agenda\n3. Approval of Minutes\na. Open Meeting December 18, 2025\nb. Closed Meeting December 18, 2025\n4. State Administrator’s Report\n5. Assistant Attorney General’s Report\n6. Title 13 Waiver Requests\n7. Title 14 Waiver Requests\n8. Administrative Closure\n9. Final Regulations\na. 33.02.03.05 Election Judges' Training Programs\nb. 33.04.02.01-.04 & .06 Inspection and Copying of Public Records; Confidentiality of\nCertain Information\nc. 33.07.04.02 Election Day Activities; Order and Decorum – Cell Phones, Pagers,\nCameras\nd. 33.07.07.01-.03 Election Day Activities; Challengers and Watchers\ne. 33.07.11.02 Compensation of Election Judges\nf. 33.07.12 Election Day Activities; Language Assistance Program\ng. 33.08.01.02 Canvassing, Definitions; General Provisions – Board Responsibilities\nh. 33.08.01.02-1 Canvassing, Definitions; General Provisions – Board Counsel\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401\n\nSBE Board Meeting Agenda\nJanuary 22, 2026\nPage 2 of 2\ni. 33.11.03.08 Absentee Ballots; Issuance and Return – When Ballots are Timely\nj. 33.16.05.03 Provisional Ballots; Canvassing of Ballots – Disposition - Disposition\nof Provisional Ballot Applications\nk. 33.21.03.04 Special Elections by Mail; Issuance and Return – Transmission of\nBallots\n10. Language Assistance Approval for Anne Arundel, Baltimore County, Baltimore City, Prince\nGeorge’s County, Montgomery County\n11. Election Plan Approvals\na. Anne Arundel\nb. Baltimore City\nc. Baltimore County\nd. Frederick\ne. Prince George’s\n12. Policy 2025-04 - Election Judge Parity Report Policy\n13. Old Business\na. Policy 2025-03 - PSR Request Policy\n14. New Business\n15. Disclosure of Campaign Contributions\n16. Schedule Next Meeting\n17. Closed Meeting\n18. Adjournment\nClosed Meeting: Part of the meeting may be closed in accordance with Open Meetings Act procedures.\nPublic Participation: Members of the public may address the Board. Pursuant to §3.2B of the Board’s bylaws,\npublic participation at a meeting must be pre-scheduled and pre-approved by the Chairman. To request approval\nto speak at a board meeting, contact Shermaine Walker at 410-269-2840 or by email info.sbe@maryland.gov no\nlater than 5 pm the day before the meeting.\nLive Streaming: A link to watch the meeting will be available on SBE’s website shortly before the meeting starts.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.721867+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8cffa18b-ba91-4a7e-a98d-2e77f48c7d87", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 293, "text": "Attendees: (Via Conference Call) Michael Summers, Chair Jim Shalleck, Vice Chair Diane Butler, Member Victoria Jackson-Stanley, Member Dan Kobrin, Assistant Attorney General Jared DeMarinis, State Administrator Katherine Berry, Deputy Administrator Melissia Dorsey, Assistant Deputy of Election Policy and Voter Operations Morgan Rhoden, Director of Communications Erin Perrone, Carroll County Election Director Julia Cox, Somerset County Election Director Dionne Church, Wicomico County Election Director DECLARATION OF QUORUM PRESENT Mr. Summers declared that a quorum was present at 1:02 PM. ADDITIONS TO THE AGENDA Mr. Summers proposed moving the State Administrator and Assistant Attorney General’s Reports to the next meeting to prioritize election plans and regulations. Title 14 and Administrative Closure Requests were placed on hold to be revisited under Old Business if time permitted; otherwise, they were to be moved to the January meeting. APPROVAL OF OPEN AND CLOSED MINUTES – 10/23/2025 & 12/04/2025 Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the minutes for the open meeting on October 23, 2025. Ms. Butler made a motion and it was seconded by Mr. Shalleck. The minutes were approved unanimously. Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the minutes for the closed meeting on October 23, 2025. Ms. Butler made a motion, and Ms. Victoria Jackson-Stanley seconded. The closed meeting minutes were approved unanimously. Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the minutes for the meeting on December 4, 2025. Ms. Butler stated that she requested edits to the minutes, which had been incorporated. Ms. Butler made a motion to approve the minutes. Mr. Shalleck seconded the motion. The minutes were approved unanimously. PROPOSED REGULATIONS Ms. Dorsey provided details on proposed regulations seeking Board approval. 33.08.05.01, 33.08.05.06, 33.08.05.08, 33.08.05.09 Risk Limiting Audits (RLA) and Batching: New regulations implement risk-limiting audits as mandated by the General Assembly.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790084+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "fe019b37-68ff-4917-92a2-a321e0eb7133", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 288, "text": "Ms. Butler made a motion to approve the minutes. Mr. Shalleck seconded the motion. The minutes were approved unanimously. PROPOSED REGULATIONS Ms. Dorsey provided details on proposed regulations seeking Board approval. 33.08.05.01, 33.08.05.06, 33.08.05.08, 33.08.05.09 Risk Limiting Audits (RLA) and Batching: New regulations implement risk-limiting audits as mandated by the General Assembly. Unlike previous audits that reviewed a flat 1% of ballots, the RLA uses a mathematical algorithm to select a random sample of ballot batches to confirm election results. Mr. DeMarinis will roll dice to generate a seed number for the software to ensure a random selection. If the initial sample does not confirm the results, the audit escalates, potentially leading to a full manual recount. 33.09.03.05 Voting System Certification: New regulations allow the agency to require that future voting system vendors demonstrate the capability to conduct RLAs as part of the procurement process. 33.19.02.01 Same Day Registration and Address Change: Research has indicated that mailing approximately 700,000 postcards to unregistered voters is not cost-effective. The agency seeks more efficient methods to notify eligible voters of same-day registration options. Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the proposed regulations. Ms. Butler moved to accept and Ms. Jackson-Stanley seconded. The Board voted unanimously to accept the proposed regulations for public comment. 33.07.10.01, 33.11.06.01, 33.17.03.03 Ballot Storage for RLAs: Establish a uniform standard for how local boards of elections create, label, and store physical ballot batches to ensure they can be easily retrieved for risk-limiting audits without staff having to search for specific records. Ms. Butler inquired as to why there was no requirement to shred unvoted absentee ballots. It was clarified that while unvoted ballots are not required to be shredded, they must follow a strict retention schedule.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790106+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "34155564-0a4e-4fdb-b2f1-634317c5c963", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 292, "text": "Ms. Butler inquired as to why there was no requirement to shred unvoted absentee ballots. It was clarified that while unvoted ballots are not required to be shredded, they must follow a strict retention schedule. Unvoted ballots are kept for 30 days after certification or the completion of a recount, while voted ballots are retained for 22 months. Mr. Summers requested a motion to accept all three proposed regulations. The motion was moved by Ms. Butler and seconded by Mr. Shalleck. The Board unanimously approved the proposed regulations for public comment. ELECTION PLANS Ms. Rhoden presented the election plans for approval for each county. Calvert County: The plan includes two early voting centers and four ballot drop boxes, both of which remain the same as in 2024. While there is a decrease in polling places compared to 2022, the number remains consistent with the 2024 plan. Carroll County: This county introduced the Charles Carroll Community Center as a new early voting center and drop box location, replacing the Copper Mine Pantherplex. The Board noted that approximately 30 precincts lack a polling place within their borders. Ms. Perrone explained that drop boxes are prioritized at early voting centers where they are most used and noted that many northern precincts are primarily farmland with no suitable buildings for polling. Cecil County: This plan maintains two early voting centers and five drop boxes, with no changes from 2024. Polling places are also consistent across 2022 and 2024, though nine locations are situated out-of-precinct due to consolidations. Garrett County: The county will use two early voting centers and two ballot drop boxes, mirroring 2024. Polling place numbers remain unchanged from previous years. Montgomery County: The plan consists of 14 early voting centers and 50 drop boxes.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790117+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b6420d7b-ead7-417a-ad4f-a594358a239f", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 294, "text": "Polling places are also consistent across 2022 and 2024, though nine locations are situated out-of-precinct due to consolidations. Garrett County: The county will use two early voting centers and two ballot drop boxes, mirroring 2024. Polling place numbers remain unchanged from previous years. Montgomery County: The plan consists of 14 early voting centers and 50 drop boxes. There is a slight decrease of two polling places compared to 2022, and 62 locations are out-of-precinct, though replacements are located very close by or have been combined. Queen Anne’s County: This plan reflects no changes from 2024, utilizing two early voting centers, three drop boxes, and the same number of polling places. St. Mary’s County: The plan includes three early voting centers, seven drop boxes, and polling place numbers identical to 2022 and 2024. Somerset County: The plan consists of one early voting center and three drop boxes. It was identified as the only county where a ferry is required to reach a polling place on an island. The lack of polling places in the lower half of the county is due to a lack of infrastructure. Ms. Cox explained that they use every available facility. She clarified that the lack of polling places in the southern portion of the county is due to a total absence of infrastructure, such as fire halls, schools, or community centers. Washington County: The county increased its early voting centers from three to four and maintained eight drop boxes. Polling places decreased from 2022 but are consistent with 2024. Wicomico County: The plan includes two early voting centers and one additional drop box. There are 43 precincts that do not have a polling place within their boundaries. Ms. Church explained her county’s precincts are tied to planning and zoning districts.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790129+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b65c1fcf-33ac-4349-8bc1-3edb20c59883", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 285, "text": "Polling places decreased from 2022 but are consistent with 2024. Wicomico County: The plan includes two early voting centers and one additional drop box. There are 43 precincts that do not have a polling place within their boundaries. Ms. Church explained her county’s precincts are tied to planning and zoning districts. She explained that while many precincts appear to lack a dedicated polling place, they utilize consolidated or adjacent locations as permitted by law. Worcester County: This county has one early voting center and one drop box, though their locations will shift between the primary and general elections due to scheduling conflicts. Polling place numbers remain the same as in 2024. Mr. DeMarinis welcomed new Election Director, Jocelyn Abbot. The Board noted that Anne Arundel County has not yet had its plan approved. The local board is expected to resubmit materials for the January meeting. Mr. DeMarinis clarified that these plans had been vetted through local open board meetings where stakeholders and the public had the opportunity to provide input. All eleven presented plans were approved unanimously by the Board. POLICY 2025-03 PSR REQUEST Mr. DeMarinis introduced Policy 2025-03, which addresses Public Information Act (PIA) requests for voter files, as the agency has recently been receiving a high volume of these requests. He explained that the policy is designed to help clarify exactly what information is entitled for release and what must remain confidential under state and federal law. By establishing a uniform standard, the policy ensures that the same information is redacted and that the price for voter lists remains consistent across all counties. Ms. Butler inquired if the policy would force counties that historically provided lists for free to begin charging fees. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790140+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8aab0c68-67ef-4665-8fba-d4b2f2153087", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 300, "text": "By establishing a uniform standard, the policy ensures that the same information is redacted and that the price for voter lists remains consistent across all counties. Ms. Butler inquired if the policy would force counties that historically provided lists for free to begin charging fees. Mr. DeMarinis clarified that while individual requests taking less than two hours may still be free under the PIA, the standardized voter lists would carry a uniform $75 charge across the board to maintain statewide consistency. Ms. Butler also raised a concern regarding the verbiage of the policy, stating that a sentence stating no one should be \"dissuaded from registering to vote because of the fear of identity theft\" constituted a personal opinion that did not belong in a formal policy. Ms. Butler suggested postponing the vote until the next board meeting to allow time to seek clarification from Mr. Kobrin. Mr. Shalleck questioned the necessity of the policy as disclosure is already governed by statute, and a separate policy might be redundant or overstep by interpreting the law rather than just following it. Mr. Kobrin responded by explaining that the policy is a necessary synthesis of two interacting laws: the National Voter Registration Act (NVRA) and the Maryland Public Information Act (PIA). He explained that there is a gray area regarding demographic information, where the NVRA requires certain disclosures but the state's PIA contains an exception that allows the Board to hold back sensitive data such as social security numbers, driver’s license numbers, personal phone numbers, and email addresses. Mr. Kobrin stated that providing this uniform guidance prevents local boards from giving out different fields of information for different requests, which would lead to unequal treatment of the public and potential legal errors. Ms. Jackson-Stanley agreed with the need for standardization. She supported Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790151+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a38a0908-f804-4633-add1-cc93bc280eef", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 295, "text": "Mr. Kobrin stated that providing this uniform guidance prevents local boards from giving out different fields of information for different requests, which would lead to unequal treatment of the public and potential legal errors. Ms. Jackson-Stanley agreed with the need for standardization. She supported Ms. Butler’s suggestion to postpone the vote until January to allow the Board to further study the legal ramifications. Ms. Jackson-Stanley emphasized that any further legal briefings from Mr. Kobrin should be provided to the entire Board. Mr. Summers agreed that moving the item to the January 2026 meeting would allow for more legal consultation. OLD BUSINESS Mr. Summers suggested moving forward with discussing the Title 14 and Administrative Closure Requests agenda items. However, Mr. DeMarinis informed the Board that Mr. Norfleet was not present on the call. Mr. DeMarinis suggested that the Board hold all remaining old business items until the January meeting. Mr. Summers agreed to this postponement. NEW BUSINESS Ms. Butler raised a concern regarding a bylaw that restricts board members from communicating with litigants suing the Board. She requested an exception for general communication with political party members. Mr. Summers clarified the intent is to prohibit discussion specifically regarding the legal case and required that any interactions with litigants be disclosed to maintaining transparency. DISCLOSURE OF CAMPAIGN CONTRIBUTIONS There were no disclosures of campaign contributions. SCHEDULE NEXT MEETING The next board meeting is scheduled for January 22, 2026, at 1:00 PM. CLOSED MEEETING Mr. Summers announced that the Board would be moving into a closed session under General Provision Article Section 3-305 to consult with counsel on litigation and cybersecurity. A motion was made by Ms. Jackson-Stanley and seconded by Ms. Butler to close the public meeting and move into a closed session. The motion passed unanimously.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790162+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "405bd205-eec6-4794-9fa3-d05c4f684197", "source_url": "local://2026-01/02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT.docx", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 12.18.2025 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 8, "word_count": 88, "text": "CLOSED MEEETING Mr. Summers announced that the Board would be moving into a closed session under General Provision Article Section 3-305 to consult with counsel on litigation and cybersecurity. A motion was made by Ms. Jackson-Stanley and seconded by Ms. Butler to close the public meeting and move into a closed session. The motion passed unanimously. ADJOURNMENT A motion to adjourn was moved by Mr. Shalleck, seconded by Ms. Butler, and approved unanimously. The open session was recessed at 2:40 PM, subject to reconvening in a closed session.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.790167+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "18a347ac-1807-45ca-b354-37c10f40fb5d", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 300, "text": "ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL’S REPORT January 22, 2026 1. Sullivan, et al., v. Summers, et al., No. 1:24-CV-00172 (D. Md.). Katherine Strauch Sullivan and David Morsberger, two registered voters from Maryland, filed suit against SBE in the federal district court in Maryland alleging that the Board’s current regulation defining “electoral process” for voter registration list purposes violates the National Voter Registration Act and First Amendment. The complaint, filed January 18, 2024, seeks declaratory and injunctive relief restricting enforcement of the regulation. On March 8, 2024, OAG filed a motion to dismiss on behalf of SBE. The plaintiffs filed a response in opposition on March 29, 2024. OAG filed a final reply on April 12, 2024. On August 30, 2024, plaintiffs filed a motion for summary judgment on a stipulated set of facts. OAG replied in opposition to the motion on September 13, 2024. The plaintiffs filed a reply to that opposition on September 23, 2024. On March 4, 2025, the district court issued a memorandum opinion and order granting plaintiffs’ motion as to Count I; and, denying SBE’s motion as to Count I. The court ruled that the regulation is preempted by the National Voter Registration Act. The parties were also ordered to file a status report on their positions relating to the remaining counts. On March 28, 2025, the parties filed a joint status report requesting the court dismiss the outstanding claims. The Court dismissed the outstanding claims, and issued a final judgment on Count I, on October 30, 2025. On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees. That agreement, which has been executed by the parties, will be submitted to the Board of Public Works for final approval. 2. Brian Della Rocca v.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949305+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "045a873b-bf93-4493-b2e0-9afd454679e2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 295, "text": "On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees. That agreement, which has been executed by the parties, will be submitted to the Board of Public Works for final approval. 2. Brian Della Rocca v. Susan Lee, et al., No. 25-1873 (4th Cir.)- On December 2, 2024, Brian Della Rocca filed a complaint against the Secretary of State of Maryland, Chairperson of the Maryland State Board of Elections, and Postmaster General of the United States, seeking to enjoin Maryland from using mail-in ballots in the conduct of its elections. Specifically, the complaint asserts that the United States Postal Service is so unreliable in delivering election mail to- and from voters, that its use to conduct mail-in balloting violated voters rights under the First and Fourteenth Amendments. The plaintiff served the suit on Secretary Lee and Chairperson Summers on January 16, 2025. OAG filed a motion to dismiss the suit on behalf of both state officials on February 6, 2025. Plaintiff opposed the motion; OAG filed a reply to the opposition on March 5, 2025. On April 11, 2025, the United States’ Postmaster General, through the United States Attorney’s Office, filed a motion to dismiss. Plaintiff opposed the Postmaster General’s motion on June 09, 2025. On July 15, 2025, the district court granted both motions to dismiss, ruling that the plaintiff did not possess standing to maintain the claim. Mr. Della Rocca appealed the matter the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit. Appellant’s brief was filed on October 8, 2025. OAG filed a brief on behalf the appellees on November 10, 2025. Mr. Della Rocca filed a reply brief on December 1, 2025. On January 16, 2026, Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949326+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "68f28f6c-aec6-4186-b68b-171d595b8047", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 291, "text": "Mr. Della Rocca appealed the matter the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit. Appellant’s brief was filed on October 8, 2025. OAG filed a brief on behalf the appellees on November 10, 2025. Mr. Della Rocca filed a reply brief on December 1, 2025. On January 16, 2026, Mr. Della Rocca filed a letter under Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 28(j) noting supplemental authority for the case. OAG is drafting a letter in response. 3. California, et al., v. Trump, et al., 1:25-CV-010810 (D. Mass)- On April 3, 2025, nineteen states, including Maryland, filed a civil complaint in the federal district court for the District of Massachusetts against the President, the United States Attorney General, the Election Assistance Commission, and the United States Secretary of Defense. The complaint alleges, amongst seven counts, that the Executive Order imposing certain requirements on the conduct of voter registration and federal elections is unlawful. On April 23, 2025, the defendants moved to consolidate the lawsuit with a second set of lawsuits filed in Washington D.C. that challenged the executive order. The court denied that motion on May 9, 2025. On May 5, 2025, the plaintiff states asked the court for a preliminary injunction to pause implementation of provisions of the Executive Order while the lawsuit was pending. The court granted the preliminary injunction after a hearing. The defendants have appealed the grant of the preliminary injunction to the First Circuit. Separately, on June 13, 2025, the defendants moved to dismiss the complaint. The plaintiff states filed a response in opposition on July 11, 2025. The Court conducted a hearing on the motion on September 4, 2025. On September 17, the Court issued a memorandum opinion and order denying the motion to dismiss.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949339+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b1806d39-7d70-4a8f-afea-76b2e11b8cce", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 277, "text": "Separately, on June 13, 2025, the defendants moved to dismiss the complaint. The plaintiff states filed a response in opposition on July 11, 2025. The Court conducted a hearing on the motion on September 4, 2025. On September 17, the Court issued a memorandum opinion and order denying the motion to dismiss. January 22, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report Thereafter, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. On Friday, December 12, 2025, the parties filed cross-motions for summary judgment. OAG worked with SBE to provide a factual declaration on Maryland’s behalf in support of the plaintiff-states’ motion for summary judgment. 4. California, et al., v. Trump, et al., 1:25-CV-010810 (1st Cir.)- This is the appeal of the preliminary injunction granted in the above matter. On October 7, 2025, the Government filed appellants’ brief. However, soon after, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. Appellees’ brief was filed with the court on January 5, 2025. Appellants’ brief in reply is due by January 26, 2026. 5. Serena Bryson, et al., v. Westley Moore, et al., No. C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections. The suit alleges that the registered voters’ electoral rights, granted by the Maryland Constitution, are violated by their inability to vote in state-run, partisan, primary elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949350+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "91d43858-511e-43b0-9213-6a986a8474d2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 300, "text": "C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections. The suit alleges that the registered voters’ electoral rights, granted by the Maryland Constitution, are violated by their inability to vote in state-run, partisan, primary elections. The voters accordingly seek declaratory judgment and an injunction preventing the State from “conducting, organizing or funding State primary elections in which unaffiliated voters are prohibited from voting.” OAG filed a motion to dismiss the complaint on July 30, 2025. Plaintiffs filed an opposition to the motion on August 29, 2025. A hearing on the motion took place on October 20, 2025. On November 14, 2025, the Court granted the motion, dismissing the case in a memorandum opinion. Plaintiffs have appealed the case to the Appellate Court of Maryland. On January 12, 2026, the unaffiliated voters filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in the Supreme Court of Maryland. The petition asks the Supreme Court to hear the appeal now, rather than waiting to potentially entertain the appeal after a judgment by the Appellate Court of Maryland. OAG is presently drafting an answer to the petition, which is due to the Supreme Court on or before January 27, 2026. January 22, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report 6. The United States of America v. Jared DeMarinis, No. 1:25-CV-03934 (D. Md.) On December 3, 2025, the United States Department of Justice filed suit against the State Administrator of Elections over his response to the department’s multiple demands to disclose Maryland’s unredacted voter registration database. The complaint alleges that the State is in violation of a provision of the Civil Rights Act of 1960 in declining to hand over the unredacted information, as demanded.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949363+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c6ee3c9d-0492-46cc-822b-1651874acd6b", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-Assistant Attorney General's Report.pdf", "title": "03-Assistant Attorney General's Report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 6, "word_count": 273, "text": "The complaint alleges that the State is in violation of a provision of the Civil Rights Act of 1960 in declining to hand over the unredacted information, as demanded. With the complaint, DOJ filed a motion to compel records pursuant to the Civil Rights Act of 1960. The motion advocates to dispense with normal process and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, and instead (in a summary fashion) compel production of the voter database. By agreement between the parties, OAG must file a response to both the complaint and the motion on or before January 30, 2026. 7. Republican National Committee, et al. v. Jared DeMarinis, et al., No. 1:25- CV-03989 (D. Md.) On December 5, 2025, the national and state entities of the Republican Party and two Republican voters registered in Maryland sued the State Administrator of Elections, State Board, and the local boards of elections for Montgomery and Howard County. The complaint alleges that election officials in Maryland are violating the National Voter Registration Act (“NVRA”) by failing to make reasonable efforts to remove ineligible voters from the voter registration database. In support of that allegation, the complaint cites estimated population statistics and compares them to SBE’s reported voter registration totals. On January 12, 2026, the Democratic National Committee moved to intervene in the case as a defendant. The plaintiffs did not oppose the motion to intervene. OAG, on behalf of SBE, took no position on the motion. The motion remains pending before the Court. By agreement between the parties, OAG must file a response to the complaint on or before January 30, 2026. January 22, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:25.949373+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a983417c-07c0-4411-b021-c14caf0fe873", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 280, "text": "State of Maryland Administrator’s Report - January 22, 2026 Important Announcements and Meetings The Maryland General Assembly came into session on January 14. We are monitoring all legislation, including our departmental requests including: ● SB91/HB265 - ACP Enrollee Management ● SB11/HB260 - Earlier Distributions from Fair Campaign Financing Fund ● SB100/HB263 - Early Voting Bus Stops Candidac●y anSdB 1C5a3m/HpaBi2g6n7 F -i nCaamncpea (igCnC FF)in Danivcies i-o Cno ntrol of Funds Between November 27 and January 13, there were 86 new committees established. 2026 Gubernatorial Elections SBE has filed 338 candidates so far. Every candidate who files this year must file another financial disclosure statement in 2026 in order to appear on the ballot. SBE will send notices to those candidates beginning in January 2026. The candidate filing deadline is February 24, 2026. MD CRIS/BCDS Replacement Update The Maryland Contribution Reporting Information System (MD CRIS) went live on December 5. The project is currently in the Operations and Maintenance (O&M) phase of the contract. The first report due in the new MD CRIS system is the Annual 2026 Report, which is due on Wednesday, January 21, at 11:59 PM. The training for filers has been successful, and the feedback has been positive, allowing staff to identify any gaps in the system from the filer's perspective. Additionally, the vendor has scheduled an open Zoom call from January 15 to January 22 to assist filers with any issues or questions. The vendor will also be available on-site for any support needed during the filing of the Annual 2026 Report. Public Outreach As we approach the end of the 2026 Annual reporting cycle, our MD CRIS training sessions have seen great engagement from users.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307876+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9907d424-a981-4593-bd43-289769a3daab", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 291, "text": "The vendor will also be available on-site for any support needed during the filing of the Annual 2026 Report. Public Outreach As we approach the end of the 2026 Annual reporting cycle, our MD CRIS training sessions have seen great engagement from users. To date, the CCF department has conducted 28 out of the 32 scheduled training sessions, providing hands-on instruction to 478 participants. These sessions were designed to ensure that every user is fully prepared for their submission by covering critical aspects of the reporting process and offering direct technical support. To ensure compliance among businesses employing lobbyists, we executed a proactive outreach campaign on November 10, 2025. A ‘Notice of Possible Filing Obligation’ was sent to entities registered with the Maryland State Ethics Commission but not yet with the State Board of Elections. The impact of this outreach has been significant in that 245 businesses have since successfully State Administrator’s Report Page 2 of 7 registered on the Maryland Business Contribution Disclosure System (MD BCDS). On January 12, 2026, we hosted a focused press event and showcase for members of the media, university representatives, and independent researchers to demonstrate the capabilities of the newly upgraded MD CRIS and MD BCDS. This event has a strong turnout with 23 attendees participating both in person and virtually. Public Financing (PF) Currently, there are 50 active public financed (PF) committees. There are three candidates who have closed out their PF committee, which is not part of the 50 active accounts. As a comparison, by January 13th in an election year, there were 37 PF committees in the 2018 cycle. In the 2022 cycle at the same time, there were 27 PF committees. In the 2024 City elections there were 5 committees registered.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307897+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1344e5ee-654a-4e8a-895e-3b865982ef7e", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 294, "text": "As a comparison, by January 13th in an election year, there were 37 PF committees in the 2018 cycle. In the 2022 cycle at the same time, there were 27 PF committees. In the 2024 City elections there were 5 committees registered. As of Counties PF Initial Submission by 1/13 in the Cmte year of the primary 1/13/2018 Montgomery 37 9 1/13/2022 Montgomery & Howard 23+4 9+1 1/13/2024 Baltimore City 5 2 1/13/2026 Maryland (Governor), 50 11 Montgomery, Howard, Prince George’s, Baltimore Co., and Anne Arundel Eleven public financed candidates are certified/qualified as of January 13. 2026. A first for the PF program, the committee ‘Neighbors for Julie Yang’ CCF ID: 15015511, maxed out on her initial Matching Fund Report (MFR). As of January 12, 2025, there is $10,570,687.29 in the State Fair Campaign Fund. This means the program can fully fund 2 Primary candidates and 1 candidate in the General. No additional funding iEsn nfeoerdceedm. ent Listed below are the civil penalties paid in December 2025. The total amount collected is $1,350: a. Cockey, Robert Bob Citizens to Elect - $600 - Failure to maintain account books and records - Contribution/Expenditure records. b. Barclay, Christopher Friends Of - $500 - Failure to maintain account books and records - Bank records c. Neighbors of Evan Glass $125 - Failure to include authority line or authority line is insufficient. d. Zach Schulz for Harford - $125 - Failure to include authority line or authority line is Page 2 State Administrator’s Report Page 3 of 7 Communications insufficient. Social Media presence remains a priority to ensure votes know their elected officials are their trusted sources for election information. We are in the final stages of planning for an aggressive voter outreach campaign focusing on voter education.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307910+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1651e9bb-ba6c-4e8f-b46a-7dcdf5cd2408", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 298, "text": "Social Media presence remains a priority to ensure votes know their elected officials are their trusted sources for election information. We are in the final stages of planning for an aggressive voter outreach campaign focusing on voter education. The campaign includes a landing page that allows people to upload the election calendar to an individual’s phone and provides information on how to register to vote, become an election judge and methods of voting. There will continue to be paid outreach focusing on election judge recruitment and voter education leading up to the 2026 Primary and General elections. There will also be resources provided to local boards for the use of organic social media content to supplement the outreach campaign. Internally, we will utilize their own organic social media content to target mis/disinformation through voter education materials and videos. LBEs will have the opportunity to order a new 2026 “Maryland Voter” sticker celebrating the 250th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence in place of the typical “I Voted” sticker. The SBE Official Voter Resource app is in testing stages. We are hoping to launch the app mid- F ebruary which will be a wonderful resource for voters during the 2026 elections. Election Management & Compliance Contractual Staffing for Election Core trainers have been onboarded and trainers are being hired for election judge training. Language Assistance Implementation: Staff has been working to implement the new state requirements for language assistance. Among other things, a determination was made as to what counties and languages will be covered. An interpretation service, the Language Line, will be provided to those counties. Additionally, various election materials are being translated into the covered languages. If LBEs want to have additional d ocuments or languages, a request will be submitted using a form prescribed by SBE.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307921+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "118fbdd4-285d-46d0-8d7f-14511dc7e2be", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 293, "text": "Among other things, a determination was made as to what counties and languages will be covered. An interpretation service, the Language Line, will be provided to those counties. Additionally, various election materials are being translated into the covered languages. If LBEs want to have additional d ocuments or languages, a request will be submitted using a form prescribed by SBE. Information Systems & Technology Applications Release 10.5 of MDVOTERS will be deployed on February 20, 2026. The release will include an overhaul of Voter Registration Correspondence and mail in ballot enhancements. MAJOR INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY DEVELOPMENT PROJECT R(MeIpToDrPti)n AgC TIVITIES AND SPENDING T he Joint Chairman’s Report for is on target for January 15, 2026 Page 3 State Administrator’s Report Page 4 of 7 Information Security N o updates Mail-In Voting (MIV) Permanent Absentee Removals Later this month, SBE will remove approximately 11,000 voters from the Permanent Absentee Ballot list in accordance with Maryland Election Law §9–311.1. Voters will be removed if they haven't voted with their mail-in ballot in the last two statewide general elections. The LBEs will mail each voter a letter explaining this action. This action does not cancel a voter’s registration, and voters can sign-up for an MIB again if they choose. Online Mail-In Ballot Requests Voters requesting a mail-in ballot via the Online Mail-in Ballot Request (OMIR) system will now be required to go through a verification process requiring the voter to be currently registered in our database before proceeding with the request. The voter will be redirected to the Online Voter Registration (OLVR) screen if their information cannot be verified. This will prevent unregistered v oters from requesting a mail-in ballot during the close of registration period. Personnel Management Worcester is beginning recruitment for a new Deputy Director.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307933+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "027bf172-c16e-4ce8-8969-5e07dbccdbb9", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 300, "text": "The voter will be redirected to the Online Voter Registration (OLVR) screen if their information cannot be verified. This will prevent unregistered v oters from requesting a mail-in ballot during the close of registration period. Personnel Management Worcester is beginning recruitment for a new Deputy Director. HR is currently working on getting the Federal Injury Report completed. This shows all injuries that h appened within a calendar year. SBE had 0 for 2025. Procurement The New Voting System (NVS): Active Procurements 1) RFP D38B5600001 was published on June 27, 2025. On September 5, 2025 SBE received 4 offers in response to the solicitation. One Offeror wMiatnhadgreedw S tehceuirri ptyr oSperovsiacle. s :T hReF PR FDP3 8isB p6l6a0n0n0e0d5 for deployment in the 2028 election. The BAFO process will be underway this week. 2) - The MSS RFP for a managed security services provider is in the final stages of RFP creation and will be submitted for DoIT review shortly. The purpose of this solicitation is to procure a security contract that strengthens the agency's cybersecurity operations, engineering capabilities, and enterprise risk management. The selected contractor will provide managed security oMpDeVraOtTioEnRsS, sVp Decaitaal iCzeendt eenr:g RinFePe Dri3n8gB e6x6p0e0r0ti0s7e, and strategic advisory services to support the continued maturation of the agency's Cyber Security Division. 3) - The MDVOTERS V hosting services RFP is in the final development stages. The purpose of this contract is for Data Center (DC) hosting services, including hardware for several election systems, network Page 4 State Administrator’s Report Page 5 of 7 administration, and DC migration/relocation services, and a managed services provider for the data centers. The primary DC is also referred to as the Voter Registration Operations Center (VROC) throughout this document. Additionally requirements include the management and operations of the Continuity of Operations Center (COOP) data center.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307944+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ac356c79-63de-4002-ac39-03f4bf991b95", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 296, "text": "The primary DC is also referred to as the Voter Registration Operations Center (VROC) throughout this document. Additionally requirements include the management and operations of the Continuity of Operations Center (COOP) data center. Recen t Awards: : 1) Statewide Pollbook Modernization BPW approved SBE’s pollbook award on 12/3/2025. The contract is slated to start in January 2026. 2) Tamper Evident Labels: Novavision has started sending tamper evident labels to LBEs effective 1/13/2026. LBEs may receive an email directly from Novavision regardly delivery information Novavision. The grand total is $48,075.66. Requests for Information: 1) Automated Ballot Tabulation Audit Contract: We are planning to post an RFI for information on additional vendor options for the Clear Ballot contract to determine if this is still a sole source or if other vendors have entered the marketplace. Voter Registration, Audits & Operations Internal Audits Monthly audits of voter registration data continue to verify compliance with federal and state laws governing voter registration. Non-Citizens Non-citizen referrals are, in general, sent to the State Prosecutor’s office on a quarterly basis. Fourth quarter 2025 referrals were made on January 16, 2026. The following summarizes relevant activity from 4th Quarter 2025: Submitted to the Office of the State Prosecutor - 19 Removal of non-citizens - 19 Removal of non-citizens who voted - 16 Removal of non-citizens who voted multiple times - 7 Non-citizens reported by Immigration & Customs Enforcement - 0 Change in status from Office of the State Prosecutor - 0 Election Operations The Election Operations has finalized the Election Judge, Same Day Registration, and Address Change Manuals for the 2026 election cycle and provided these to all local boards of elections. We are in the process of reviewing each LBE’s customization of manuals and forms so that they are compliant and standardized.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307955+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "fec0a64b-b771-47d0-9fb4-24a09ac5dae0", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 166, "text": "We are in the process of reviewing each LBE’s customization of manuals and forms so that they are compliant and standardized. We continue to track election judge recruitment, pay rates, and training schedules in preparation for the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary. Page 5 State Administrator’s Report Page 6 of 7 Voting Works We continue our work with and SBE and LBE stakeholders to implement Risk Limiting Audits for the upcoming 2026 election cycle in congruence with SB 313 (2025). This audit will replace the manual audit and complement our automated tabulation audit by verifying the outcome of the election prior to state certification. Registration & Election Management Database (MDVOTERS) ERIC Reports for November were received. This is our first Cross State report since ERIC has loaded New Jersey data. In-State Updates are up, this is 3 months worth of data instead of 2 months. NCOA: 130,736 In-State Updates: 84,906 Cross-State: 20,082 The Current Total Registered Voters as of January 2, 2026 was 4,299,525 active voters in Maryland.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307962+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0b4c5bd7-afba-4ff0-8b4d-e721c9f2f478", "source_url": "local://2026-01/03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026.pdf", "title": "03-State Administrator's Report- January 22, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 9, "word_count": 232, "text": "This is our first Cross State report since ERIC has loaded New Jersey data. In-State Updates are up, this is 3 months worth of data instead of 2 months. NCOA: 130,736 In-State Updates: 84,906 Cross-State: 20,082 The Current Total Registered Voters as of January 2, 2026 was 4,299,525 active voters in Maryland. Motor Vehicle Administration (MVA) Transactions During December 2025, MVA collected the following voter registration transactions: New Registration - 8,408 Residential Address Changes - 15,733 Last Name Changes - 2,400 Political Party - 5,165 Last 13 Months of MVA Transactions - Chart: MVA Transactions - Monthly Changes: New Address Last Political Registrations Changes Name Party Page 6 State Administrator’s Report Page 7 of 7 Nov-2025 Month-to-Month - Change -28.6% -23.7% -24.0% -25.9% Same Month - Previous Year Change 2056.6% 1101.8% 417.1% 1591.1% Same Month - 2 Years Ago CDheacn-2ge0 25 -31.3% -11.4% -28.9% -9.9% Month-to-Month - Change 26.2% 16.8% 30.4% 24.2% 1.3% 4.1% -24.3% -12.9% Same Month - Previous Year Change Same Month - 2 Years Ago Change 8.7% 25.3% 14.4% 30.2% MSR- Removals Exercise of Delegated Duties We will use this section of the State Administrator’s Report to report when we have exercised duties assigned to the State Board of Elections but delegated to the State Administrator and staff. Since the last State Administrator’s Report: __x___ No delegated duties were exercised. _____ The following delegated duties were exercised. Page 7", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.307971+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2af31448-460d-43e9-824d-062cebf4c8e3", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 253, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman State Administrator Jim Shalleck, Vice Chairman Diane Butler Katherine Berry Deputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley Memorandum To: From: State Board Mem bers Date: Melissia Dorsey Re: January 22, 2026 Final Adoption of Regulations ___________________________________________________________________________________________________________ At the next board meeting, we will present for final adoption proposed changes to the following 1 COMAR provision (see enclosure): 1. 33.02.03.05 Election Judges' Training Programs 2. 33.04.02.01-.04 & .06 Inspection and Copying of Public Records; Confidentiality of Certain Information 3. 33.07.04.02 Election Day Activities; Order and Decorum – Cell Phones, Pagers, Cameras 4. 33.07.07.01-.03 Election Day Activities; Challengers and Watchers 5. 33.07.11.02 Compensation of Election Judges 6. 33.07.12 Election Day Activities; Language Assistance Program 7. 33.08.01.02 Canvassing, Definitions; General Provisions – Board Responsibilities 8. 33.08.01.02-1 Canvassing, Definitions; General Provisions – Board Counsel 9. 33.11.03.08 Absentee Ballots; Issuance and Return – When Ballots are Timely 10. 33.16.05.03 Provisional Ballots; Canvassing of Ballots – Disposition - Disposition of Provisional Ballot Applications 11.33.21.03.04 Special Elections by Mail; Issuance and Return – Transmission of Ballots Maryland Register The proposed changes to the regulations were adopted at the October 23, 2025 meeting. They were published in the December 12, 2025 edition of the (Vo. 52, Issue 25), and the public comment period closed on January 12, 2025. No public comments were received. If you have any questions before the next meeting, please contact me. I will also be available at the board meeting to answer any questions. Attachment. 1 IFtaAlXic (iz4e1d0 )t e9x7t4 i-s 2n0e1w9 section or language.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801513+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "29409b49-b653-462c-840d-2f757b642897", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 242, "text": "No public comments were received. If you have any questions before the next meeting, please contact me. I will also be available at the board meeting to answer any questions. Attachment. 1 IFtaAlXic (iz4e1d0 )t e9x7t4 i-s 2n0e1w9 section or language. Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200 MD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401 Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 02 MEETINGS AND TRAINING Chapter 03 Judges' Manuals and Training Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 10-205, and 10-206, Annotated Code of Maryland .05 Judges' Training Program. A.-B. (text unchanged) C. Requirements for Training Compensation. (1) A local board shall pay at least $50 to an election judge who completes the training program[.], and serves as an election judge in the election for which the training was offered. (2) If the training program includes a training class that is offered through virtual instruction or online delivery and a hands-on practice session, the election judge shall complete the hands-on practice session to be entitled to compensation. (3) An election judge who is required to complete a training program prior to both a primary and general election is entitled to compensation for each training program,so long as the election judge serves as an election judge in the election for which the training was offered. Explanation: This regulation implements HB586, enacted in 2025, requiring election judges to work during the voting period to earn pay for training.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801535+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "bdefd793-89e3-48db-b9ed-465b16b2f22b", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 141, "text": "Explanation: This regulation implements HB586, enacted in 2025, requiring election judges to work during the voting period to earn pay for training. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 04 INSPECTION AND COPYING OF PUBLIC RECORDS Chapter 02 Confidentiality of Certain Information Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4) and 3-506(a)(2); General Provisions 4-201(b) and 4-301(a)(2)(i), State Government Article, §§7-309 and 7-310; [State Government Article, §§10- 613(b) and 10-615(2)(i)]; Annotated Code of Maryland .02 Persons Entitled to Seek Confidentiality A.-D. (text unchanged) E. Participants in Address Confidentiality Programs. Subject to notification to the State or a local board of participation in the program described in § 3–2403 of the Courts and Judicial Proceedings Article, or the program described in § 7-303 of the State Government Article, an individual’s information shall be designated as confidential. [E]F. (text unchanged) .03 Applications A. (text unchanged) B.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801543+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6d3180d0-63c0-47b1-a208-59e3fa658b48", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 251, "text": "Participants in Address Confidentiality Programs. Subject to notification to the State or a local board of participation in the program described in § 3–2403 of the Courts and Judicial Proceedings Article, or the program described in § 7-303 of the State Government Article, an individual’s information shall be designated as confidential. [E]F. (text unchanged) .03 Applications A. (text unchanged) B. Form. (1) A person seeking to keep the person's residence address, telephone number, and email address of a record confidential shall apply in writing on a form approved by the State Administrator. (2) The form shall be filed: (a) At the appropriate local board if the applicant wishes to keep the applicant's residence address, telephone number, and email address provided on a voter registration application confidential; or, (b) At the State or local board at which the applicant filed the certificate of candidacy or statement of organization if the applicant wishes to keep the applicant's residence address, telephone number, or email address provided on a certificate of candidacy or statement of organization confidential. (3) A person seeking confidentiality need not complete the form if: (a) A person is a participant in the program described in § 3–2403 of the Courts and Judicial Proceedings Article, and the Administrative Office of the Courts notifies the State Board; or (b) A person is a participant in the program described in § 7-303 of the State Government Article, and the Secretary of State notifies the State Board. C. (text unchanged) .04 Consideration of Request A.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801553+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "54c17234-d43d-4d20-a7a8-a3922d42a739", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 285, "text": "C. (text unchanged) .04 Consideration of Request A. Discretion Required. (1) Except as provided in §A(2) and §A(3) of this regulation, the appropriate board shall determine, in its discretion, whether to grant a request for confidentiality. (2) The appropriate board shall automatically grant a request made by the State on behalf of a victim or witness under Regulation .02D(2) of this chapter. (3) Exceptions to Discretion. Board review shall not be required to designate as confidential the identifying information of the following: (a) Participants in the program described in § 3–2403 of the Courts and Judicial Proceedings Article; or (b) Participants in the program described in § 7-303 of the State Government Article. B.-C. (text unchanged) .06 Status Reviews A.-C. (text unchanged) D. The provisions of this regulation shall not apply to designations of confidentiality for participants in the program described in § 3–2403 of the Courts and Judicial Proceedings Article. Explanation: These changes to this regulation clarify statewide address confidentiality procedures. The regulation reflects recent legislation regarding judges and their families. Additionally, clarification is provided regarding differences in processes used for individuals who seek confidentiality through SBE versus those who seek confidentiality through the Address Confidentiality Program of the Secretary of State. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 07 ELECTION DAY ACTIVITIES Chapter 11 Election Judges Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 10-202, 10–203, and 10–205, Annotated Code of Maryland .02 Compensation of Election Judges A.-C. (text unchanged) D. Alternative to Compensation. A public high school student who earns hours toward the service-learning requirement for graduation under COMAR 13A.03.02.05 for their time in service as an election judge shall not be compensated for that service under subsections A and B of this regulation.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801564+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f7a32e22-b0fc-48f0-88a2-b1c397e39150", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 100, "text": "Alternative to Compensation. A public high school student who earns hours toward the service-learning requirement for graduation under COMAR 13A.03.02.05 for their time in service as an election judge shall not be compensated for that service under subsections A and B of this regulation. Explanation: This regulation implements HB586, enacted in 2025, which requires students who serve as election judges to choose between earning pay or service credit. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 08 CANVASSING Chapter 01 Definitions; General Provisions Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 11-201, [and] 11-301, and 11-302(a), Annotated Code of Maryland .02 Board Responsibilities.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801570+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c7b70877-c892-4d53-a94f-d8d62f014e9f", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 299, "text": "Explanation: This regulation implements HB586, enacted in 2025, which requires students who serve as election judges to choose between earning pay or service credit. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 08 CANVASSING Chapter 01 Definitions; General Provisions Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 11-201, [and] 11-301, and 11-302(a), Annotated Code of Maryland .02 Board Responsibilities. In General. (1) - (3) (text unchanged) (4) Oath or Affirmation. (a) [In an election where there is no early voting, each member of the local board shall, not later than 5 p.m. on election day, take and subscribe the following oath or affirmation:] Each member of the local board shall take and subscribe the following oath or affirmation: I, ........, do swear, (or affirm as the case may be,) that I will support the Constitution of the United States; and that I will be faithful and bear true allegiance to the State of Maryland, and support the Constitution and Laws thereof; and that I will, to the best of my skill and judgment, diligently and faithfully, without partiality or prejudice, execute the office of Board of Canvassers, according to the Constitution and Laws of this State. (b) The oath or affirmation shall be administered and recorded by the clerk of the circuit court. (c) The oath shall expire 35 days after election day. (5) Timing of Oath or Affirmation. (a) Each member of the local board shall take and subscribe the oath or affirmation not later than 9:00 a.m. 8 business days before the first day of early voting; or (b) If a local board of elections has obtained a waiver under Election Law §11- 302(b)(1), Annotated Code of Maryland, each member of the local board shall take and subscribe the oath or affirmation, not later than 5:00 p.m. on election day.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801582+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c1c73a15-dad0-471b-9440-928424f8a6ac", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 268, "text": "B. (text unchanged) Explanation: The change to this regulation updates the process of swearing in of the LBE Boards of Canvassers to reflect the need for canvassers to be sworn in prior to early canvassing. It also clarifies the expiration of the oath, which will be 35 days after the local board certifies the election. .02-1 Board Counsel. Oath or Affirmation Required. (1) Each local board counsel shall take and subscribe the following oath or affirmation: I, ........, do swear, (or affirm as the case may be,) that I will support the Constitution of the United States; and that I will be faithful and bear true allegiance to the State of Maryland, and support the Constitution and Laws thereof; and that I will, to the best of my skill and judgment, diligently and faithfully, without partiality or prejudice, serve as counsel to the ........ Board of Canvassers, according to the Constitution and Laws of this State. (2) The clerk of the circuit court or chairman of the local board shall administer and record the oath or affirmation in §A(1) of this regulation to the local board counsel. (3) The oath or affirmation shall be administered [on or before the day of the election but not later than 5 p.m. on that day.] as follows: (a) Not later than 9:00 a.m.8 business days before the first day of early voting; or (b) If a local board of elections has obtained a waiver under Election Law §11- 302(b)(1), Annotated Code of Maryland, not later than 5:00 p.m. on election day. (4) The oath shall expire 35 days after election day.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801594+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f36b8c3c-4216-42bc-a540-f772711da37e", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 264, "text": "B. (text unchanged) Explanation: The change to this regulation updates the process of swearing in of Local Board Counsel to reflect the need for counsel to be sworn in prior to early canvassing. It also clarifies the expiration of the oath, which will be 35 days after the local board certifies the election. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 11 ABSENTEE BALLOTS Chapter 03 Issuance and Return Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 2-305, 9-303, 9-305, 9-306, 9-310, 11- 301, 11-302, and 11-304, Annotated Code of Maryland .08 When Ballots Are Timely. A. - B. (text unchanged) C. Untimely Ballots. [A ballot that is not timely received as provided in this regulation may not: be counted.] (1) A ballot that is not timely received as provided in this regulation shall: (a) Be retained separately from timely ballots; and (b) Not be counted. (2) A ballot that is received after the local board certifies the election may not be: (a) Presented for rejection to the board of canvassers; (b) Presented to the local board of elections; or (c) Counted. Explanation: This regulation codifies the existing practice of the local boards for untimely mail- in ballots received after canvass is completed. Specifically, the board of canvassers need not be reconvened to reject the ballots. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 16 PROVISIONAL VOTING Chapter 05 Canvass of Ballots—Procedures Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 3-305(e), 3-306, 9-402, 9-403, 9-404, 9- 406, and 11-303(c) and (e), Annotated Code of Maryland .03 Disposition of Provisional Ballot Application A.-B. (text unchanged) C. Acceptance of Provisional Ballot Application.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801605+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5a610ebb-0672-40ab-8448-7b686f2b07aa", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 216, "text": "Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 16 PROVISIONAL VOTING Chapter 05 Canvass of Ballots—Procedures Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 3-305(e), 3-306, 9-402, 9-403, 9-404, 9- 406, and 11-303(c) and (e), Annotated Code of Maryland .03 Disposition of Provisional Ballot Application A.-B. (text unchanged) C. Acceptance of Provisional Ballot Application. The local board shall accept a provisional ballot application only if: (1) The voter is registered to vote, including a voter whose registration status was pending in the precinct register but who satisfied the verification requirement before the start of the provisional canvass; (2) The voter signed the provisional ballot application; (3) A voter who was required under COMAR 33.07.06 to provide personal identification information satisfied the identification requirement before the start of the provisional canvass; (4) In a primary election[, the party affiliation the voter indicated on the provisional ballot application is the party affiliation of the voter’s record;]: (a) The party affiliation indicated by the voter on the provisional ballot application is the party affiliation of the voter’s record; or (b) The party affiliation indicated by the voter on the provisional ballot application is not the party affiliation of the voter’s record and the ballot has a nonpartisan Board of Education contest. (5)-(8) (text unchanged) D. Full or Partial Count of Provisional Ballot.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801615+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b4507f9c-278a-4ff1-ab22-10e7bdb60a58", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 293, "text": "Full or Partial Count of Provisional Ballot. If the local board accepts the provisional ballot application, the local board shall count: (1) The entire provisional ballot if the ballot is the correct ballot style for the voter based on the address and party on the provisional ballot application; or (2) Only votes for the specific contests for which the voter is entitled to vote based on the address and party on the provisional ballot application, if the ballot is not the correct ballot style for the voter based on the address on the provisional ballot application. E. (text unchanged) Explanation: This regulation implements HB 839 of the 2025 MGA Session. The law requires LBEs to count votes for nonpartisan Board of Education contests, even if the voters vote the wrong party’s ballot, in a primary election. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 21 SPECIAL ELECTIONS BY MAIL Chapter 03 Issuance and Return Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102, 9-501, 9-502, and 9-504—9-506, Annotated Code of Maryland; 52 U.S.C. § 20302 .04 Transmission of Ballots. A. - B. (text unchanged) C. Mailing Deadline. A local board shall mail vote-by-mail ballots: (1) To absent uniformed services voters and overseas voters, at least 45 days before the day of the special election; and (2) To all other voters, at least [14] 30 days before the day of the special election. Explanation: SB262, enacted in 2025, changed the timing of special elections, and lengthened the time in advance of special elections when mail ballots must be sent. This change to this regulation reflects that new timing. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 7 ELECTION DAY ACTIVITIES Chapter 12 Language Assistance Program Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4); 2-202(b); and 15.5-104, Annotated Code of Maryland .01 Definitions A.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801626+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d1f83c31-f297-43ff-8c00-2aa5b40780c3", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 300, "text": "This change to this regulation reflects that new timing. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 7 ELECTION DAY ACTIVITIES Chapter 12 Language Assistance Program Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4); 2-202(b); and 15.5-104, Annotated Code of Maryland .01 Definitions A. In this chapter, the following terms have the meanings indicated. Terms defined. (1) “Designated language” has the meaning stated in Election Law Article, § 15.5– 101(b), Annotated Code of Maryland. (2) “Licensed translation software services” means a tool or service that provides interpretation of spoken communication between designated languages and English. (3) “Limited English proficiency” has the meaning stated in Election Law Article, § 15.5– 101(c), Annotated Code of Maryland. (4) “Translated” means the process of a trained individual converting from one written language to another while preserving the original meaning. (5) “Voting materials” has the meaning stated in Election Law Article, § 15.5–101(e), Annotated Code of Maryland. .02 Timing for Determination of Significant Need for Language Assistance. Not later than 8 months preceding a Statewide primary election, the State Board shall determine whether there is significant need for language assistance in a county. .03 Designating Forms and Instructions Necessary for Voting A. Timing. Not later than 7 months preceding a Statewide primary election, the State Board may determine if forms and instructions not named in Election Law Article, § 15.5–101(e), Annotated Code of Maryland, shall be designated as necessary to voting. Procedure. The State Board shall consider recommendations from the State Administrator when designating forms and instructions necessary to voting. .04 Notice of Significant Need for Language Assistance A. To the public. Not later than 6 months preceding a Statewide primary election, the State Board shall publish its determinations of significant need for language assistance that includes: (1) A list of local boards affected; and (2) The designated languages.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801636+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "eb1a2db6-c240-4aff-ae0d-8d289ea0cd21", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 289, "text": "To the public. Not later than 6 months preceding a Statewide primary election, the State Board shall publish its determinations of significant need for language assistance that includes: (1) A list of local boards affected; and (2) The designated languages. To Local Government officials. Not later than 6 months preceding a Statewide primary election, the State Administrator shall provide notice of the determination of significant need for language assistance by the State Board to: (1) The local board of elections; and (2) The Chief Financial Officer of the jurisdiction. .05 Local Board Requirements Upon determination by the State Board of a significant need for language assistance, the local board shall: A. No later than 11 weeks preceding a Statewide primary election, post translated, downloadable voting materials in designated languages on its website. Place a State Board approved sign, in each designated language, in each early voting center and polling place. Ensure the availability in each early voting center or polling place of: (1) Licensed translation software services; and (2) A tool or document that communicates in all designated languages and explains how to use the licensed translation software service. Make a reasonable effort to: (1) Recruit election judges who can communicate in designated languages; (2) Assign election judges who speak the designated languages to each early voting center; (3) Determine which polling places are most likely to serve voters who speak designated languages; and (4) Assign election judges who speak the designated languages to work in polling places that are most likely to serve voters who speak designated languages. Include information about the language assistance program in training materials and programs for election judges and local board of elections staff. .06 Language Assistance in Addition to Required Assistance A.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801646+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8544819b-7760-437f-b3af-82bf64db958f", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 299, "text": "Include information about the language assistance program in training materials and programs for election judges and local board of elections staff. .06 Language Assistance in Addition to Required Assistance A. Form. (1) The State Board shall: (a) Prescribe a form for a local board to request approval to provide language assistance services in addition to those required by Regulation .05 of this chapter; and (b) Provide the form to local boards not later than 1 year preceding a Statewide primary election. (2) The form shall include questions about: (a) The population to be served; (b) The proposed services; (c) Reasons for the local board’s request; and (d) Whether approving the request would impose additional costs or duties on the State Board. (3) A local board seeking to provide language assistance services that exceed requirements shall: (a) Submit the State Board prescribed form for each additional service or language which it proposes to provide; and (b) Request State Board approval at least 6 months preceding a Statewide primary election during which the local board proposes to provide additional language assistance services. Approval of additional language assistance. (1) Upon receipt of a form requesting approval to provide additional language assistance, the State Administrator shall review and make a recommendation to the State Board to accept or reject the request. (2) The State Board shall: (a) Vote on a local board’s proposal within 30 days of receipt of a form requesting approval to provide additional language assistance; and (b) Within 2 days of its vote on a request to provide additional language assistance, notify the local board of the outcome of the vote. Explanation: This regulation implements HB983, enacted in 2025, which requires in-language assistance in elections in counties with substantial populations of eligible voters who are not fully fluent in English.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801656+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c560103e-3c00-495a-a0f9-09e0d5fb6549", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 14, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 272, "text": "Explanation: This regulation implements HB983, enacted in 2025, which requires in-language assistance in elections in counties with substantial populations of eligible voters who are not fully fluent in English. These regulations describe the notification of coverage and support SBE will provide to LBEs, and the services LBEs will provide where coverage applies. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 07 ELECTION DAY ACTIVITIES Chapter 04 Order and Decorum Authority: Election Law Article, §§2-102(b)(4),[and] 2-202(b), and 15.5-104, Annotated Code of Maryland .02 Cell Phones, Pagers, Cameras, etc. A. (text unchanged) B. Exceptions. This regulation does not apply to: (1) Cameras used by media representatives as long as the media representatives are recording polling place operations and not a screen or ballot; (2) Cellular telephones, pagers, or computer equipment used by election officials or law enforcement officers, when acting in their official capacities; or (3) Cellular telephones or other electronic devices used by a voter: (a) To provide lawful proof of residency or identification as required by this title; (b) To review electoral information to assist in marking the voter’s own ballot prior to casting; [or] (c) To record a digital image of the voter’s own marked ballot prior to casting for personal use if it records only the ballot and may not be used in violation of Election Law Article, §16-206, Annotated Code of Maryland [.]; or, (d) To communicate through a translator or use licensed translation software services as described in Election Law Article, §15.5–103(B), Annotated Code of Maryland. Explanation: This amendment allows for use of cell phones and electronic devices in polling places to obtain interpretation services, including from LanguageLine Solutions.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801667+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "654de43c-547c-422b-a26a-18ae1e66ddd6", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 15, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 300, "text": "Explanation: This amendment allows for use of cell phones and electronic devices in polling places to obtain interpretation services, including from LanguageLine Solutions. Title 33 STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS Subtitle 07 ELECTION DAY ACTIVITIES Chapter 07 Challengers and Watchers Authority: Election Law Article §§2-102(b)(4), 2-202(b), 10-303 and 10-311, Annotated Code of Maryland .01 Designating Entities. State Board as Designating Entity. The State Board may: (1) Designate [an individual] a registered Maryland voter as an accredited challenger or watcher if the individual submits a written request that includes polling places in more than one county; and (2) Require the individual to attend a training session, read any State Board issued information, or both. Local Board as Designating Entity. A local board may: (1) Designate [an individual] a registered Maryland voter as an accredited challenger or watcher if the individual submits a written request that includes polling places in its jurisdiction; and (2) Require the individual to attend a training session, read any State Board issued information, or both. Other Designating Entities. (1) If a designating entity other than the State Board or a local board intends to distribute instructions to the individual or individuals it designates, the designating entity: [(1)] (a) May not provide information that conflicts with the Election Law Article, Annotated Code of Maryland, Title 33 of COMAR, and other State Board issued information; and [(2)] (b) May submit a written draft of the instructions to the State Board for review. (2) If a designating entity is a group of voters supporting or opposing a candidate, principle, or proposition on the ballot that may designate challengers and watchers, that group of voters shall be a political action committee or a person who is required to file a disclosure under Election Law Article §13-306 or 13-307, Annotated Code of Maryland.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801680+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2004d600-07a1-4e2f-b07d-a9e1b42fe4a6", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 16, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 298, "text": "Certificate. (1) A designating entity shall provide each challenger or watcher it designates with a signed certificate, in the form prescribed by the State Board. (2) The certificate shall be signed by: (a) If a candidate is the designating entity, the candidate; (b) If a political party is the designating entity, an officer, executive director of the entity, or other person authorized to sign on behalf of the entity; [or] (c) If a group of voters is the designating entity, the chairman or treasurer of the political committee[.] (d) If the group of voters is the designating entity, the Treasurer of an independent expenditure entity as defined in COMAR 33.20.01.01, (3) The signature of the designating entity shall be: (a) An original signature; or (b) A copy of an original signature. (4) [A designated entity shall provide the local board with:] Except as provided in paragraph E, and not later than 1 business day before the start of early voting, a designating entity shall provide the local election director with: (a) A copy of a completed certificate with an original signature; [Copies of completed certificates; or] (b) A list of individuals designated as challengers and watchers[.]; and; (c) A list of all polling places the challengers and watchers will attend. (5) Failure to submit the required documents in subparagraph (4) of this regulation to the local election director may result in the nonaccredition of the challenger and watcher. Substitutions (1) A designating entity may substitute names of accredited challengers and watchers up to and including on election day only after making a submission to the local election director in paragraph D. (2) The designating entity shall notify the local election director of the substitution in writing prior to the substitution. .02 Permissive Activities of Accredited Challengers or Watchers.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801688+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "049152ae-2cfc-4fe0-ba70-5843580e8aa2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 17, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 199, "text": "Substitutions (1) A designating entity may substitute names of accredited challengers and watchers up to and including on election day only after making a submission to the local election director in paragraph D. (2) The designating entity shall notify the local election director of the substitution in writing prior to the substitution. .02 Permissive Activities of Accredited Challengers or Watchers. In General.An accredited challenger or watcher may perform the activities defined in Election Law Article, §10-311(b), Annotated Code of Maryland. Before Voting. (1) Except as provided in §B(2) of this regulation, before voting begins on each day of early voting or election day, an accredited challenger or watcher may observe the election judges setting up the voting room, including: (a) Observing the election judges print voting system reports and review these reports; (b) [Verifying] Observing the number of voters who have been checked-in and the number of votes that have been cast; and (c) [Verifying] Observing that required security seals have been applied on the voting system. (2) An election judge shall not permit an accredited challenger or watcher entry to the early voting center or polling place during the 30 minutes immediately before the opening of the polls.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801698+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7f3bdc62-cbc3-4659-b677-66e20351372a", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 18, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 252, "text": "During Voting Hours. (1) (a) The Chief Judges shall determine the location of accredited challengers or watchers in the voting room. (b) When determining the location of the accredited challengers and watchers in the voting room, the Chief Judges shall ensure: (i) The accredited challenger or watcher may see and hear each person as the person offers to vote; and (ii) The efficient management of the voting room. (c) The Chief Judges are not required to locate the accredited challengers and watchers directly behind the check-in table or in a position where they can directly view the electronic pollbook. (2) During voting hours, an accredited challenger or watcher [shall] may, accompanied by a Chief Judge and at a convenient time for the election judges, observe: (a) [Verify the] The security seals on voting units to which voters have not been assigned; and (b) [Verify the] The number of voters who have been checked in to vote. After Voting. (1) To observe the post-voting hours procedures, an accredited challenger or watcher shall be in the polling place before voting hours end. (2) During this time, an accredited challenger or watcher may observe: (a) [Observe the] The election judges print voting system reports and review these reports; and (b) [Verify that] That required security seals have been applied on the voting system.[; and] [(c) Observe the transmission by modem of election results if the local board has instructed the Chief Judges to perform this task.] .03 Prohibited Acts of Challengers or Watchers. Prohibited Acts.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801706+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ffc06d5c-6530-4d92-ad4b-5f1f8f52262b", "source_url": "local://2026-01/04a-Final Regulations COMAR.pdf", "title": "04a-Final Regulations COMAR", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 19, "chunk_total": 20, "word_count": 143, "text": "Prohibited Acts. A challenger or watcher may not: (1) (text unchanged) (2) Bring into a polling place any device listed in COMAR 33.07.04.02, [or] political materials, or campaign materials[, newspapers, or news magazines]; (3)-(7) (text unchanged) B. (text unchanged) Explanation: These regulation revisions provide clarification that challengers and watchers should be registered Maryland voters. They also specify who can sign certificates on behalf of a central committee, outline the requirements and deadline for the submission of paperwork for accreditation of challengers and watchers, and how to submit documentation for substitutions. Additionally, the revisions seek to provide clearer wording for the permissive and prohibited activities and remove the text that describes the transmission by modem of election results as we no longer transmit results by modem at the polling places. Finally, they define a group of registered voters who may be challengers and watcher.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:26.801712+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "925fed86-ba26-401e-a3ae-eb91193daed2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 293, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman State Administrator Jim Shalleck, Vice Chairman Diane Butler Katherine Berry Deputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley Memorandum TO: State Board Members FROM: Allen Norfleet, Director Division of Candidacy and Campaign Finance DATE: January 22, 2026 SUBJECT: Title 13 Waiver of late filing fees standards Enclosed are the waiver requests, which were submitted by campaign committees that have been assessed late filing fees. The attached Waiver Request Information Page contains an overview of each committee as well as the Administrator’s recommendation for Board approval on granting a waiver request. In the past the Board has considered the following facts in determining whether just cause exists to grant a waiver.  Administrative error of any kind on the part of the Division.  The lateness is due to extenuating circumstances, i.e. physical illness or death in the family.  The late report is the first late report and allows the committee to close, or contains minimal financial activity.  The fee will cause undue financial hardship, if the liability of the fine is the personal responsibility of the officers.  Computer problems occurred which made timely filing impossible. However, the filer still must have demonstrated a good faith effort to timely file. Prior to the meeting please review each waiver request. Note the recommendations that you may disagree with or have questions on that you would like to discuss. Pursuant to Election Law Article §13-337 (b) (3), the State Administrator has denied one waiver request. No Board action is required on the denials. As of January 14, 2026 SBE has collected $4,353.00 in late fees year to date. SBE collected $4,353.00 for the month of January in late fees. Please feel free to contact me at 410-269-2856 if you have any questions.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496821+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "4d15ff7d-abfb-4877-88fd-76e359985e17", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 247, "text": "No Board action is required on the denials. As of January 14, 2026 SBE has collected $4,353.00 in late fees year to date. SBE collected $4,353.00 for the month of January in late fees. Please feel free to contact me at 410-269-2856 if you have any questions. FAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200 MD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401 Title 13 Late Fee Waivers 1. El-Amin, Mahasin Friends of 2. Hundal, Manpreet People for Deny 1. Munyeneh, Kayce Friends of Maryland Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page General Account Name El-Amin, Mahasin Friends of CCF ID: 1011928 Status: Active Date Established 9/21/17 Date Waiver Requested 1/06/26 Account Type Campaign Account Officers Current Treasurer Rasheedah El-Amin Start Date: Responsible Treasurer End Date: 9/21/17 Current Chairman Tracey Twyman Responsible Chairman End Date: 9/21/17 Reports Requested Late Report Affidavit Date Received Fees Total Fees 2024 PG Special 1/06/26 $1000 $1000 $ $ $ $ Total: $ All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP 2022 Pre-Primary 1 $20 Paid N/A 2022 Pre-General $20 Paid N/A Recent Financial Activity History/ Three Reports Report Contributions Expenditures Cash Balance Outstanding/ Loans/ Obligations 2025 Annual $25,285.0 $31,190.00 $80,882.00 $1,100.00 2024 Pre-General $500.00 $5,453.00 $81,834 $1,100.00 2024 Pre-Primary $9,860.00 $12,487.00 $86,787.00 $1,100.00 Reason for Waiver The Candidate did not file to run in the special election.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496840+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0ce951d8-f8ce-4aa8-858d-05a0185a652a", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 292, "text": "Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant 1/5/26, 3:23 PM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: Request to Remove from Special Elections Lisa Hanzook -SBE- <lisa.hanzook@maryland.gov> Fwd: Request to Remove from Special Elections 1 message Devki Dave -SBE- <devki.dave2@maryland.gov> Mon, Jan 5, 2026 at 2:55 PM To: Lisa Hanzook -SBE- <lisa.hanzook@maryland.gov> ---------- Forwarded message --------- From: Mahasin Amin <aminforclerk@gmail.com> Date: Mon, Jan 5, 2026 at 2:50 PM Subject: Fwd: Request to Remove from Special Elections To: <devki.dave2@maryland.gov> Sincerely, Mahasin El Amin Sent from my iPhone Begin forwarded message: From: Mahasin El Amin <mahasin@votemahasin.com> Date: August 1, 2024 at 1:56:39 PM EDT To: Erin Dennis -SBE- <erin.dennis@maryland.gov> Cc: Mahasin Amin <aminforclerk@gmail.com>, Rasheedah El-Amin <treasurerforamin@gmail.com>, Rasheedah El-Amin <Rasheedah@votemahasin.com> Subject: Re: Request to Remove from Special Elections Thank you very much!!!!! Sincerely, Mahasin El Amin Sent from my iPhone On Aug 1, 2024, at 11:19 AM, Erin Dennis -SBE- <erin.dennis@maryland.gov> wrote: Thank you, Mahasin. We've taken care of it. Erin W. Dennis Maryland State Board of Elections 151 West Street, Suite 200 |P.O. Box 6486 Annapolis, Maryland 21401 (P) 410.269.2928 |(P) 800.222.8683 <image001.png> https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=24da9818af&view=pt&search=all&permthid=thread-f:1853508009654407265&simpl=msg-f:1853508009654407265 1/2 1/5/26, 3:23 PM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: Request to Remove from Special Elections <2d19ecfe-e044-469a-a0b6-891a41a2449d.png> <fb.png> <insta.png> <yt2.png> On Wed, Jul 31, 2024 at 4:52 PM Mahasin Amin <aminforclerk@gmail.com> wrote: Hello Erin, Thank you so much for working on this issue and following up. Per our discussion, I never filed to run in Prince George’s Co. special election. I had to change my account “Friends of Mahasin El-Amin” in order to file as the filing requirement. I’m kindly asking for the selection to be removed from my account to reflect that I’m running in the gubernatorial election. I am not asking for my account to be closed. It’s should remain open.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496852+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ac2bee2d-c31b-4a09-830c-b80e97fc9359", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 237, "text": "I had to change my account “Friends of Mahasin El-Amin” in order to file as the filing requirement. I’m kindly asking for the selection to be removed from my account to reflect that I’m running in the gubernatorial election. I am not asking for my account to be closed. It’s should remain open. Please let me know if you have any questions. It would be greatly appreciated if you would kindly confirm receipt to make sure I got the right email. Once again THANK YOU!!!! Sincerely, Mahasin El Amin Sent from my iPhone -- Devki Dave Maryland State Board of Election Division of Candidacy and Campaign Finance 151 West Street, Suite 200, P.O.Box 6486 Annapolis, Maryland 21401-0486 Phone number-410-269-2874 https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=24da9818af&view=pt&search=all&permthid=thread-f:1853508009654407265&simpl=msg-f:1853508009654407265 2/2 Maryland Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page General Account Name Hundal, Manpreet People for CCF ID: 1014766 Status: Active Date Established 4/15/22 Date Waiver Requested 12/10/25 Account Type Campaign Account Officers Current Treasurer Ripjeet Gill Start Date: Responsible Treasurer End Date: 4/15/22 Current Chairman Manpreet Hundal Responsible Chairman End Date: 4/15/22 Reports Requested Late Report Affidavit Date Received Fees Total Fees 2022 Post General 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 2022 Pre_General 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 2023 Annual 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 2022 Pre-Primary 2 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 2022 Pre-Primary 1 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 2022 Pre-General 2 2/29/24 $140.00 $140 2024 Annual 2/29/24 $1000 $1000 Total:$6,140.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496862+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f9cad93a-0949-4184-b3da-de2daa733862", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 300, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Recent Financial Activity History/ Three Reports Report Contributions Expenditures Cash Balance Outstanding/ Loans/ Obligations 2024 Annual $0 $0 $1,207.00 $0 2023 Annual $0 $0 $1,207.00 $0 2022 Post General $0 $0 $1,207.00 $0 Reason for Waiver The candidate states the reports where not filed timely because of divorce, homelessness, unemployment, and moving out of State. Division Comments Maryland Contribution Disclosure System Administrator’s Decision Grant and close account 12/5/25, 4:14 PM Late Fee Waiver Request Form Late Fee Waiver Request Form x The respondent's email (preethundal846@gmail.com) was recorded on submission of this form. Email* preethundal846@gmail.com Campaign Account Name/Business Entity: * Manpreet Hundal,People for CCF ID NUMBER / BCDS ID NUMBER: * Your CCF ID and BCDS ID can be looked up at https://campaignfinance.maryland.gov/Public/ViewCommittees or https://businessdisclosur e-elections.maryland.gov/Public/ViewBusiness 01014766 Name of the Requestor(s)* Manpreet K Hundal https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1inDO-Hn6YfFCY9Vu7pcdaTzUQ3uEBS1QJS4Mltm0TCk/edit?ts=63f4f498#responses 1/3 12/5/25, 4:14 PM Late Fee Waiver Request Form MDCRIS - OFFICER POSITION * Chairman Treasurer Candidate None - Submitting for BCDS BCDS - OFFICER POSITION * Filer Principal Officer None - Submitting for MDCRIS Waiver of late fees for the following Campaign Finance Report(s) * Check the Campaign Finance Report(s) that apply. Annual Report Pre-Primary Report 1 Pre-Primary Report 2 Pre-General Report 1 Ballot Issue Report Pre-General Report 2 Post-General Report None - Submitting for BCDS https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1inDO-Hn6YfFCY9Vu7pcdaTzUQ3uEBS1QJS4Mltm0TCk/edit?ts=63f4f498#responses 2/3 12/5/25, 4:14 PM Late Fee Waiver Request Form Waiver of late fees for the following Business Contribution Disclosure Statement Report (s): * Check the Business Disclosure Report(s) that apply. Spring Business Contribution Statement Fall Business Contribution Statement None - Submitting MDCRIS Total Amount of late fee(s) $ * 7000.00 The basis for the request * The request must provide just cause for the waiver of the late fee(s). Divorce, moved out of state, homelessness, unemployment.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496874+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0678301a-37a3-415b-936d-8f794ae5fc20", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 293, "text": "Spring Business Contribution Statement Fall Business Contribution Statement None - Submitting MDCRIS Total Amount of late fee(s) $ * 7000.00 The basis for the request * The request must provide just cause for the waiver of the late fee(s). Divorce, moved out of state, homelessness, unemployment. Print Name and Date: * By printing your name below, you certify that the information provided is true and accurate to the best of your knowledge and under the penalties of perjury. Manpreet K Hundal This form was created inside of State of Maryland. Forms https://docs.google.com/forms/d/1inDO-Hn6YfFCY9Vu7pcdaTzUQ3uEBS1QJS4Mltm0TCk/edit?ts=63f4f498#responses 3/3 Waiver Request Information Page General Account Name Munyeneh, Kayce Friends of CCF ID: 01015309 Status: Active Date Established 7/09/24 Date Waiver Requested 8/26/25 Account Type Campaign Account Officers Current Treasurer Lamar Henerson Start Date: 7/09/24 Responsible Treasurer Current Chairman Kayce Munyeheh 7/09/24 Responsible Chairman Waiver Request Dates Late Report Affidavit Date Fees Total Fees Received 2025 Post General Special 12/02/25 $1000 $1000 2025 Pre-General Special 12/02/25 $1000 $1000 2025 Pre-Primary 2 Special 12/02/25 $1000 $1000 2025 Pre-Primary 1 Special 12/02/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $4,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP 2025 Annual $1000 Paid N/A 2024 Post General Special $435 Paid N/A 2024 Pre-General 2 $1000 Paid N/A Recent Financial Activity History Report Contributions Expenditures Cash Balance Outstanding/ Loans/ Obligations 2024 Post General $275.00 $956.00 $14,559 $0 2024 Pre-General 2 $8,420.00 $1,728.00 $15,241.00 $0 2024 Pre-General $8,550.00 $0 $8,550.00 $0 Reason for Waiver The candidate states that the treasurer resigned, and he lost access to the email account, along with information on how to file. Division Comments The candidate states they do not have funds to pay, but the reports state otherwise.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496884+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "02edc6fe-e842-489f-ae27-df251b04878a", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 293, "text": "Division Comments The candidate states they do not have funds to pay, but the reports state otherwise. Administrator’s Decision Deny, non-compliant. 12/23/25, 11:25 AM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: CCFID 01015309 Victorica Smith -SBE- <victorica.smith@maryland.gov> Fwd: CCFID 01015309 Erika Riley -SBE- <erika.riley@maryland.gov> Tue, Dec 23, 2025 at 9:29 AM To: Victorica Smith -SBE- <victorica.smith@maryland.gov> Hi Vicky, Kayce Munyeneh meant to have you on this email thread, but your email address was spelt incorrectly so I just wanted to forward to you. Best, Erika Riley Maryland State Board of Elections (410) 269-2911 151 West Street, Suite 200 P.O. Box 6486 Annapolis, Maryland 21401-0486 ---------- Forwarded message --------- From: Kayce Munyeneh <kaycemunyeneh@gmail.com> Date: Tue, Dec 23, 2025 at 6:24 AM Subject: CCFID 01015309 To: erika.riley@maryland.gov <erika.riley@maryland.gov> Cc: <victoria.smith@maryland.gov> Good morning, Thank you for speaking with me earlier this year. I appreciate the time and guidance you provided. Since that conversation, I have closed out all of the reports and made repeated efforts to correct any issues with my account, despite not having access to certain information that was previously maintained by my former campaign manager. I have worked in good faith to reconstruct the records and address the reporting issues to the best of my ability. Most recently, reports were flagged as being in error. I believe it is because the final numbers reflect a negative balance. I used personal funds to cover outstanding campaign expenses when the campaign account was in default, including the remaining balance owed for campaign signs. The negative figure reflects a use of personal funds rather than unreported activity, and there were no outstanding obligations at the close of the reporting period. I submitted a final waiver request, but I haven't received any notification or update about its status.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496895+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d2cc0602-4f23-4a78-bdc3-37d848c16afd", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 293, "text": "The negative figure reflects a use of personal funds rather than unreported activity, and there were no outstanding obligations at the close of the reporting period. I submitted a final waiver request, but I haven't received any notification or update about its status. Assistance in confirming receipt of the waiver request and understanding when I might expect a response would be greatly appreciated. I am providing a copy of what I sent as a request for waiver below: APPEAL OF LATE FILING PENALTY Submitted by: Kayce Munyeneh, Candidate for Prince George’s County Council, District 5 To the Honorable Members of the Board of Elections: https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=d7a5ab707f&view=pt&search=all&permmsgid=msg-f:1852309713417770815&simpl=msg-f:1852309713417770815 1/4 12/23/25, 11:25 AM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: CCFID 01015309 I respectfully submit this appeal requesting reconsideration and removal of the late filing penalties assessed against my campaign committee. While I acknowledge the importance of timely and accurate reporting, I believe the unique and extraordinary circumstances of my situation warrant relief. The delays in my filings were not due to disregard, negligence, or an attempt to conceal information. Rather, they were the result of simultaneous, unavoidable, and severe disruptions that would have compromised any committee’s ability to meet reporting deadlines—especially one operating with limited volunteer support. From September 2024 through May 2025, my campaign experienced an unprecedented series of challenges that directly affected our compliance capacity. These disruptions included: (1) the necessity of initiating litigation involving the Town of Bladensburg/Cheverly, (2) the sudden overseas deployment of my treasurer, a military service member, and (3) the unexpected resignation of my campaign manager, who was the only individual with full access and working knowledge of the reporting system. Each event had a direct, documented impact on the committee’s ability to complete, reconcile, and submit reports on time. 1.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496906+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8d5f69d4-5fdf-41da-bcb3-f713b0b23737", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 283, "text": "Each event had a direct, documented impact on the committee’s ability to complete, reconcile, and submit reports on time. 1. Ongoing Litigation Demands Significantly Disrupted My Capacity to Manage Administrative Tasks During this period, I was required to initiate legal action involving the Town of Bladensburg (Cheverly), a matter that demanded substantial time, energy, and attention. As the first Black Mayor of Cheverly and someone who has always prioritized legal compliance and transparency in government, this situation placed me under intense pressure. The litigation process required: Extensive document gathering Meetings with counsel Detailed review of historical records Multiple filings and responses Public scrutiny and community engagement These demands occurred concurrently with the campaign filing period. As a local elected executive and active candidate, I had no administrative staff to absorb this workload. The legal obligations substantially diminished the time and focus I could dedicate to troubleshooting campaign reporting disruptions, especially after losing key personnel. 2. Treasurer’s Unplanned Overseas Military Deployment My campaign treasurer is an active-duty member of the United States military. In early 2025, he was unexpectedly deployed overseas, leaving the country without sufficient notice to complete handoff procedures or maintain consistent communication. His absence created immediate and unavoidable obstacles: He solely handled financial reconciliation. He alone held certain passcodes and internal tracking sheets. His deployment limited his availability due to time zone changes, restricted communications, and operational duties. Given the nature of military service, there was no reasonable way to prevent or mitigate this disruption. This circumstance was completely outside of my control, and I acted in good faith to reassign responsibilities as quickly as possible. However, replacing a treasurer mid-cycle is a complex administrative process that itself caused delays. 3.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496917+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d895b619-412a-44cf-9d42-1e8d64f2e3b5", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 290, "text": "Given the nature of military service, there was no reasonable way to prevent or mitigate this disruption. This circumstance was completely outside of my control, and I acted in good faith to reassign responsibilities as quickly as possible. However, replacing a treasurer mid-cycle is a complex administrative process that itself caused delays. 3. Abrupt Resignation of Campaign Manager With Exclusive Access to Reporting System Compounding these issues, my campaign manager—who had sole working knowledge of the reporting platform and maintained the organized log of expenditures—quit unexpectedly. He did so while retaining the only detailed understanding of how the reports were being structured, categorized, and prepared. https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=d7a5ab707f&view=pt&search=all&permmsgid=msg-f:1852309713417770815&simpl=msg-f:1852309713417770815 2/4 12/23/25, 11:25 AM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: CCFID 01015309 His resignation created several barriers: He did not provide a transition plan. He did not leave behind written documentation. He held exclusive access credentials and was responsible for maintaining the expenditure spreadsheet. He alone knew the order, categorization, and reconciliation method for receipts and transactions. This placed me in a position where I had to re-create from scratch a reporting structure that was already underway, without the benefit of his institutional knowledge. I made diligent efforts to recover access, reconstruct data, and bring new volunteers up to speed. However, campaign finance reporting is detail-intensive, and improper or rushed submission could result in errors that themselves violate regulations. I chose accuracy over speed while simultaneously managing litigation, governance, and parenting responsibilities—all while preparing for a special election cycle. 4. Demonstrated Good Faith, No Intent to Conceal, and Immediate Corrective Action Despite the obstacles described above, I worked diligently to rectify the situation as soon as it became administratively feasible. Once I regained access and reconstructed the necessary information, I submitted all required filings.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496928+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "996acea6-0b56-4a10-acb1-59ecb977c63d", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 297, "text": "Demonstrated Good Faith, No Intent to Conceal, and Immediate Corrective Action Despite the obstacles described above, I worked diligently to rectify the situation as soon as it became administratively feasible. Once I regained access and reconstructed the necessary information, I submitted all required filings. There is no evidence of: Missing funds Concealed expenditures Improper contributions Intentional delay The lateness was purely procedural and caused by circumstances beyond my control. Additionally: All financial records were maintained. All expenditures were legitimate and documented. The reporting delays did not impede the public’s right to transparency. My record as an elected official underscores my respect for public accountability and ethical governance. I have never had compliance issues in any previous cycles. 5. The Standard Applied Should Consider Reasonableness and Equity Under Extraordinary Circumstances Maryland election regulations recognize that penalties should not punish candidates who make good-faith efforts under extenuating circumstances. The Board has discretion to waive or reduce fines when: The delay is not intentional The circumstances are beyond the candidate’s control The candidate acts promptly upon regaining capacity There is no harm to the public trust This case meets all of these conditions. Between litigation pressures, the military deployment of my treasurer, and the resignation of my campaign manager—the only person who had full access to the reporting data system—my committee operated under conditions not reasonably https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=d7a5ab707f&view=pt&search=all&permmsgid=msg-f:1852309713417770815&simpl=msg-f:1852309713417770815 3/4 12/23/25, 11:25 AM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: CCFID 01015309 foreseeable or preventable. I respectfully request that the Board waive the penalties associated with my late filings. My delays were the direct result of overlapping and uncontrollable circumstances that severely hindered administrative capacity. Throughout this period, I remained committed to transparency, actively preserved all financial records, and worked in good faith to submit complete and accurate reports as soon as possible.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496939+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "fb02a909-fe8c-4f44-80be-de4702edbc0c", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 273, "text": "I respectfully request that the Board waive the penalties associated with my late filings. My delays were the direct result of overlapping and uncontrollable circumstances that severely hindered administrative capacity. Throughout this period, I remained committed to transparency, actively preserved all financial records, and worked in good faith to submit complete and accurate reports as soon as possible. I appreciate the Board’s understanding and consideration. My service as a public official has always been rooted in integrity, and I take compliance obligations seriously. I ask that you recognize the unique challenges I faced and grant relief accordingly. Respectfully submitted, Kayce Munyeneh At this time, I am unable to log into MDCRIS, which is preventing me from correcting any filing errors. I respectfully request assistance with: Restoring my access to MDCRIS; Guidance on how the negative balance should be properly reflected, given the circumstances; and Directions on how to proceed with the outstanding fines while the waiver request is pending. I used a significant amount of my personal funds to support my campaign, and do not currently have the ability to pay the remaining $4,000 in fines in full. If payment plans or other interim options are available, I would be grateful for guidance on how to proceed. Thank you for your time and assistance. I appreciate your help in resolving this matter and bringing the account into full compliance. Warm regards, Kayce Munyeneh Kayce Munyeneh 240-478-4077 (p) 301-560-6604 (f) CONFIDENTIALITY NOTE: The preceding email message contains information that may be confidential, proprietary, or legally privileged, and may constitute non-public information. This message is intended to be conveyed only to the intended named recipient(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496949+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b5f72980-15c2-4af7-b741-b171b41a0fc2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05a-Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 130, "text": "I appreciate your help in resolving this matter and bringing the account into full compliance. Warm regards, Kayce Munyeneh Kayce Munyeneh 240-478-4077 (p) 301-560-6604 (f) CONFIDENTIALITY NOTE: The preceding email message contains information that may be confidential, proprietary, or legally privileged, and may constitute non-public information. This message is intended to be conveyed only to the intended named recipient(s). If you are not an intended recipient of this message, do not read it; instead, please advise the sender by reply email, and delete this message and any attachments. Unauthorized individuals or entities are not permitted access to this information. Any disclosure, copying, distribution, or taking any action in reliance on the contents of this information, except its delivery to the sender, is strictly prohibited and may be unlawful. https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=d7a5ab707f&view=pt&search=all&permmsgid=msg-f:1852309713417770815&simpl=msg-f:1852309713417770815 4/4", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:27.496956+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1585b190-ad7d-4471-b531-7d117914e8a2", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 172, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman State Administrator Jim Shalleck, Vice Chairman Diane Butler Katherine Berry Deputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley Memorandum TO: State Board Members FROM: Allen Norfleet, Director Division of Candidacy and Campaign Finance DATE: January 22, 2026 SUBJECT: Waiver of late filing fees – Persons Doing Public Business, Title 14 of the Election Law Article Enclosed are the waiver requests, which were submitted by businesses that have been assessed late filing fees. The attached Waiver Request Information Page contains an overview of each entity as well as the Agency’s recommendation to the Board. The Board should consider the following factors in determining whether just cause exists to grant a waiver. 1. Administrative error of any kind on the part of the Division. 2. The lateness is due to extenuating circumstances, i.e. physical illness or death in the family; or 3. Computer problems occurred which made timely filing impossible. However, the filer still must have demonstrated a good faith effort to timely file. Prior to the meeting please review each waiver request.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509177+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "da059d18-977c-41c4-b335-d5e2a0d0fb0a", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 297, "text": "Administrative error of any kind on the part of the Division. 2. The lateness is due to extenuating circumstances, i.e. physical illness or death in the family; or 3. Computer problems occurred which made timely filing impossible. However, the filer still must have demonstrated a good faith effort to timely file. Prior to the meeting please review each waiver request. Note the recommendations that you may disagree with or have questions on that you would like to discuss. § 14-107(c) Late Filing Fees (1) As provided in this subsection, the State Board may impose fees for late filing of: (i) a statement required under § 14–104 of this title; or (ii) an amended statement required under subsection (b) of this section. (2) The State Board may impose late filing fees in the same amounts and in the same manner as provided under § 13–331(a) and (b) of this article for late filing of campaign finance reports. (3) Late filing fees imposed under this subsection shall be distributed to the FAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200 MD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401 Insert Subject Line Insert Date Page 2 of 2 Fair Campaign Financing Fund. Pursuant to COMAR 33.20.07.01C, the State Administrator has denied nine late fee waiver request. No Board action is required on the denial As of January 14, 2026, SBE has collected $13,260.00 in late fees year to date for Title 14 Late Fee Waivers. SBE collected $13,260.00 for the month of January in late fees. Title 14 Waiver List 1. Advance Digital Systems 2. Affirm 3. Altaris, LLC 4. American Association of Dental Boards 5. American Forest & Paper 6. Beckley Enterprise Inc 7. Association American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network 8.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509199+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "aa0e2df4-6b0a-4dbf-83c1-d8aef40ef2f0", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 293, "text": "SBE collected $13,260.00 for the month of January in late fees. Title 14 Waiver List 1. Advance Digital Systems 2. Affirm 3. Altaris, LLC 4. American Association of Dental Boards 5. American Forest & Paper 6. Beckley Enterprise Inc 7. Association American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network 8. The Baltimore Orioles Limited Partnership 9. CAI-MD Legislative Action Committee 10. Chesapeake Turf, LLC 11. In Market Media, LLC 12. Innosoft Corporation 13. Linnel, Inc 14. Lutherville Station, LLC 15. Restaurant Association of Maryland Education Foundation 16. Security Land and Development LP 17. Southern Management Companies, LLC 18. Cigna Health and Life Insurance Company 19. Coakley & Williams Construction LLC 20. Daikin US Corporation 21. Washington Area New Automobile Dealers Association 22. Delmarva Chicken Association 23. Express Scripts Holding Inc 24. Hill International, Inc. 25. Hyster-Yale Materials Handling, Inc. 26. International Code Council Inc. Title 14 Waiver List 27. Ritchie Land Reclamation LLC 28. Sinai Hospital of Baltimore, Inc 29. Tides At River Marsh LLC 30. UFCW Local 27 31. Deny 1. Capitol Paving of D.C., Inc 2. Community Bridge, Inc 3. ECA Solar 4. International Sleep Products Association 5. Maryland Association of Green Industries 6. Maryland Grain Producers Association 7. Maryland Green Industries Council 8. American Council of Engineering Companies of MD 9. Clear Ballot Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Advance Digital Systems BID ID: 14001719 Date Established 6/26/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/15/26 Account Type Title 14-Person Doing Public Business Officers Principal Officer Ash Kupar Start Date: 6/26/23 Current Filer Ash Kupar Start Date: 6/26/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Spring 2025 6/30/25 $1000 $1000 $ $ Total:$1,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509210+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "164f9ed0-9bd3-49d3-b7e4-8cd263d1f342", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 272, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $1000 paid N/A $ Reason for Waiver The business states they are a very small business, certified MBE, and they had a death in the family and was away from the office. The fine of $1000.00 would be a hardship. Agency Comments Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Affirm BID ID: 14001847 Date Established 02/01/2024 Date Waiver Requested 12/02/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Cheye-Ahn Corona Start Date: 02/01/2024 Current Filer Brittany Harris Start Date: 02/01/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 (2019) 12/02/2025 $20.00 $20.00 Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $80.00 Paid No Reason for Waiver Lobbyist end date was not showing on account, but has now been added. Agency Comments Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Altaris, LLC BID ID: 14000987 Date Established 05/01/2018 Date Waiver Requested 12/02/2025 Account Type Title 14-Person Doing Public Business Officers Principal Officer Chuck Mullens Start Date: 05/01/2018 Current Filer Chuck Mullens Start Date: 05/01/2018 Waiver Request Dates Late Report (Fine ID) Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 (1985) 12/02/2025 $20.00 $20.00 Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $535.00 Waived No Reason for Waiver Trouble accessing the system to file. Agency Comments Grant.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509220+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7d2c8152-04bb-4db5-80b9-445400fbdb85", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 261, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $535.00 Waived No Reason for Waiver Trouble accessing the system to file. Agency Comments Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name American Association of Dental Boards BID ID: 14002164 Date Established 11/30/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/30/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Kimber Cobb Start Date: 11/30/24 Current Filer Kimber Cobb Start Date: 11/30/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $20.00 $20.00 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver We are a small non-profit that supports dental regulatory boards. We are out of state and weren't aware that filings are required twice a year. We respectfully request a waiver for the one day of oversight. Additionally, the pay link does not work and we receive an error message. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name American Forest & Paper Association BID ID: 16017158 Date Established 1/01/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/15/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Julie Landry Start Date: Current Filer Frazier William Start Date: Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/15/25 $385 $385 Spring 2025 12/15/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $1385.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509230+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f45a3980-ff28-4b64-9e0a-c16fa1b417a8", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 279, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they were never officially notified of the reporting obligation with the Maryland State Board of Elections by either the Board itself, or our contracted lobbyist, Lorenzo Bellamy. The \"Notice of Possible Filing Obligation\" sent by the SBE and postmarked November 10, 2025 was received in our office on December 10, 2025. They are requesting the fees be waived. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant for $692.50 Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Beckley Enterprise Inc BID ID: 14002028 Date Established 11/12/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Kelly Shanholtzer Start Date: 11/12/24 Current Filer Lisa Schwier Start Date: /12/2411 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $40 $40 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they were unable to get into the system because of the update. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network BID ID: 14001596 Date Established 5/02/22 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Bill Sherman Start Date: 5/03/22 Current Filer Lance Kilpatrick 5/03/22 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/03/25 $40.00 $40.00 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509240+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b155a56c-71b9-4a47-9b7b-be91bb45ced1", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 277, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2025 $100.00 Waived $ Reason for Waiver On May 8, 2025 I submitted a FINAL affidavit indicating my intention to cease reporting because I did not meet the requirements. So, I am surprised to get this. I have left a voicemail and email with the Board. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant and close. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name The Baltimore Orioles Limited Partnership BID ID: 16017141 Date Established 8/01/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/10/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Kerry Watson Start Date: 8/01/23 Current Filer Kamaal Jones Start Date: 8/01/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/09/25 $175 $175 Spring 2025 12/09/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2024 12/069/25 $1000 $1000 Spring 2024 12/09/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2023 12/09/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $4,175.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The business was informed on Novemver10 of the filing requirements. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half $2087.00 Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name CAI-MD Legislative Action Committee BID ID: 16017082 Date Established 11/01/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Igar Conev Start Date: 11/01/25 Current Filer Anita Fowler Start Date: 11/01/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $40.00 $40.00 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509249+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "aeb30994-4e7a-4996-86dc-8debd14c3f8c", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 296, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver Business is a nonprofit with a small budget. They promise to stay in compliance for future filings as they were unaware of filing requirements. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Chesapeake Turf LLC BID ID: 14000352 Date Established 2/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14- Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Gregory Tucker Start Date: Current Filer Cherie Tucker Start Date: Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/02/25 $20.00 $20.00 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $80.00 Paid N/A Fall 2021 $120.00 Paid N/A Reason for Waiver Attempted to file in November when new system went live and there was not a link to do so and could not find the options to file after missing the opportunities for training and did not realize how simple the filing would be after receiving a reminder to file. We will pay if waiver is not granted, but the payment link does not work currently. Thank you! Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name InMarket Media, LLC BID ID: 16017116 Date Established 10/10/2025 Date Waiver Requested 12/02/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Jason Knapp Start Date: 10/10/2025 Current Filer Jason Knapp Start Date: 10/10/2025 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 (1686) 12/02/2025 $20.00 $20.00 Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509259+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "842cb349-d5d5-4b0d-b9bd-b7326d8a188d", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 261, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Registered on 12/01/2025 and were still trying to figure out how to work the system. Agency Comments Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Innosoft Corporation BID ID: 14002078 Date Established 1/28/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Person Doing Public Business Officers Principal Officer Srinivas Challapalli Start Date: 1/28/25 Current Filer Srinivas Challapalli Start Date: 1/28/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/02/25 $20.00 $20.00 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver Entering data in the new BCDS System is delayed as the emails went to Spam. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Linnel Inc BID ID: 14001837 Date Established 04/30/2024 Date Waiver Requested 11/12/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Hafeezah Muhammad Start Date: 04/30/2024 Current Filer Kirnpreeet Dosanjh Gill Start Date: 04/30/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Spring 2025 (526) 11/25/2025 $1,000.00 $1,000.00 Total: $1,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Filer says they created a duplicate account under a different name. They wish to close out this duplicate account. Agency Comments Grant and close.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509268+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "44e3a0a2-ce58-48bf-90f8-1958114df5c6", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 273, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Filer says they created a duplicate account under a different name. They wish to close out this duplicate account. Agency Comments Grant and close. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Lutherville Station LLC BID ID: 14002042 Date Established 11/22/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Mark Renbaum Start Date: 11/22/24 Current Filer Mark Renbaum Start Date: 11/22/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/02/25 $20.00 $20.00 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver I’m so sorry. This was the first time for me doing the filing. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Restaurant Assoc. of Maryland Education Foundation BID ID: 14000569 Date Established 8/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14- Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Marshall Weston Start Date: 8/01/15 Current Filer Elisa Hemgen Start Date: 8/01/15 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/03/2025 $40.00 $40.00 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver This was my first time filing and I was unaware we had two seperate accounts I had to log into. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509278+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "93b75334-3af2-4fc7-ad85-d3e10fe37360", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 297, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver This was my first time filing and I was unaware we had two seperate accounts I had to log into. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Security Land and Development LP BID ID: 14001708 Date Established 1/20/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Conrad Cafritz Start Date: 1/20/23 Current Filer Conrad Cafritz Start Date: 1/20/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $40.00 $40.00 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The original email with the instructions and login information to the new filing system that was rolled out on November 10th was not received or seen. A new email was just sent today, December 3rd and the login to the new system was completed. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Cigna Health and Life Insurance Company BID ID: 14000447 Date Established 2/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Anthony Gruppo Start Date: 2/01/15 Current Filer Jason Loftus Start Date: 2/01/15 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $20 $20 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business state they need the password information to log into the account.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509288+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f3a9c8fc-c07e-4c1a-9ec2-506ed8ac9784", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 269, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business state they need the password information to log into the account. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Southern Management Companies, LLC BID ID: 14000312 Date Established 1/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Monica Tesnow Start Date: 1/01/15 Current Filer Monica Tesnow Start Date: 1/01/15 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/02/25 $20.00 $20.00 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver Unable to log into on-line system to complete report. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Coakley & Williams Construction LLC BID ID: 16017087 Date Established 8/03/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/15/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Terrence Caulfield Start Date: 8/03/23 Current Filer Connor Groh Start Date: 8/03/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $140 $140 $ $ Total: $140.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they are a first-time filer and had no transactions to report. This was an oversight and they will ensure they are compliant in the future.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509297+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c664a340-516e-40ec-868a-4e2eda70e615", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 212, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they are a first-time filer and had no transactions to report. This was an oversight and they will ensure they are compliant in the future. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Daikin US Corporation BID ID: 14002184 Date Established 4/01/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Charlie McCrudden Start Date: 4/01/25 Current Filer Charlie McCrudden Start Date: 4/01/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/03/25 $40 $40 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states the October notices of the new business filing systems were captured by their company's spam filter. There was no intent to avoid filing. They were notified by the consultant to look for the October email and that's when they realized the failed to file. The settings on the spam folder have been changed to recognize emails from the Maryland State Board of Elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509305+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "4618f451-8dfa-44c1-946c-4c16c406325d", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 289, "text": "There was no intent to avoid filing. They were notified by the consultant to look for the October email and that's when they realized the failed to file. The settings on the spam folder have been changed to recognize emails from the Maryland State Board of Elections. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Washington Area New Automobile Dealers Association BID ID: 14000214 Date Established 1/01/2015 Date Waiver Requested 12/05/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer John ODonnell Start Date: 1/01/25 Current Filer John Bloom Start Date: 1/01/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/05/25 $80 $80 $ $ Total: $80.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business had trouble with the new registration system. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System RECONSIDERATION Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Delmarva Chicken Association BID ID: 14002169 Date Established 9/01/22 Date Waiver Requested 1/06/26 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Holly Porter Start Date: 9/01/22 Current Filer Christina Disilvestro Start Date: 9/01/22 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 $1000 $1000 Spring 2024 $1000 $1000 Fall 2023 $1000 $1000 Spring 2023 $1000 $1000 Fall 2022 $1000 $1000 Total:$5,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they are a501 (c) (6) and had thought they were exempt from filing.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509315+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c01c2e6f-af04-4611-8042-6cef07cdd955", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 14, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 286, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they are a501 (c) (6) and had thought they were exempt from filing. Agency Comments Grant EXECUTIVE DEPARTMENT WES MOORE STATE OF MARYLAND GOVERNOR COMMISSION MEMBERS: STATE ETHICS COMMISSION JENNIFER K. ALLGAIR CRAIG D. ROSWELL, Chair Executive Director KAREN D. MORGAN 45 CALVERT STREET, 3rd FLOOR WILLIAM J. COLQUHOUN MARIELA OLIVARES ANNAPOLIS, MARYLAND 21401 General Counsel JAMES N. ROBEY, JR. 410-260-7770 / 1-877-669-6085 KATHERINE P. THOMPSON GENEAU M. THAMES https://ethics.maryland.gov Staff Counsel ANA L. HENRY Assistant General Counsel PANSY S. WATSON Assistant Counsel January 6, 2026 Mr. Allen Norfleet Maryland State Board of Elections 151 West Street, Suite 200 Annapolis, MD 21401 Re: Title 14 Referrals Dear Mr. Norfleet, Please note that the State Ethics Commission will not take additional action under § 5-716 of the Public Ethics Law against Delmarva Chicken Association. They have either filed the required forms or have been notified by our office. The enforcement of this provision is designated under § 5-716(n) as a criminal matter under the jurisdiction of the Office of the State Prosecutor. Please let us know if you have any questions. Sincerely, Katherine P. Thompson Staff Counsel Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Express Scripts Holding Inc BID ID: 14000262 Date Established 1/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Bradley Phillips Start Date: 1/01/15 Current Filer Jason Loftus Start Date: 1/01/15 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $20 $20 $ $ Total: $20.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509328+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ae03f8d9-1328-463e-9bd7-9126d6956bb7", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 15, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 284, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business state they needed the password information to log into the account. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Hill International, Inc. BID ID: 16017057 Date Established 4/04/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/08/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Will Dengler Start Date: 4/04/24 Current Filer Michael Winata Start Date: 4/04/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/04/25 $60 $60 Spring 2025 12/04/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $1,060.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver First time user Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Hyster-Yale Materials Handling, Inc. BID ID: 14002217 Date Established 9/26/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/22/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Chuck Pascarelli Start Date: 9/25/25 Current Filer Liz Brooks Start Date: 9/26/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/22/25 $435 $435 $ $ Total: $435.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they attempted to log into file the final version on 12/11 but did not have access to the new system. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name International Code Council Inc.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509338+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a2b34854-5c3d-468a-8d99-4ce23319f710", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 16, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 275, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states they attempted to log into file the final version on 12/11 but did not have access to the new system. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name International Code Council Inc. BID ID: 16017148 Date Established 7/15/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/16/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Avi Rose Start Date: 7/15/24 Current Filer Avi Rose Start Date: 7/15/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/16/25 $435 $435 $ $ Total: $435.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states the late filing was the result of a misunderstanding of the reporting requirements and exemptions. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Ritchie Land Reclamation LLC BID ID: 16017151 Date Established 7/01/22 Date Waiver Requested 12/16/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Eric Newquist Start Date: 7/01/22 Current Filer Michelle Davis Start Date: 7/01/22 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 12/16/25 $435 $435 Spring 2025 12?16/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2024 12/16/25 $1000 $1000 Spring 2024 12/15/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2023 12/12/25 $1000 $1000 Spring 2023 12/12/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2022 12/12/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $6,435.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509347+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7ae6a312-26cf-426d-a914-733e9fdb68e3", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 17, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 244, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business is filing in response to letter received late in November. They were not aware of the change in the law and the letter from MDSBOE sent these out recently. They did not make any financial contributions during this time and as soon as they were made aware, they called and emailed and tried to figure things out. Upon receiving access to the system, the reports were filed. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Sinai Hospital of Baltimore, Inc BID ID: 14000579 Date Established 8/01/15 Date Waiver Requested 12/15/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Daniel Blum Start Date: 8/01/15 Current Filer Nancy Kane Start Date: 8/01/15 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 $385 $385 $ $ Total: $385.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver Inquiry was made in Sept 2025 regarding whether Sinai Hospital needed to file the report since it is a 510(c)(3) entity. It was confirmed that they did not need to file the report. Therefore, they did not file a report. A FINAL REPORT was filed today. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant and close account.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509356+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d41c4d75-31bc-4caa-8fbd-588ec9f8ae47", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 18, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 231, "text": "It was confirmed that they did not need to file the report. Therefore, they did not file a report. A FINAL REPORT was filed today. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Grant and close account. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Tides At River Marsh LLC BID ID: 16017130 Date Established 1/21/25 Date Waiver Requested 12/30/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Charles Fairchild Start Date: 1/21/25 Current Filer Hunter Fairchald Start Date: 1/21/25 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Spring 2025 12/30/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2025 12/30/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $2000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver Tides at River Marsh LLC is requesting a waiver related to Late Filing Fines for the Spring & Fall 2025 reports. In speaking with our lobbyist (R&R Solutions), we did not believe we were required to file these reports . Ms. Jennifer Williams (Principal with R&R Solutions) consulted with the Maryland Sate Ethics Commission and was under the impression that our entity (Tides at River Marsh LLC) was exempt from the filing requirements. We are a family owned business and the payment of the fee would be a hardship. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509365+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "50d96fa5-8713-40a8-8478-697459a20feb", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 19, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 250, "text": "Ms. Jennifer Williams (Principal with R&R Solutions) consulted with the Maryland Sate Ethics Commission and was under the impression that our entity (Tides at River Marsh LLC) was exempt from the filing requirements. We are a family owned business and the payment of the fee would be a hardship. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name UFCW Local 27 BID ID: 16017025 Date Established 9/01/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/23/24 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Kelly Crone Start Date: 12/23/24 Current Filer Jason Chorpenning Start Date: 12/23/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 11/25/25 $1000 $1000 Spring 2025 11/25/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $2,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP N/A $ $ Reason for Waiver The business states Kelly recently assumed the role of comptroller following the retirement of the prior comptroller, and the transition led to a misunderstanding of reporting deadlines. The late filings were not intentional and was not an attempt to avoid disclosure or compliance with Maryland election law. Once I became aware of the issue, they acted promptly to address the outstanding filings and to bring all reporting obligations into compliance. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Capitol Paving of D.C. Inc.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509375+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "3903d8b8-af09-4eb3-9909-7c3a376ff90f", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 20, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 293, "text": "Once I became aware of the issue, they acted promptly to address the outstanding filings and to bring all reporting obligations into compliance. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Reduce to half. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Capitol Paving of D.C. Inc. BID ID: 14000912 Date Established 05/02/2018 Date Waiver Requested 11/12/2025 Account Type Title 14-Person Doing Public Business Officers Principal Officer Panizo Randolph Start Date: 05/02/2018 Current Filer Francisco Neto Start Date: 05/02/2018 Waiver Request Dates Late Report (Fine ID) Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 (1597) 11/12/2025 $1,000.00 $1,000.00 Total: $1,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $1,000.00 No No Spring 2022 $285.00 No No Spring 2021 $120.00 No No Reason for Waiver Mistakenly thought closing out a contract without closing their account would free them of their filing obligation. Agency Comments Deny, non-compliant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Community Bridge Inc. Profile BID ID: 14001999 Date Established 7/25/24 Date Waiver Requested 12/03/25 Account Type Title 14-Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Denise Shelton Start Date: 7/25/24 Current Filer Angelique Fullwood Start Date: 7/25/24 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 N/A $40.00 $40.00 $ $ Total: $40.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2025 $1000.00 Outstanding N/A $ Reason for Waiver Affidavit was filed on 11/18/2025. There should not be a fine for late filing, because it was filed in advance. Attached is the proof of filing. Please remove the fine. Thank you.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509386+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f8b90d6a-1996-4dc7-a320-389c634fa322", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 21, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 285, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2025 $1000.00 Outstanding N/A $ Reason for Waiver Affidavit was filed on 11/18/2025. There should not be a fine for late filing, because it was filed in advance. Attached is the proof of filing. Please remove the fine. Thank you. Please let us know if there is anything that we have missed. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Deny, business asked for report to be unfiled and filed report late. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name ECA Solar Profile BID ID: 14002220 Date Established 01/22/2025 Date Waiver Requested 11/26/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Todd Fryatt Start Date: 04/30/2024 Current Filer Kaitlin K. Kelly O’Neill Start Date: 04/30/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Spring 2025 (600) 11/26/2025 $1,000.00 $1,000.00 Total: $1,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Business was unaware of filing requirements with SBE until receiving notice from the State Ethics Board. They are now working to be in compliance. Agency Comments Deny, non-compliant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name International Sleep Products Association BID ID: 14001503 Date Established 10/01/2022 Date Waiver Requested 12/01/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employs a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Ryan Trainer Start Date: 10/01/2022 Current Filer Alison Keane Start Date: 10/01/2022 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Spring 2025 (491) 12/01/2025 $1,000.00 $1,000.00 Fall 2024 (398) 12/01/2025 $1,000.00 $1,000.00 Total: $2,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509395+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "86fe3577-4bd3-4b70-b66d-ec1dda293d35", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 22, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 293, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Spring 2024 $1,000.00 No No Fall 2023 $1,000.00 No No Spring 2023 $1,000.00 No No Reason for Waiver Business states that previous filers had left the company and they are now working to get the account into compliance. Agency Comments Deny, non-compliant. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Maryland Association of Green Industries BID ID: 14002036 Date Established 05/01/2024 Date Waiver Requested 12/01/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Current Filer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 11/26/2025 $60.00 $60.00 Total: $60.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Internal system error that prevented them from filing, they worked it out with Devki and Sam and filed as soon as possible. Agency Comments Deny, fees pre-exist new system. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Maryland Grain Producers Association BID ID: 14002038 Date Established 05/01/2024 Date Waiver Requested 12/01/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Current Filer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 11/26/2025 $60.00 $60.00 Total: $60 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Internal system error that prevented them from filing, they worked it out with Devki and Sam and filed as soon as possible. Agency Comments Deny, fees pre-exist new system.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509404+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a73fe628-64a7-4c4e-907d-fb7a27536c8a", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 23, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 300, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Internal system error that prevented them from filing, they worked it out with Devki and Sam and filed as soon as possible. Agency Comments Deny, fees pre-exist new system. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Maryland Green Industries Council BID ID: 14002037 Date Established 05/01/2024 Date Waiver Requested 12/01/2025 Account Type Title 14-Employing a Lobbyist Officers Principal Officer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Current Filer Lindsay Thompson Start Date: 05/01/2024 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 11/26/2025 $60 $60 Total: $60 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees Report Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP Reason for Waiver Internal system error that prevented them from filing, they worked it out with Devki and Sam and filed as soon as possible. Agency Comments Deny, fees pre-exist new system. Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name American Council of Engineering Companies of MD BID ID: 14001941 Date Established 7/01/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/17/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Maria Donovan Start Date: 7/01/23 Current Filer Maria Donovan Start Date: 7/01/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2024 5/28/25 $1000 $1000 $ $ Total: $1000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The Business states at the time this Business Contribution Disclosure was due in the Fall of 2024, the American Council of Engineering Companies' Executive Director had abruptly resigned and there was no staff to complete the filing.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509413+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9978cfe7-905e-4411-bfdc-0e34edd54ae9", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "05b-Title 14 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 24, "chunk_total": 25, "word_count": 220, "text": "Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The Business states at the time this Business Contribution Disclosure was due in the Fall of 2024, the American Council of Engineering Companies' Executive Director had abruptly resigned and there was no staff to complete the filing. The current Filer joined January 13, 2025 and have since entered all filings as required and by the appropriate deadline. They are requesting a waiver of the fine for Fall of 2024. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Denied non-compliant Business Contribution Disclosure System Waiver Request Information Page-Late Fees General Account Name Clear Ballot Group, Inc. BID ID: 14002223 Date Established 9/06/23 Date Waiver Requested 12/11/25 Account Type Title 14-Employing Lobbyist/ Person Doing Business Officers Principal Officer Bob Hoyt Start Date: 9/06/23 Current Filer Matthew Dwyer Start Date: 9/06/23 Waiver Request Dates Late Report Date Received Fees Total Fees Fall 2025 10/31/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2024 10/31/25 $1000 $1000 Spring 2024 10/31/25 $1000 $1000 Fall 2023 10/31/25 $1000 $1000 Total: $4,000.00 All required notices were sent to this campaign account for the above listed report(s). Prior Waiver and Fees R eport Late Fee Waiver/payment Referred OSP $ $ Reason for Waiver The business was recently informed by their lobbyist of the reporting requirements. Division Comments Administrator’s Decision Deny, non-compliant", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.509421+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8da747d0-7d1e-4a7c-8d50-042a91a72abd", "source_url": "local://2026-01/05c-Administrative Closure Request CCF (rev)pdf.pdf", "title": "05c-Administrative Closure Request CCF (rev)pdf", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 473, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nMemorandum\nTO: State Board Members\nFROM: Allen Norfleet, Director\nDivision Candidacy and Campaign Finance\nSUBJECT: Administrative Closure of Campaign Accounts\nDATE: January 22, 2026\nPursuant to §13-313 of the Election Law Article, the State Board has the\nauthority to administratively close a political committee upon determination that\ngood cause exists and when other criteria are met:\n1. The campaign finance entity could be terminated under §13-309 (b)\n(4) of this subtitle except for the existence of one or more outstanding obligations\nand each of those obligations is more than 5 years old;\n2. No responsible officer currently is appointed and serving; or\n3. Other extenuating circumstances exist to justify terminating the\ncampaign finance entity.\n(b.) The termination of a campaign finance entity under this section does not\nlimit the right of:\n(1) The State Board, or the State Prosecutor or the State’s Attorney, to\npursue an enforcement action against the former responsible officers of, or any\ncandidate formerly affiliated with, the campaign finance entity; or\n(2) A creditor to bring an action against the former responsible officers of, or\nany candidate affiliated with, the campaign finance entity\nAttached are forms, which have been prepared for the Board’s review of\naccounts that meet the requirements for administrative closure.\nIf you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me at 410-269-\n2856.\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401\n\nREQUEST TO ADMINISTRATIVELY CLOSE CAMPAIGN ACCOUNT\nAccount Name Baltimore County Victory Slate\nCCF ID Number 2008721\nAccount Established Date 8/24/2006\nOutstanding Debts/Loans 00.00\nCash Balance (741.69)\nBank Account Balance 00.00\nLast Filed Campaign Finance 2026 Annual Report\nReport (CFR)\nOutstanding Late Fees * $3,955.00 with OSP\nOffice of the State Prosecutor (OSP) Action/s Taken:\nOSP investigated and charged the treasurer William McCollum with embezzlement. He was\nindicted for his ongoing theft scheme and plead guilty.\nList name and address of last known officers:\nCandidate Chairperson\nNo active chairperson\nLast known chairperson\nPanopoulos, Phyllis H.\n1913 Lyden Road\nLutherville, MD 21093\nTreasurer\nAaron Bloom Mr. Bloom was kind enough to step in and help\n10045 Red Run Blvd. file the closeout/termination reports.\nSuite 250\nOwings Mills, MD 21117\nBriefly describe why this committee should be closed:\nThis committee should be closed due to the following:\nThe treasurer was found to have embezzled funds from this committee. He was charged and\nfound guilty. It is not feasible to reconcile this account with this knowledge. Surplus funds were\ndonated to The Cavanagh House, Inc. a 501c3 and a registered Maryland Charity.\n*If additional space is required, attach a breakdown of outstanding late fees, including\nreport due dates and outstanding late fees.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:29.599172+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "3d605098-7c1f-4412-8ba2-d02dcb1ecb53", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 234, "text": "Message ID <CAG=V=azMo=N416gZsviD-r+_k=MV8Xjr-sGj6ey JLfdex 7Udqg@mail.gmail.com> From \"Natalie Ogilvie-Watkins To <elections@aacounty.org> Ce a ee Date Mon, Jan 5, 2026 at 03:52 PM (Incoming) Subject Good afternoon:polling question.. Thread Subject Good afternoon: polling question.. Return-Path es Good afternoon! lama voter in AACo and wanted to start by saying thank you all for everything you do. You all have maintained the integrity our elections and | greatly appreciate that. | hope you all had a nice holiday! | wanted to bring something to your attention as | know you will consider the reason behind my concern. It has come to my attention that the earligh heights fire department may be used as a polling spot as it was in the 2024 elections? | believe before that time, the Severna Park Library was utilized for the polling spot. lam aware the fire chief, Dave Crawford, is running for County executive, and although | know you all have no intention to have a conflict of interest as you wouldn't have known he would run when the polling location was changed. | ask if this is true, to Please consider returning to the original polling place of the Severna Park Library so voters can be absolutely sure there isn't a conflict of interest. Thank you so much for your time!! Natalie Ogilvie Watkins Owner & Director https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003399494 1/thread/866699000033994945?action=print 1/14/26, 10:14 AM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003399494 1 /thread/8666990000339949457?actio. .. 11 1/14/26, 10:11 AM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000033907740/thread/866699000033907744?actio...", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649775+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "06b12136-9246-4c9b-86e3-f84b3ea20357", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 281, "text": "Thank you so much for your time!! Natalie Ogilvie Watkins Owner & Director https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003399494 1/thread/866699000033994945?action=print 1/14/26, 10:14 AM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003399494 1 /thread/8666990000339949457?actio. .. 11 1/14/26, 10:11 AM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000033907740/thread/866699000033907744?actio... Message ID <CABNa5rpEO6Fq8eg2R35z7jZarhctqsMiYoabQPt=x_VEOgWEuA@mail.gmail.com> From \"Joan Cole i To <elections@aacounty.org> Ce a OO eeeee——SSSCCis Date Mon, Jan 5, 2026 at 08:01 AM (Incoming) Subject Concern About 2026 Polling Locations Thread Subject Concern About 2026 Polling Locations Return-Path PO Dear Anne Arundel County Board of Elections, Thank you for the work you do to ensure that our elections are fair and safe and to serve our candidates who run in our elections. | understand that your office will be considering proposed voting locations in the coming days, and | would like to express my deep concern, as a voter and candidate in Anne Arundel County, about the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling location this year. While this location served as a good location in 2024, | believe there could be an appearance of a conflict of interest this year because amember of the fire service at this location is running for office. | believe that it would be in the best interest of our county elections to move the polling location to Severna Park Library, where polling has been held previously. While the fire station proved to be a useful polling location in 2024, there are other factors to consider in this decision. The Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station is not a county or state owned public facility, which does not align with county and state efforts to prioritize public over privately owned locations. In addition, and more importantly, this could be a very close election determined by a small number of precincts.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649801+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "df272cb1-307e-4123-8dbd-2751ee06a842", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 278, "text": "The Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station is not a county or state owned public facility, which does not align with county and state efforts to prioritize public over privately owned locations. In addition, and more importantly, this could be a very close election determined by a small number of precincts. With the current state of politics in our nation and the common suggestions and conspiracy theories about our elections being rigged, eliminating this location from the equation will take the pressure off what could be perceived as questionable results in the election. Asa voter and a candidate in this upcoming election, | would greatly appreciate your office eliminating this unnecessary issue as a concern. Thank you for your consideration. Joan Cole Joan Cole Candidate for MD State Delegate - District 31 https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000033907 740/thread/866699000033907744?action=print 11 1/14/26, 4:52 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000341 75807/thread/866699000034 175811 ?action... Message ID <CAJw_A47JAwUyA6TGjKAYGgphuybVDmeV19v2SK6kVLq=23UqvA@mail.gmail.com > From \"Carolyn Matthews a To <elections@aacounty.org> Ce <info.sbe@maryland. Gov > eS Date Thu, Jan 8, 2026 at 02:17 PM (Incoming) Subject 2026 voting location Severna Park Thread Subject 2026 voting location Severna Park Return-Path es Good afternoon | understand that today is the day for determining voter locations for the 2026 elections. As the Earleigh Heights fire station is run by Chief Dave Crawford, one of the candidates for county executive, |add my voice to those requesting a move back to the Severna Park library. This move will make for a more transparent process and avoid any appearance of impropriety. Thank you for your consideration of this matter. And thank you for all your hard work to keep our elections safe. Carolyn Matthews https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000341 75807/thread/8666990000341 75811 ?action=print 1/1 1/14/26, 4:53 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000034664267/thread/866699000034664271 ?action....", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649815+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5365495f-d095-4b60-9189-cdcf06dc841b", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 265, "text": "This move will make for a more transparent process and avoid any appearance of impropriety. Thank you for your consideration of this matter. And thank you for all your hard work to keep our elections safe. Carolyn Matthews https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000341 75807/thread/8666990000341 75811 ?action=print 1/1 1/14/26, 4:53 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000034664267/thread/866699000034664271 ?action.... Message ID <1435223696.4561501.1767913681741@mail.yahoo.com> From “Jessica MacBridc ns To \"“elections@aacounty.org\"<elections@aacounty.org> Ce a ee Ee | f0.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Thu, Jan 8, 2026 at 06:08 PM (Incoming) Subject Consideration of 2026 Polling Locations Thread Subject Consideration of 2026 Polling Locations Return-Path Dear Members of the Anne Arundel County Board of Elections, As a voter in Anne Arundel County, | want to begin by sincerely thanking you for the time, care, and dedication you bring to administering our elections. | truly appreciate the work you do to ensure that our voting process remains fair, accessible, and trustworthy. | understand that you will soon be reviewing polling locations for the 2026 election cycle, and | wanted to share a concern regarding one location used in 2024—the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station. While it served voters well during the last election, | have recently learned that a member of the fire service at that location is planning to run for office in the upcoming cycle. Because of this, | worry there could be an appearance of a conflict of interest, even if none exists. Given the challenges our elections have faced in recent years related to misinformation and public trust, | believe it would be in the best interest of both the candidates and the electorate to avoid even the perception of impropriety.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649829+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8fb7313c-da00-480b-b9a6-53308ce9fa06", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 287, "text": "Because of this, | worry there could be an appearance of a conflict of interest, even if none exists. Given the challenges our elections have faced in recent years related to misinformation and public trust, | believe it would be in the best interest of both the candidates and the electorate to avoid even the perception of impropriety. Returning the polling location to the Severna Park Library for the 2026 cycle would help eliminate any concerns and ensure that the integrity of the process remains beyond question—especially in the event of a close race. | also appreciate that such a move would align with county and state efforts to prioritize publicly owned facilities as polling locations. My hope is simply to support an election process that inspires confidence and protects all involved. Thank you again for your continued service to our county and for thoughtfully considering this request. Respectfully, Jessica MacBride Severna Park, MD https://kb.vote_md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003466426 7/thread/86669900003466427 1?action=print 1/1 1/16/26, 4:37 PM kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504347 7/thread/866699000035043481 action... Message ID <CACVDqge+D+Ytv9GCpsbbrVNvVa_zyZ-myGle4dcVO=KeiObVhNg@mail.gmail.com: From “Luke Tudball To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce a eS Date Thu, Jan 15,2026 at 03:41 PM (Incoming) Subject Please REject Earleigh Heights Fire Station as a Polling Station this Election Cycle Thread Subject Please REject Earleigh Heights Fire Station as a Polling Station this Election Cycle Return-Path Po To Whom It May Concern, lam writing as |am very concerned about the potential use of Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling location in the forthcoming elections this year. know that this location has been used as a polling place in past years but this yea there is a large cause for concern as the current Fire Chief based at that location running as a candidate in the County Executive race.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649842+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "330a0ad1-e6b2-447d-8688-2d013397382f", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 288, "text": "While | have no issue with the gentleman running for office, indeed | applaud his willingness to serve, it certainly rings a number of alarm bells that he, and other voters, would be required and asked to vote in his place of work. This is especially true as this is a high profile, county-wide race, and this location has been used for early voting ir addition to election day itself. |am aware that this location is larger but it is also, ¢ lam led to believe, substantially more expensive than other local locations such Severna Park Public Library which has also been used as a polling location very successfully in the past. lam writing to ask that in your deliberations and decision-making for the forthcoming elections you take into account our strong reservations with this location and reject this location as a polling station this cycle. A much better choice would be, as previously mentioned, the Severna Park Public Library and v ask you to select that location instead. In these challenging times | am aware that your work is difficult and | commend you for the job you do but we must do everything we can to avoid the negative optics surrounding the elections which include being very thoughtful about conflict of interest, implied bias, and unfair influence. Once again, | have no issu whatsoever with the Fire Chief running for office, but | do not believe it is fair or right for the Board of Elections to expect us to cast our votes at his place of work where he could have an outsized influence on proceedings. Thank you for your time and consideration. https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000350434 7 7/thread/86669900003504348 1 ?action=print 1/2 1/16/26, 4:37 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504347 7/thread/866699000035043481 ?action....", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649857+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "dba32a68-f7e5-41f4-aa25-444174a1c7ac", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 257, "text": "Thank you for your time and consideration. https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000350434 7 7/thread/86669900003504348 1 ?action=print 1/2 1/16/26, 4:37 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504347 7/thread/866699000035043481 ?action.... A Yours Luke Tudball https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504 34 7 7/thread/866699000035043481 ?action=print 2/2 1/16/26, 4:38 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504 7246/thread/86669900003504 72507?action.... Message ID From To Date Subject Thread Subject Return-Path Hi Mr. Demaris, <SAOPR20MB3310807B1F 0O2CB62AC7BD5E2E08CA@SAOPR20MB3310.namprd20.p rod.outlook.com> \"Dana Schallheim \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> \"elections@aacounty.org\" <elections@aacounty. 07 is Thu, Jan 15, 2026 at 03:36 PM (Incoming) No Polling Place at Earleigh Heights Fire Station No Polling Place at Earleigh Heights Fire Station | am writing to ask that the SP Library be used as a polling place in lieu of the fire station to avoid even the smallest semblance of conflict interest between the fire station and a candidate who has strong ties with the fire station. During this time of deep uncertainty please make the right decision and choose only the most neutral of locations. Best, Dana https://kb.vote_md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003504 7246/thread/86669900003504 7250? action=print 11 1/16/26, 4:35 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035043739/thread/866699000035043743?action.... Message ID <CAJw_A47PKOz+WOue=ryX ERGKTP26jWO02xpFaU8=G TbQLNhLdXxg@mail.gmail.com > From \"Carolyn Matthews To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Thu, Jan 15, 2026 at 03:43 PM (Incoming) Subject Severna park polling place Thread Subject Severna park polling place Return-Path PO | am writing to express my concern about using the Earleigh Heights VFD as a voting location for the 2026 elections. As the fire station is run by Chief Dave Crawford, one of the candidates for county executive, | add my voice to those requesting a move back to the Severna Park library. This move will make for a more transparent process and avoid any appearance of impropriety.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649869+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "99a5c853-a0a4-41d1-9ea0-5980c8997d56", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 278, "text": "As the fire station is run by Chief Dave Crawford, one of the candidates for county executive, | add my voice to those requesting a move back to the Severna Park library. This move will make for a more transparent process and avoid any appearance of impropriety. While | understand that the size of the building and the traffic pattern at the library has been a concern, | believe the impartiality of the place is more important. | wrote to the county board regarding my concerns, and their answer was that it is no different from using county property if an incumbent were running - it is still the candidate's home turf. If we were planning to hold the elections at the County HQ building, | would agree. But | think the library is sufficiently removed from an official's day-to-day business to make that analogy a stretch. There is no accusation or suspicion about Mr. Crawford's intentions inthis election. It is not about any individual. The request to move is simply a good government caution to keep public trust in an election. Thank you for your consideration of this matter. And thank you for all your hard work to keep our elections safe. Carolyn Matthews Anne Arundel County resident https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035043739/thread/866699000035043743?action=print 11 1/16/26, 4:33 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035083798/thread/866699000035083802?action.... Message ID <658721072.1207657.1768582584966 @mail.yahoo.com> From “Louis Biondi yy To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc “elections@aacounty.org\"<elections@aacounty. org ns po Date Fri, Jan 16, 2026 at 11:56 AM (Incoming) Subject Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station Thread Subject Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station Return-Path Pot | would like to voice my objection to using the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling place in the upcoming election cycle.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649882+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "96fb5e65-12ae-429a-aaf7-0ce68025540c", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 271, "text": "Given the fact that a senior member of the EHVFS is running in the upcoming election, | see a definite possibility that some voters may see this as a potential conflict of interest or undue influence over the election. In these days of conspiracy theories, rumor mongering, and outright lying about election results, | believe the State Board of Elections should go out of its way to ensure the appearance of propriety and choose an alternative polling place. The Severna Park Branch of the Anne Arundel County Library would be a suitable alternative. My wife and | have voted there many times in the past and found it a convenient and comfortable place to vote. Thank you for your consideration in this matter. Louis G. Biondi https://kb.vote_md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035083798/thread/8666990000350838027?action=print 1/1 1/16/26, 4:11 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 2954 7/thread/866699000035129551 ?action. .. Message ID <1895927199.692041.1768591583409 @mail.yahoo.com> From \"Pmoats3561 xn To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Fri, Jan 16, 2026 at 02:26 PM (Incoming) Subject Please consider Severna Park Library Thread Subject Please consider Severna Park Library Return-Path a Mr. Demarinis, For many reasons the Severna Park library would be a better polling place compared to Earleigh Heights Vol.Fire Dept. This is because of the quality of the parking lots of each. (Library's much better). It is also because of traffic flow on E. Earleigh Hts Rd. It is far busier than that on W. McKenzie Rd, in part because of the shopping center that also accesses E. Earleigh Heights. Please seriously consider moving the polling place back to the library. | feel confident most in- person voters, and poll workers will appreciate it. It may be less expensive as well.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649895+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9c989902-a0fe-4ce1-80c2-5c94ecea3bf9", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 287, "text": "Earleigh Hts Rd. It is far busier than that on W. McKenzie Rd, in part because of the shopping center that also accesses E. Earleigh Heights. Please seriously consider moving the polling place back to the library. | feel confident most in- person voters, and poll workers will appreciate it. It may be less expensive as well. Thank you for your consideration. -Pamela Moats https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 2954 7/thread/866699000035129551 ?action=print 11 1/16/26, 2:47 PM State of Maryland Mail - Revised Polling Place Plans | Maryland Revised Polling Place Plans David Garreis -SBE- To: Cyril Komp -SBE- Cc: Richard Siejack - Morgan Rhoden -SBE- Rebecca Browder Tue, Jan 13, 2026 at 10:12 AM Katherine Berry -SBE- elissia Dorsey -SBE- , Cj Our Board received comments about using the Earleigh Height Volunteer Fire Hall in Severna Park due to the Fire Chief running for County Executive in the Republican Primary. The State Board of Elections (SBE) approved this site for Early Voting at last month's meeting. The AAC Board previously discussed this matter during our September 2025 and January 2026 meetings. The Board Attorney advised that all candidates must follow the same rules regarding site access. The Attorney also noted that excluding this site would imply that local boards could not use any public buildings if a County Executive were running for re-election, or schools if School Board members were on the ballot. Anne Arundel County faces significant logistical challenges with the Severna Park Library. The voting room is too small for the required number of election judges, voters, and equipment. Historically, this is one of the busiest early voting sites in the state, yet it lacks adequate parking. Previously, election judges were forced to park at an adjacent nursing home.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649906+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2fd83e94-04e2-4f4a-bf15-9a4e0764beb6", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 EP public comments and response.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 EP public comments and response", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 181, "text": "Anne Arundel County faces significant logistical challenges with the Severna Park Library. The voting room is too small for the required number of election judges, voters, and equipment. Historically, this is one of the busiest early voting sites in the state, yet it lacks adequate parking. Previously, election judges were forced to park at an adjacent nursing home. In contrast, the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Hall is a long-standing and excellent location. It offers a large room and a substantial parking lot with ample overflow space. This provides more room for electioneers and reduces the safety concerns present at the library. It is also centrally located at the corner of Ritchie Highway with easy access from Route 10, Route 100, and public transit. To mitigate any concerns, the Board decided we shall contact all candidates in writing to notify them of the laws and guidelines regarding electioneering, access to the polling room, and the posting of signage. If you have any questions or need further information, please don't hesitate to ask. David [Quoted text hidden] https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/7ik=badd2b88 12&view=pt&search=all&permmsgid=msg-f: 185421492338448 1268&simpl=msg-f 1854214923384481268 1/1", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:43.649915+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "bcd54698-b0b4-45f2-90ff-18995641d6fd", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 271, "text": "1/21/26, 1:44 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035156097/thread/866699000035156101 ?action. .. Message ID <1656268454.1656407.1768700618133@mail.yahoo.com> From \"Barry To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Sat, Jan 17, 2026 at 08:43 PM (Incoming) Subject AA County polling locations Thread Subject AA County polling locations Return-Path a To: JARED DeMARINIS, STATE ADMINISTRATOR OF ELECTIONS Dear Mr. DeMarinis, | understand that the State Board of Elections is meeting this coming week to discuss the placement of polling stations across Maryland including Anne Arundel county. The Anne Arundel County Board of Elections has provisionally approved the use of the Earleigh Heights Fire Station as a polling station. However, a candidate for office is currently a leader at Earleigh Heights. | feel this could create a potential conflict of interest which would be bad for the State of Maryland. Therefore | am asking you not to approve the EHVFS and, instead, choose the Severna Park Public Library on McKinsey Road. The Library has been my polling place for many years and is convenient to a great number of citizens. It is also less expensive. And most important, it would not permit the suspicion of unfair influence in the election. Given the extremes of partisanship at every level of government right now, and the escalation of political rhetoric, specifically calling into question the validity of our election system, the bedrock of our democracy, it is urgent that polling locations, as well as other features of the upcoming election, be without reproach or appearance of favoritism or undue advantage to either side. Thank you for your dedicated work in support of American democracy. Sincerely, Barry Kessler Barry Kessler https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 5609 7/thread/866699000035156101?action=print 11 1/21/26, 1:25 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035139543/thread/866699000035 139547 ?action...", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877125+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "63bea75e-9fb0-4732-86a6-d1de6afe1c7e", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 292, "text": "Thank you for your dedicated work in support of American democracy. Sincerely, Barry Kessler Barry Kessler https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 5609 7/thread/866699000035156101?action=print 11 1/21/26, 1:25 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035139543/thread/866699000035 139547 ?action... Message ID <OF64A9DD-3D13-4A36-AA2F-C9B8B947A346@yahoo.com> From \"Louise Raphac| To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 12:19 PM(Incoming) Subject Neutral polling site Thread Subject Neutral polling site Return-Path PO] Please vote for a neutral polling site. Thank you, Louise Raphael https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035139543/thread/8666990000351 39547 ?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 1:24 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035156548/thread/866699000035156552?action. .. Message ID <CAKAyqDSEmdK2JkVHkhH36D=dW5sZqJ=6SomM7iJa7JuRNaFEtA@mail.gmail.com > From \"Suz Lev To <info.sbe@maryland.gov>,<elections@aacounty.org>,<eoatki22@aacounty.org>, <eoelli21@aacounty.org>,<eoevan23@aacounty.org>,<eorhei23@aacounty.org>, <eopral23@aacounty.org> Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 01:33 PM (Incoming) Subject Polling place conflict Thread Subject Polling place conflict Return-Path Po Dear Mr. Demarinis, lam a resident of Severna Park and live not far from the Earleigh Heights fire station. Dave Crawford, the fire chief at EHVFD, has announced plans to run for County Executive. |am writing to urge you to no longer consider the Earleigh Heights fire station as a polling place for the upcoming elections as this is a direct conflict of interest. | also note it appears that the fire station is building a sign, potentially a campaign sign in front of the station- also a gross conflict of interest. Please consider alternate polling places such as the Severna Park Library. Regards, Suzanne Leveille Severna Park https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035156548/thread/866699000035156552?action=print 11 1/21/26, 12:58 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152508/thread/8666990000351525127?actio. .. Message ID <1510135466.1409365.1768764180883@mail.yahoo.com> From \"Marie McCarren To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc \"elections@aacounty.org\"<elections@aacounty. org nn pd Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 02:23 PM (Incoming) Subject Earleigh Heights Thread Subject Earleigh Heights Return-Path es Dear Sirs/Madams: | am a registered Republican, and my brother-in-law is a long-time volunteer firefighter, but even | don't like the idea of Earleigh Heights Fire Station being used as a polling place when one of the candidates is a firefighter there.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877171+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "688403b8-894b-45a0-beac-2db567e6a2b1", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 297, "text": "Surely we can agree that just doesn't look good. Sincerely, Marie McCarren Armold, MD https://kb.vote_md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152508/thread/866699000035152512?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 12:57 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035156927/thread/866699000035156931?actio... Message ID <62504AE9-7599-418C-B325-B43A22A449E5@gmail.com> From \"Ericamcfarland To <info.sbe@maryland.gov>,<elections@aacounty.org nn SSCs ee Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 03:03 PM (Incoming) Subject Polling location conflict Thread Subject Polling location conflict Return-Path PO Good afternoon, lam writing to alert you to a possible conflict of interest in the upcoming elections. Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Department has been used as a polling place in the past, however, in this election, the Chief of EHVFD is a candidate. | believe that due to this, EHVFD should not be used asa polling location, but instead Severna Park library could be utilized. Please consider the possibility of the conflict of interest that would arise if a polling place is alsoa candidate's workplace. Thanks, Erica McFarland Unaffiliated voter https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 5692 7/thread/8666990000351 56931 ?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 12:57 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154334/thread/8666990000351543387?actio... Message ID <CAChWDHE3L402LQ--4veuRX1La6t_RD-HBASbCi-3S80KydU2hA@mail.gmail.com> From \"Debi Jasen iy To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc <elections@aacounty. org > eS Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 03:06 PM (Incoming) Subject Polling Station - Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station Thread Subject Polling Station - Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station Return-Path PO To Whom It May Concern at the Maryland State Board of Elections; lam greatly concerned that the Earleigh Heights fire station is being considered as a possible polling station, considering that someone in a leadership position there is running for office. The Severna Park library does not employ anyone with a conflict of interest, and is a perfectly adequate voting location. It's also much easier to enforce electioneering rules and counter voter intimidation methods at Severna Park library than it is at the Earleigh Heights fire station. Please do not approve the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling location this year.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877185+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "742d9292-4049-45c5-bcd2-659a9a3d8d90", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 293, "text": "It's also much easier to enforce electioneering rules and counter voter intimidation methods at Severna Park library than it is at the Earleigh Heights fire station. Please do not approve the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling location this year. Thank you for your consideration. Sincerely, Debi Jasen https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154334/thread/8666990000351543387?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 12:56 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035139846/thread/8666990000351398507?actio... Message ID <621030415.958669.1768767741646@mail.yahoo.com> From \"Khristine Smith i To NOADDRESS Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 03:22 PM (Incoming) Subject concerns about earleigh heights fire department continuing to be used as a polling station Thread Subject concerns about earleigh heights fire department continuing to be used as a polling station Return-Path Co Good afternoon, | am writing to you to please consider taking the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire department off the list of potential polling places this coming November election. | am a voter and Anne Arundel county resident. | lived and voted in Severna park many many years. Polling locations should not have a sense of conflict of interest. polling places should be neutral and safe for everyone to feel comfortable voting. This November, a man named Dave Crawford is running for Anne Arundel County Executive, he is also on the leadership team and volunteer firefighter at the Earleigh Heights fire department. This should not be allowed. He will easily be able to persuade or make voters uncomfortable when they go in, he may even have his campaign signs up and visible closer to the voter booths then legally allowed. Severna Park library on McKinnsey Rad. is local and neutral. They have parking and additional parking right behind at the senior living place. | know you are meeting this week to consider polling locations, | urge you to keep this in mind when deciding for the county/state.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877197+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ebfbc7c4-4edb-445d-a197-9a6ebfdf7b72", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 289, "text": "Severna Park library on McKinnsey Rad. is local and neutral. They have parking and additional parking right behind at the senior living place. | know you are meeting this week to consider polling locations, | urge you to keep this in mind when deciding for the county/state. Thank you very much, Khristine Smith Khristine Smith https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035139846/thread/8666990000351398507?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 12:56 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035164424/thread/8666990000351644287?actio... Message ID <CABzhFFSzpQcOvKZ6COMsWUbnbTotHchYhOtV6VvaGrqZzXd3Tg@mail.gmail.com > From \"Dawn Myers To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce <elections@aacounty. or ¢ > eS Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 04:52 PM (Incoming) Subject Earleigh Heights Fire Department Voting Location Thread Subject Earleigh Heights Fire Department Voting Location Return-Path Dear Members of the Maryland State Board of Elections, lam writing to respectfully object to the proposed relocation of on of the voting sites serving my district from the Severna Park Library on McKinsey Road to the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Department. The Severna Park Library has long served as a familiar, centrally located, and accessible early voting site for voters in this area. Moving the location to the outskirts of the district creates an unnecessary barrier for voters, particularly those with limited transportation options, mobility concerns, or constrained work schedules. From a voter-access standpoint alone, the change is difficult to justify when a proven and well- known alternative is available. Additionally, | understand the cost of the move is significantly more. As we are substantially cutting County Government, this needs to be aconcern as well. More importantly, |am concerned about the appearance of a conflict of interest associated with using the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Department as a polling location in the current election cycle. The Fire Department has publicly endorsed candidates in the past, and at present, its chief is himself a candidate for public office.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877213+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "628e397c-7e1e-4ae1-8735-d8f523fee9ad", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 202, "text": "More importantly, |am concerned about the appearance of a conflict of interest associated with using the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Department as a polling location in the current election cycle. The Fire Department has publicly endorsed candidates in the past, and at present, its chief is himself a candidate for public office. While | do not suggest any actual impropriety, the optics of situating a polling place ata facility so closely associated with an active candidate are troubling. In an era when public confidence in elections is under strain, election administration must not only be fair but must be clearly and unmistakably perceivedas fair. Avoiding even the appearance of partiality or undue influence is essential to maintaining trust in what has historically been a rock-solid electoral process in Maryland. Given these concerns, | respectfully urge the State Board not to approve the use of the Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Department as a voting location and instead to retain or reinstate the Severna Park Library on McKinsey Road, which has successfully served voters in prior elections and does not raise similar concerns. Thank you for your careful consideration of this matter and for your continued work to ensure accessible, impartial, and trustworthy elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877222+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "cf339988-c6f8-486e-ae47-30e66b358f4e", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 295, "text": "Thank you for your careful consideration of this matter and for your continued work to ensure accessible, impartial, and trustworthy elections. Dawn Myers https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035164424/thread/866699000035164428?action=print 11 Message ID <CAFod5huG6ePYnWNZs7F=dU3QLYMrGzZHoSPxFMEW2wL8hTKQEA@mail.gmail.co m> From \"Ruth Stem ber ger ae To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce <elections@aacounty. or ¢ > eS Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 05:32 PM (Incoming) Subject Earliegh Heights Fire Station and avoiding a conflict of interest Thread Subject Earliegh Heights Fire Station and avoiding a conflict of interest Return-Path es Dear Board of Elections members, With the potential conflict of interest between the EHVFS and the candidate running for election who isa leader at the EHVFS, | urge you to make the public library in Severna Park our polling place, and to not approve the fire station as a polling place. Thank you for showing your support for avoiding any possible conflict of interest in these times wherelections must be free and fair and polling places must be independent of any particular candidate. Kind regards, Ruth Stemberger Email is not a secure method of communication. Please be aware of this when determining the type of information you include in emails and/or that you request that | include in emails to you. If you request that | use email to communicate with you, | will consider that informed consent for me to use this method of communication and that you understand the security risks of doing so. The email contains confidential information. This email is for the intended recipient only. If you receive this email in error, please reply to notify me that you were not the intended recipient and delete the email. Thank you. https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152859/thread/8666990000351528637?action=print 1/21/26, 12:55 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152859/thread/8666990000351528637?actio... 11 1/21/26, 12:55 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154807/thread/866699000035154811 ?actio.... Message ID From To Cc Date Subject Thread Subject Return-Path <9351CA51-D97F-4753-8A68-66F65185CA95@gmail.com> 4 \"Dr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877237+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "07c338c8-b370-4c14-b777-bfa6c7c0fa46", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 264, "text": "This email is for the intended recipient only. If you receive this email in error, please reply to notify me that you were not the intended recipient and delete the email. Thank you. https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152859/thread/8666990000351528637?action=print 1/21/26, 12:55 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035152859/thread/8666990000351528637?actio... 11 1/21/26, 12:55 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154807/thread/866699000035154811 ?actio.... Message ID From To Cc Date Subject Thread Subject Return-Path <9351CA51-D97F-4753-8A68-66F65185CA95@gmail.com> 4 \"Dr. Benna Z Sherman \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> “elections@aacounty.org\"<elections@aacounty. org Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 07:47 PM (Incoming) choice of polling place choice of polling place In these challenging times we do not want there to be any semblance of conflict of interest or unfair influence over the elective process. The EHVFS would clearly be associated positively with one of the candidates. I’m asking you to choose Severna Park Public Library rather than the EHVFS as our polling station. Thank you for your consideration. Benna Z Sherman, PhD Licensed Psychologist Severna Park MD 21146 General email: Patient email: https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154807/thread/8666990000351 54811 ?action=print 1/21/26, 12:55 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035154807/thread/866699000035154811 ?actio.... ee a Author of \"How to Get and Give Love-- Relationship Maps\" Facebo0k | as CONFIDENTIALITY NOTICE: Please note that the confidentiality or security of email cannot be guaranteed. Please restrict email communication to administrative matters. Your continued use of email constitutes your acknowledgement of these confidentially and security limitations. The most secure way of contacting me is by voicemail or via Secure Messaging in your Clier Portal at benna-sherman.clientsecure.me \"Three things in human life are important. The first is to be kind. The second is to be kind. The third is to kind.\" -- Henry James https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003515480/7/thread/8666990000351548 11 ?action=print 2/2 1/21/26, 12:54 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035169025/thread/8666990000351690297actio....", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877251+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "839aa055-85de-49ff-ae01-4e1c2d26e947", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 291, "text": "The most secure way of contacting me is by voicemail or via Secure Messaging in your Clier Portal at benna-sherman.clientsecure.me \"Three things in human life are important. The first is to be kind. The second is to be kind. The third is to kind.\" -- Henry James https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003515480/7/thread/8666990000351548 11 ?action=print 2/2 1/21/26, 12:54 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035169025/thread/8666990000351690297actio.... Message ID <CAAzn4Xut+qKqnYmns9N3BeETLh- OVOgtAL7VyGFPqG=Xd4Cp4tQ@mail.gmail.com> From \"Fish Stark To <elections@aacounty. org > B= Date Sun, Jan 18, 2026 at 08:57 PM (Incoming) Subject Anne Arundel County Polling Locations Thread Subject Anne Arundel County Polling Locations Return-Path Po Dear Maryland State Board of Elections, Asa voter in Anne Arundel County and the Vice Chair of the Annapolis Democratic Party, | write to urge you to reject Earleigh Heights Volunteer Fire Station as a polling location and replace it with Severna Park Public Library. A candidate for office in Anne Arundel County is the Chief of the Earleigh Heights Fire Department and is promoting this on his literature. To be clear, this is not a concern; he has every right to highlight this experience to voters. However, it means the Earleigh Heights Fire Department is no longer a politically neutral location in this particular election and therefore is an inappropriate place for a polling station. Best, Fish Stark https://kb.vote_md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035169025/thread/8666990000351690297?action=print 1/1 1/21/26, 12:53 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035166424/thread/8666990000351664287?actio... Message ID <CADgsYje680eTWu9uuMjFg1KhFwOYt7U=LqqdZ6NOA59VkFy8sQ@mail.gmail.com> From \"Nancy P Anselm To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc <elections@aacounty. org > eS Date Mon, Jan 19, 2026 at 10:35 AM(Incoming) Subject Polling Place Severna Park Public Library Thread Subject Polling Place Severna Park Public Library Return-Path PO To: JARED DeMARINIS, STATE ADMINISTRATOR OF ELECTIONS Dear Mr. DeMarinis, I understand that the State Board of Elections is meeting this coming week to discuss the placement of polling stations across Maryland including Anne Arundel county.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877263+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "06ad5d8f-4f60-40c5-8341-239ed5546d73", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 222, "text": "DeMarinis, I understand that the State Board of Elections is meeting this coming week to discuss the placement of polling stations across Maryland including Anne Arundel county. The Anne Arundel County Board of Elections has provisionally approved the use of the Earleigh Heights Fire Station as a polling station. However, a candidate for office is currently a leader at Earleigh Heights. I feel this could create a potential conflict of interest which would be bad for the State of Maryland. Therefore I am asking you not to approve the EHVFS and, instead, choose the Severna Park Public Library on McKinsey Road. The Library has been my polling place for many years and is convenient to a great number of citizens. It is also less expensive. And most importantly, it would not permit the suspicion of unfair influence in the election. Given the extremes of partisanship at every level of government right now, and the escalation of political rhetoric, specifically calling into question the validity of our election system, the bedrock of our democracy, it is urgent that polling locations, as well as other features of the upcoming election, be without reproach or appearance of favoritism or undue advantage to either side. Thank you for your dedicated work in support of American democracy. Sincerely, Nancy Anselm https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035166424/thread/866699000035166428?action=print 11 1/21/26, 12:52 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 78789/thread/8666990000351787937?actio...", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877272+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "cae3ab3e-1d13-4ba2-82f0-4bcb5ae6b9a7", "source_url": "local://2026-01/AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2.pdf", "title": "AA 2026 Election Plan additional public comments part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 290, "text": "Thank you for your dedicated work in support of American democracy. Sincerely, Nancy Anselm https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035166424/thread/866699000035166428?action=print 11 1/21/26, 12:52 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 78789/thread/8666990000351787937?actio... Message ID <633D1296-810E-4F B3-A54B-8DD2ED83CBEA@kzmarketing.com> From \"KZ Marketing is To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Mon, Jan 19, 2026 at 01:22 PM (Incoming) Subject Please use the SP Library rather than EHVFS for polling Thread Subject Please use the SP Library rather than EHVFS for polling Return-Path ro Hello - While | applaud the diversity of candidates and am also an enthusiastic supporter of Earleigh Heights, it seems there is a conflict of interest to use Earleigh Heights Fire Station as a polling station when one of the candidates is on the Board, as it would for any candidate who has influence at the facility. Please use SP Library as the polling location, rather than Earleigh Heights Fire Station to avoid the potential conflict or even appearance of conflict. This will help ensure elections are free and fair, without influence. Thank you. Karen Zaniker, SP resident Sent from my iPhone Karen Zaniker https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 78789/thread/8666990000351 78793?action=print 11 1/21/26, 12:51 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 881 99/thread/8666990000351882037?actio.... Message ID <CAAroGTNkCRy7O6mwXOxibEQNawwntm YehNWAe- GUBHESWoQBtA@mail.gmail.com> From i To <info.sbe@ maryland. gov nnn a ee Date Mon, Jan 19, 2026 at 02:54 PM (Incoming) Subject Earleigh Heights Fire Station polling concern Thread Subject Earleigh Heights Fire Station polling concern Return-Path Po To Whom it May Concern lama registered voter, and am concerned with Earleigh Heights Fire Station being used as a polling place when one of the candidates is a firefighter there. This could lead to awkward interactions and possible suspicions about the election there. Could you use the Severna Park Library, which has been used in previous years as a polling location? Sincerely, M Kelly Arnold, MD https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000351 881 99/thread/8666990000351882037?action=print 1/1", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:08:58.877284+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "59a8b0f5-2b4f-40f8-8da0-4356b6bf88ed", "source_url": "local://2026-01/Election Plan Board Presentation Part 1.pdf", "title": "Election Plan Board Presentation Part 1", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 47, "word_count": 258, "text": "PART 3 2 0 2 6 E l e c t i o n P l a n Early Voting Centers, Ballot Drop Boxes, and Polling Place Plans PART 2 L o cal B oar d s of E le ct ions I n cl u d e d i n t h is R ep or t : Anne Arundel County (Polling Place Plans) (pending ) Baltimore County (pending ) Frederick County (included) Prince George's County (included) ELECTION PLAN REQUIREMENTS Purpose: In 2025, HB0274 was passed which changed the process of approving early voting centers, ballot drop boxes, and polling plans. Previously, each section was submitted and approved individually. Now, all three sections are presented together for approval. This report includes all three sections under the guidance of the following regulations*: Election Plan, General: ▫ 33.01.08.01- 04 General Provisions, Election Plan Early Voting Centers: ▫ 33.17.02.02 Early Voting, Early Voting Centers, Selecting Early Voting Centers Ballot Drop Boxes: ▫ 33.11.01.04 Absentee Ballots, Definitions; General Provisions; Ballot Drop Boxes Polling Place Plans: ▫ 33.15.03.01 Polling Places, Required Plan ▫ 33.15.03.02 Polling Places, New or Changed Polling Place *links to DSD website where the most updated version of COMAR can be searched for the above listed regulations. Recommendation: Approve F r e d e r i c k EARLY VOTING CENTERS • Required Number of EVCs 5 • Optional Number of EVCs: 1 • Total Number of EVCs: 5 Changes from 2024? Two new EVCs from 2024. One of the new locations was used in a prior election cycle.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:03.515701+00:00"}
