06 No have always voted at CENTER - GYM this location Redistricting, avoid ELMWOOD voter confusion, voters 14005 ELEMENTARY 531 DALE AVE No 14007 No have always voted at SCHOOL-GYM this location Major Construction & redistricting.14-10 & 14-14 will be moving RED HOUSE RUN from Overlea High 14011 1717 WEYBURN RD No 14010 Yes ELEM - CAFE school to Red House Run for the 2026 cycle.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.747595+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c2ee559f-21f8-4bbd-bbb4-a68a893acade", "source_url": "local://2026-01/Election Plan Presentation - Part 2.pdf", "title": "Election Plan Presentation - Part 2", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 43, "chunk_total": 44, "word_count": 270, "text": "The new location is 1.6 miles away Moving from a private location to a public NOTTINGHAM location for the 2026 14013 MIDDLE SCHOOL - 5210 KING AVE No 14003 Yes Election Cycle. The CAFE new location is 0.7 miles away. Major Construction & redistricting.14-10 & 14-14 will be moving RED HOUSE RUN from Overlea High 14014 ELEMENTARY 1717 WEYBURN RD No 14010 Yes school to Red House SCHOOL-CAFE Run for the 2026 cycle. The new location is 1.6 miles Redistricting, avoid HAWTHORNE voter confusion, voters 15009 ELEMENTARY 125 KINGSTON RD No 15010 No have always voted at SCHOOL - GYM this location Redistricting, avoid ESSEX voter confusion, voters 15014 ELEMENTARY 100 MACE AVENUE No 15013 No have always voted at SCHOOL-GYM this location Redistricting, avoid SANDALWOOD voter confusion, voters 15023 ELEMENTARY 900 S. MARLYN AVE No 15017 No have always voted at SCHOOL-CAFE this location STEMBRIDGE Redistricting, avoid COMMUNITY 99 STEMMERS RUN voter confusion, voters 15024 No 15011 No CENTER - RD have always voted at CONFERENCE RM this location SENECA Redistricting, avoid ELEMENTARY 545 CARROLLWOOD voter confusion, voters 15026 No 15008 No SCHOOL - RD have always voted at CAFETERIA this location Redistricting, avoid MARTIN BLVD voter confusion, voters 15027 ELEMENTARY 210 RIVERTON RD No 15005 No have always voted at SCHOOL - GYM this location Redistricting, avoid MIDDLE RIVER 800 MIDDLE RIVER voter confusion, voters 15028 MIDDLE No 15004 No ROAD have always voted at SCHOOL-CAFE this location Baltimore County - Precinct Reduction Info N/A Baltimore County - Voter Density Map Baltimore County - Minority Population Map Baltimore County - Income Map Baltimore County - Voter Turnout Map", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.747605+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "3c87137b-dc55-49cb-9f97-b5641a54d0d0", "source_url": "local://2026-01/January State Board of Election comments 2026.pdf", "title": "January State Board of Election comments 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 376, "text": "My name is Kelly Foster and I live in Frederick Maryland. Thank you for the\nopportunity to make public comment today. I attend many of the Frederick County\nBoard of Elections meetings. Over time, I realized that decisions were being made\nin executive sessions after the open meeting was completed. I began filing\ncomplaints with the Open Meetings Compliance Board. The Compliance Board\nfound that Frederick County has violated the Act 6 times.\nIn a recent opinion, the Compliance Board declined to find the Frederick County\nBoard of Elections in violation when they did not provide documents which they\nplanned to vote on during their meeting. The Compliance Board declined because\nthe Act does not cover local boards of Election. The Local Boards of Election\nTransparency Act became Election law 2-208 last year and requires local boards to\nprovide documents they plan to vote on during meetings.\nI am asking that your board remind all local boards that they are required by\nElection Law 2-208 to provide documents to the public before taking a vote. I\nrequest that your board remind the local boards that Closing Statements are part\nof the public record and should be provided to the public when they are asked.\nRecently, Howard County would not even respond to a request for their closing\nstatement during a recent complaint to the compliance board.\nTransparency is essential in elections. SecurethevoteMD requested parity\ninformation on Election Judges from all jurisdictions. Only a handful of\njurisdictions provided information. MPIA requests were ignored by many. Two\ncounties required payment of more than $300 for the lists to redact personal\ninformation, while others provided the information for free. Where is the equity?\nI requested parity information from Frederick and was originally told there was no\nresponsive document. When I pointed to Election Law 10-203, I was told that the\nBoard has delegated the approval to the Election director. This seems to be a clear\nviolation of Election Law and possibly the Open Meetings Act. Frederick was not\nthe only county where the board never voted to approve Election Judge parity list\nduring an Open meeting.\n\nI thank you for addressing election judge party with policy 2025-04. Can\nsomething be done about Open Meetings Act Compliance for local boards?", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.793527+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "38804bef-462b-4e99-979c-c946534d857c", "source_url": "local://2026-01/Language Assistance Memo 2026.docx", "title": "Language Assistance Memo 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_docx", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 433, "text": "To: \t\tState Board of Elections\n\nDate:\t\tJanuary 22, 2026\n\nRe: \t\tDetermination of Significant Need for Language Assistance\n\nNew State Law: Election Law Article, Title 15.5\n\nIn 2025, the General Assembly and Governor enacted Chs. 277, 278, Acts 2025, Establishing the Language Assistance Program, codified in Title 15.5 of the Election Law, creating language assistance requirements in State elections. This law sets forth obligations for local boards of elections in counties with a “significant need for language assistance” based on the State Board’s determinations. The law provides the standards for the State Board to use in determining whether a “significant need for language assistance” exists in any given county, as well as the languages for which the need exists. Title 15.5–101 directs the State Board to find a significant need for language assistance wherever the best available data, including the U.S. Census Bureau’s American Community Survey or data of comparable quality, show that in a county:\n\nMore than 2% of the citizen voting age population has limited English proficiency and uses a single shared language other than English; or\n\nMore than 4,000 individuals in the citizen voting age population have limited English proficiency and use a single shared language other than English.\n\nBest Available Data\n\nAs Maryland’s law explicitly acknowledges, the U.S. Census Bureau’s survey data are widely recognized as the highest-quality source of statistical knowledge about the United States. The Census Bureau’s American Community Survey supplies annually-updated information based on a large sample with a high response rate, for small geographies and concerning detailed topics that include each of the datapoints necessary to administer the language assistance law.\n\nThe State Board of Elections’ staff determined that while the American Community Survey data contain the information necessary to determine where a significant need for language assistance exists in Maryland, the published version of these data does not provide the necessary geographical detail. To obtain counts of citizen voting age population by English proficiency and language spoken and by Maryland county, the State Board requested a custom tabulation. This request was submitted to the Census Bureau’s American Community Survey Office in August 2025. The Bureau delivered the custom tabulation in December 2025.\n\nResults\n\nAccording to the best available data, there are five local jurisdictions with a significant need for language assistance in 2026. In each instance, there is at least one group of more than 4,000 voting-age citizens who are limited English proficient and who use a single non-English language. In addition, Spanish-speaking voting-age citizens who are limited-English proficient constitute more than 2% of the citizen voting-age population in both Montgomery and Prince George’s Counties.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.797404+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5e1d76de-1626-4dab-8c56-8cb1e03156ba", "source_url": "local://2026-01/Policy 2025-03 PSR Request Policy.pdf", "title": "Policy 2025-03 PSR Request Policy", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 253, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nPolicy 2025-03 PSR Request Policy\nIt is the policy of the State Board of Elections to administer and maintain voter registration\nrecords accurately and transparently. In doing so, the State Board must balance the requirements\nfor public disclosure of registration records imposed by laws like Election Law § 3-505 against\nvoters’ privacy interest in the personally identifying information they provide for registration. No\none should be dissuaded from registering to vote because of the fear of identity theft or\nunwarranted intrusion into their private life.\nTherefore, the State Board of Elections provides the following policy for disclosure of voter\nregistration applications pursuant to public information requests.\nThe following information should be redacted from disclosure before any registration\napplication is released publicly:\n1. Maryland Driver’s License Number\n2. Social Security Number\n3. Personal Phone Number\n4. Personal Email Address\n5. Agency through which the Voter Registered or Updated their Registration\nAll other information contained in the voter registration application may be disclosed\nwithout redaction.\nAll PSR requests that are countywide or by district requests will be charged $75 and\nstatewide data requests will be charged $125. Individual record requests will be charged as a\ncountywide request after 2 hours of data gathering has occurred for a requester that sends\nmultiple requests.\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.834813+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0fcc77bd-df20-4831-ae92-00a8f5c5b047", "source_url": "local://2026-01/Policy 2025-04 Election Judge Parity Reporting.pdf", "title": "Policy 2025-04 Election Judge Parity Reporting", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-01"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 123, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nPolicy 2025-04 Election Judge Parity Reporting\nPursuant to Election Law Article §10-201, the Election Director shall assign election judges to\nachieve parity to the extent practicable at each polling place. In order to assist in the recruitment\nof election judges, the Election Director shall provide a vacancy report monthly to their local\nboard of elections members beginning in February 2026. The vacancy report should be a\nstatistical report which includes the polling place and party breakdown by election judge type.\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.857970+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "14c860ba-ce98-4b73-9a28-53fb02859980", "source_url": "local://2026-02/01.13.26 Transmittal letter from Howard County to State Board re Amendment to Bylaws.pdf", "title": "01.13.26 Transmittal letter from Howard County to State Board re Amendment to Bylaws", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 229, "text": "Ann M. Balcerzak, Esq. - President Guy C. Mickley - Director\nDonna K. Thewes - Vice President Charlotte B. Davis - Deputy Director\nTimothy J. Mummert, Esq. - Member\nRaymond M. Rankin – Member https://www.howardcountymd.gov/board-elections\nPaul P. Rivers – Member www.facebook.com/hocoboe\nLorraine Lawrence-Whittaker, Esq. – Counsel @HoCoBOEMaryland\nBOARD OF ELECTIONS\nJanuary 16, 2026\nVia Email Only\nMaryland State Board of Elections\nAttn: Jared DeMarinis, State Administrator\n151 West Street\nSuite 200\nAnnapolis, MD 21401\nRE: Amendment to Bylaws of the Howard County Board of Elections\nDear Mr. DeMarinis:\nEnclosed is a proposed amendment to Article 3, Section 3.2, of the Bylaws\nof the Howard County Board of Elections which was considered and approved by\nthe Board at its regularly scheduled meeting on December 9, 2025. The proposed\namendment is respectfully submitted to the State Board of Elections for its review\nand approval pursuant to Section 2-202(b)(4) of the Election Law Article of the\nAnnotated Code of Maryland.\nPlease do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions or require\nadditional information.\nVery truly yours,\nLorraine Lawrence-Whittaker, Esq.\nCounsel, Howard County Board of Elections\nEnc: Amendment to Bylaws\ncc: Ann M. Balcerzak, Esq.\nDonna K. Thewes\nTimothy J. Mummert, Esq.\nRaymond M. Rankin\nPaul P. Rivers\nGuy Mickley\n9770 PATUXENT WOODS DR, STE 200  COLUMBIA, MARYLAND 21046\nOFFICE: 410-313-5820  FAX: 410-313-5899  TTY (MD Relay): 711", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.885105+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "bc2ff5f0-a027-4cf7-9946-73b42030ba37", "source_url": "local://2026-02/01.16.26 proposed Amendment of Article 3, Section 3.2 of the Bylaws Of The Howard County Board of Elections approved 12.09.25.pdf", "title": "01.16.26 proposed Amendment of Article 3, Section 3.2 of the Bylaws Of The Howard County Board of Elections approved 12.09.25", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 240, "text": "Proposed Amendment of the Bylaws Of The Howard County Board of\nElections approved by the Board at a regularly scheduled meeting on\nDecember 9, 2025:\nI. Article and Section of Bylaws amended: Article 3- Meetings, Section 3.2 –\nRules of Order, Subsection B – Participation in Meetings, Paragraph 3.\nThis paragraph currently reads as follows:\n3. Public participation at Board meetings is allowed without advance notice.\nAn individual may speak for no more than three (3) minutes and an individual\nrepresenting a group may speak for not more than five (5) minutes. Written\ncomments are accepted prior to the meeting or during the public comment\nportion. The public comment portion of the meeting shall not exceed fifteen\n(15) minutes.\nThe proposed Amendment to read as follows:\n3. Public participation at {Board} A meeting{s} is allowed {without advance\nnotice} WITH ADVANCED NOTIFICATION NO LATER THAN 4:00 P.M. ON\nTHE THURSDAY PRIOR TO THE MEETING. THE BOARD PRESIDENT\nSHALL SCHEDULE AND PRE-APPROVE PUBLIC PARTICIPATION. THE\nPRESIDENT IN HIS/HER/THEIR DISCRETION MAY ALLOW PUBLIC\nPARTICIPATION EVEN IF THE REQUEST WAS NOT SCHEDULED AND\nPRE-APPROVED. An individual may speak for no more than three (3)\nminutes and an individual representing a group may speak for not more than\n{five (5)} THREE (3) minutes. Written comments are accepted prior to the\nmeeting {or during the public comment portion} WITHIN THE ABOVE TIME\nFRAME. The public {comment} portion of the meeting shall not exceed\nfifteen (15) minutes.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.904864+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ef988d38-1c9b-47a3-af46-6db5d5d6f640", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02 Agenda.pdf", "title": "02 Agenda", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 305, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nEric Bryant\nState Board of Elections’ Meeting\n151 West Street, Suite 200, Annapolis, MD 21401\nFebruary 19, 2026 2:00 p.m. Virtual\n1. Declaration of Quorum Present\n2. Additions to the Agenda\n3. Approval of Minutes\na. Open Meeting January 22, 2026\nb. Closed Meeting January 22, 2025\n4. State Administrator’s Report\n5. Assistant Attorney General’s Report\n6. Title 13 Waiver Requests\n7. Title 14 Waiver Requests\n8. Confidentiality Requests\n9. Proposed Regulations\na. CCF\ni. 33.22.01.01 Disclosure by Person Employing a Lobbyist, Statement of\nContributions, Registration\nii. 33.22.02.01 Disclosure by Person Employing a Lobbyist, Penalties, Late\nFiling Fee\nb. Challenger and Watcher\n10. Election Plan Approvals\na. Baltimore City\nb. Prince George’s County\n11. Howard County By-Laws\n12. Language Assistance Supplemental for Montgomery County\n13. Voting System State Certification\n14. Old Business\na. Postmarks on Mail-In Ballots\nb. Policy 2025-03 - PSR Request Policy\n15. New Business\n16. Disclosure of Campaign Contributions\n17. Schedule Next Meeting\n18. Closed Meeting\n19. Adjournment\nClosed Meeting: Part of the meeting may be closed in accordance with Open Meetings\nAct procedures.\nPublic Participation: Members of the public may address the Board. Pursuant to §3.2B\nof the Board’s bylaws, public participation at a meeting must be pre-scheduled and pre-\napproved by the Chairman. To request approval to speak at a board meeting, contact\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401\n\n02 Agenda\n2/19/2026\nPage 2 of 2\nShermaine Walker at 410-269-2840 or by email info.sbe@maryland.gov no later than 5\npm the day before the meeting.\nLive Streaming: A link to watch the meeting will be available on SBE’s website shortly\nbefore the meeting starts", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:10.966065+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9099192b-a3a8-4924-841b-0a6b1fa3fa85", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 287, "text": "State of Maryland State Board of Elections – January 22, 2026 Meeting Attendees: (Via Conference Call) Michael Summers, Chair Jim Shalleck, Vice Chair Diane Butler, Member Victoria Jackson-Stanley, Member Eric Bryant, Member Dan Kobrin, Assistant Attorney General Jared DeMarinis, State Administrator Melissia Dorsey, Assistant Deputy of Election Policy and Voter Operations Morgan Rhoden, Director of Communications Allen Norfleet, Director of Candidacy and Campaign Finance Division David Garreis, Anne Arundel County Election Director Ruie Lavoie, Baltimore County Election Director DECLARATIONW OenFd Qy UHOoRneUsMty -PBReEy,S PErNinTc e George’s County Election Director Mr. Summers declared that a quorum was present at 1:03 PM. He welcomed Eric Bryant as a new mADemDIbTeIrO tNo Sth TeO B ToaHrEd .A MGrE. NBDryAa nt expressed gratitude for the opportunity to serve. TAhPePrRe OwVeAreL nOoF a OdPdiEtNio nAsN tDo tChLeO aSgEeDnd Ma.I NUTES – 12/18/2025 Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the minutes for the open meeting from the December 18, 2025 board meeting. Mr. Shalleck moved to approve and was seconded by Ms. Jackson-Stanley. The minutes were approved unanimously. Mr. Summers requested a motion to approve the minutes for the closed meeting from December 18, 2025. Ms. Butler requested the approval be deferred to the next meeting to allow more time f or review and correction. Mr. Summers deferred the approval of the closed meeting minutes uSTnAtiTl tEh Ae DFeMbIrNuIaSrTy RmAeTeOtiRn’gS REPORT Mr. DeMarinis provided a high-level overview of the written report. Announcements & Important Meetings The General Assembly session has begun. HB260 (Fair Campaign Finance), a departmental bill, aimed at the distribution of fair campaign finance funds, was the first bill heard by the new Labor Elections Government Committee. There was also a hearing regarding the management of the address confidentiality program.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385019+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c92b9c22-380f-43cd-bc20-154fb545cef7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 288, "text": "DeMarinis provided a high-level overview of the written report. Announcements & Important Meetings The General Assembly session has begun. HB260 (Fair Campaign Finance), a departmental bill, aimed at the distribution of fair campaign finance funds, was the first bill heard by the new Labor Elections Government Committee. There was also a hearing regarding the management of the address confidentiality program. Candidacy and Campaign Finance Division The filing deadline for the annual reports was January 21. Over 83% of committees filed timely. Staff are providing support for late filers. Mr. DeMarinis noted that many committees waited until State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 2 of 9 the last second to log in. While there were learning curves with the new filing template, he stated that stakeholders generally appreciated the clarity of the new system. Public Outreach An aggressive voter outreach campaign is launching, focusing on voter education and election judge recruitment. SBE is developing a new app for the 2026 election. The app is intended to provide voters with easy access to information on registration, how to become an election judge, methods of voting, and critical deadlines. Procurements The new voting system procurement is tracking normally. The e-pollbook procurement award has been given, and gap analysis is underway for the 2028 election. Mr. DeMarinis did not exercise delegated duties. Mr. Shalleck asked about user reception of the new campaign finance reporting system. Mr. DeMarinis noted some learning curves but overall positive feedback regarding the new system. Mr. Shalleck asked whether a company was selected for the new voting system. Mr. DeMarinis stated that a vendor has not yet been selected. Ms. Butler requested that voter outreach ads include instructions on checking voter registration to reduce provisional ballots.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385039+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7e9ce096-12c5-451c-a31c-9280d036c790", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 298, "text": "Mr. DeMarinis noted some learning curves but overall positive feedback regarding the new system. Mr. Shalleck asked whether a company was selected for the new voting system. Mr. DeMarinis stated that a vendor has not yet been selected. Ms. Butler requested that voter outreach ads include instructions on checking voter registration to reduce provisional ballots. She also inquired about the removal of voters from the permanent absentee list and statistics regarding non-citizen voting referrals. Mr. DeMarinis explained that letters are being sent to voters on the permanent mail-in ballot list who have not voted by mail in the last two elections. The letter informs them they are being removed from the permanent list but clarifies that their voter registration status remains active. Ms. Butler asked if this letter could be combined with information for inactive voters. Mr. DeMarinis clarified they must be kept separate because the recipients are active voters who simply chose a different method of voting (e.g., early voting or Election Day). Ms. Butler requested clarification on statistics regarding non-citizen referrals. Mr. DeMarinis confirmed that 19 referrals were sent to the prosecutor's office, and 18 were removed. The data indicated that 16 non-citizens voted, and 7 voted numerous times. Mr. DeMarinis clarified that numerous times referred to voting in different elections, not multiple times within a single election. Ms. Jackson-Stanley discussed the good faith effort standard for waiving late fees related to the nAeSwSI SsyTsAteNmT. ATTORNEY GENERAL’S REPORT Sullivan, et al., v. Summers, et al. 1. , No. 1:24-CV-00172 (D. Md.). Katherine Strauch Sullivan and David Morsberger, two registered voters from Maryland, filed suit against SBE in the federal district court in Maryland alleging that the Board’s current regulation defining “electoral process” for voter registration list purposes violates the National Voter Registration Act and First Amendment.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385052+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "789d91f8-73a4-4774-8b46-e36f322ab41a", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 275, "text": "Summers, et al. 1. , No. 1:24-CV-00172 (D. Md.). Katherine Strauch Sullivan and David Morsberger, two registered voters from Maryland, filed suit against SBE in the federal district court in Maryland alleging that the Board’s current regulation defining “electoral process” for voter registration list purposes violates the National Voter Registration Act and First Amendment. The complaint, filed January 18, 2024, seeks declaratory and injunctive relief restricting enforcement of the regulation. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 3 of 9 On March 8, 2024, OAG filed a motion to dismiss on behalf of SBE. The plaintiffs filed a response in opposition on March 29, 2024. OAG filed a final reply on April 12, 2024. On August 30, 2024, plaintiffs filed a motion for summary judgment on a stipulated set of facts. OAG replied in opposition to the motion on September 13, 2024. The plaintiffs filed a reply to that opposition on September 23, 2024. On March 4, 2025, the district court issued a memorandum opinion and order granting plaintiffs’ motion as to Count I; and, denying SBE’s motion as to Count I. The court ruled that the regulation is preempted by the National Voter Registration Act. The parties were also ordered to file a status report on their positions relating to the remaining counts. On March 28, 2025, the parties filed a joint status report requesting the court dismiss the outstanding claims. The Court dismissed the outstanding claims, and issued a final judgment on Count I, on October 30, 2025. On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385063+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "4581ce23-3cea-4335-951f-8517c823ffb7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 296, "text": "On March 28, 2025, the parties filed a joint status report requesting the court dismiss the outstanding claims. The Court dismissed the outstanding claims, and issued a final judgment on Count I, on October 30, 2025. On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees. That agreement, which has been executed by the parties, will be submitted to the Board of Public WoBrrkiasn f oDre flilnaa Rl oacpcpar ov.v Saul.s an Lee, et al. 2. , No. 25-1873 (4th Cir.)- On December 2, 2024, Brian Della Rocca filed a complaint against the Secretary of State of Maryland, Chairperson of the Maryland State Board of Elections, and Postmaster General of the United States, seeking to enjoin Maryland from using mail-in ballots in the conduct of its elections. Specifically, the complaint asserts that the United States Postal Service is so unreliable in delivering election mail to- and from voters, that its use to conduct mail-in balloting violated voters rights under the First and Fourteenth Amendments. The plaintiff served the suit on Secretary Lee and Chairperson Summers on January 16, 2025. OAG filed a motion to dismiss the suit on behalf of both state officials on February 6, 2025. Plaintiff opposed the motion; OAG filed a reply to the opposition on March 5, 2025. On April 11, 2025, the United States’ Postmaster General, through the United States Attorney’s Office, filed a motion to dismiss. Plaintiff opposed the Postmaster General’s motion on June 09, 2025. On July 15, 2025, the district court granted both motions to dismiss, ruling that the plaintiff did not possess standing to maintain the claim. Mr. Della Rocca appealed the matter the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385075+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "bf90e96d-cc6a-444f-abc4-913dea11aa1b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 296, "text": "Plaintiff opposed the Postmaster General’s motion on June 09, 2025. On July 15, 2025, the district court granted both motions to dismiss, ruling that the plaintiff did not possess standing to maintain the claim. Mr. Della Rocca appealed the matter the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit. Appellant’s brief was filed on October 8, 2025. OAG filed a brief on behalf the appellees on November 10, 2025. Mr. Della Rocca filed a reply brief on December 1, 2025. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 4 of 9 On January 16, 2026, Mr. Della Rocca filed a letter under Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 28(j) noting supplemental authority for the case. OAG is drafting a letter in resCpaolinfosren. ia, et al., v. Trump, et al., 3. 1:25-CV-010810 (D. Mass)- On April 3, 2025, nineteen states, including Maryland, filed a civil complaint in the federal district court for the District of Massachusetts against the President, the United States Attorney General, the Election Assistance Commission, and the United States Secretary of Defense. The complaint alleges, amongst seven counts, that the Executive Order imposing certain requirements on the conduct of voter registration and federal elections is unlawful. On April 23, 2025, the defendants moved to consolidate the lawsuit with a second set of lawsuits filed in Washington D.C. that challenged the executive order. The court denied that motion on May 9, 2025. On May 5, 2025, the plaintiff states asked the court for a preliminary injunction to pause implementation of provisions of the Executive Order while the lawsuit was pending. The court granted the preliminary injunction after a hearing. The defendants have appealed the grant of the preliminary injunction to the First Circuit. Separately, on June 13, 2025, the defendants moved to dismiss the complaint.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385086+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "dfc7607d-4049-4899-be4d-a979bf89ea6b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 291, "text": "The court granted the preliminary injunction after a hearing. The defendants have appealed the grant of the preliminary injunction to the First Circuit. Separately, on June 13, 2025, the defendants moved to dismiss the complaint. The plaintiff states filed a response in opposition on July 11, 2025. The Court conducted a hearing on the motion on September 4, 2025. On September 17, the Court issued a memorandum opinion and order denying the motion to dismiss. Thereafter, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. On Friday, December 12, 2025, the parties filed cross-motions for summary judgment. OAG worked with SBE to provide a factual declaration on Maryland’s behalf in Csuaplifpoorrnti ao,f etth ae lp., lva.i Tnrtiuffm-spt,a ette sa’l .m, otion for summary judgment. 4. 1:25-CV-010810 (1st Cir.)- This is the appeal of the preliminary injunction granted in the above matter. On October 7, 2025, the Government filed appellants’ brief. However, soon after, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. Appellees’ brief was filed with the court on January 5, 2025. Appellants’ brief in repSleyr eisn ad uBer ybsyo nJa, neut aarl.y, v2. 6W, 2es0t2le6y. Moore, et al. 5. , No. C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections. The suit alleges that the registered voters’ electoral rights, granted by the Maryland Constitution, are violated by their inability to vote in state-run, partisan, primary elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385097+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a6c9acb6-41e0-471c-9dc7-7c0712ff05e1", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 272, "text": "C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections. The suit alleges that the registered voters’ electoral rights, granted by the Maryland Constitution, are violated by their inability to vote in state-run, partisan, primary elections. The voters accordingly seek declaratory judgment and an injunction preventing State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 5 of 9 the State from “conducting, organizing or funding State primary elections in which unaffiliated voters are prohibited from voting.” OAG filed a motion to dismiss the complaint on July 30, 2025. Plaintiffs filed an opposition to the motion on August 29, 2025. A hearing on the motion took place on October 20, 2025. On November 14, 2025, the Court granted the motion, dismissing the case in a memorandum opinion. Plaintiffs have appealed the case to the Appellate Court of Maryland. On January 12, 2026, the unaffiliated voters filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in the Supreme Court of Maryland. The petition asks the Supreme Court to hear the appeal now, rather than waiting to potentially entertain the appeal after a judgment by the Appellate Court of Maryland. OAG is presently drafting an answer to the petition, which is dTuhee tUon tihteed S Sutparteesm oef CAomuerrti coan vo. rJ abreefdo rDee JManaurianriys 27, 2026. 6. , No. 1:25-CV-03934 (D. Md.) On December 3, 2025, the United States Department of Justice filed suit against the State Administrator of Elections over his response to the department’s multiple demands to disclose Maryland’s unredacted voter registration database.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385108+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2345020e-2dec-4c33-a3c7-5c90f4942ef7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 299, "text": "Md.) On December 3, 2025, the United States Department of Justice filed suit against the State Administrator of Elections over his response to the department’s multiple demands to disclose Maryland’s unredacted voter registration database. The complaint alleges that the State is in violation of a provision of the Civil Rights Act of 1960 in declining to hand over the unredacted information, as demanded. With the complaint, DOJ filed a motion to compel records pursuant to the Civil Rights Act of 1960. The motion advocates to dispense with normal process and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure, and instead (in a summary fashion) compel production of the voter database. By agreement between the parties, OAG must file a response to both the complaint anRde tphueb mlicoatnio Nn aotnio onra bl Cefoomrem Jiatnteuea, reyt 3al0. ,v 2. 0Ja2r6e.d DeMarinis, et al. 7. , No. 1:25- CV-03989 (D. Md.) On December 5, 2025, the national and state entities of the Republican Party and two Republican voters registered in Maryland sued the State Administrator of Elections, State Board, and the local boards of elections for Montgomery and Howard County. The complaint alleges that election officials in Maryland are violating the National Voter Registration Act (“NVRA”) by failing to make reasonable efforts to remove ineligible voters from the voter registration database. In support of that allegation, the complaint cites estimated population statistics and compares them to SBE’s reported voter registration totals. On January 12, 2026, the Democratic National Committee moved to intervene in the case as a defendant. The plaintiffs did not oppose the motion to intervene. OAG, on behalf of SBE, took no position on the motion. The motion remains pending before the Court. By agreement between the parties, OAG must file a response to the complaint on or before January 30, 2026.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385118+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1bebcc31-aaed-4f59-85b2-ac8fedcb137d", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 278, "text": "The plaintiffs did not oppose the motion to intervene. OAG, on behalf of SBE, took no position on the motion. The motion remains pending before the Court. By agreement between the parties, OAG must file a response to the complaint on or before January 30, 2026. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 6 of 9 TITLE 13 WAIVERS Mr. Norfleet presented waiver requests for late filing fees. He explained that Title 13 applies to candidate committees, Political Action Committees (PACs), super PACs, and ballot issue committees. Mr. Norfleet noted that his auditors often work with these committees for months to resolve issues. The division takes difficult life circumstances into consideration when recommending approvals. Mr. Norfleet recommended two approvals and one denial. Mr. Summers requested a motion to accept the recommendation. The motion was moved by Ms. Butler and seconded by Ms. Jackson- Stanley. The motion was approved unanimously. Ms. Butler expressed gratitude to Mr. Norfleet for the division's hard work in assisting candidates wTIhToL mE a1y4 b We AstIrVuEgRglSin g with the filing process or facing personal difficulties. Mr. Norfleet presented the waiver requests for Title 14, explaining that this category applies to business entities that either employ a lobbyist in the General Assembly or hold government contracts with the state, county, or municipality valued at over $200,000. Mr. Norfleet noted a significant increase in the volume of requests due to the implementation of the new filing system and a successful outreach initiative. By cross-referencing data with the Ethics Commission, the division identified and registered 275 new filers who had previously failed to report Mr. Norfleet presented a recommendation for 30 approvals and 9 denials. Ms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385129+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c55cc708-38b6-4e1c-b0db-71637a8dce5f", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 290, "text": "Mr. Norfleet noted a significant increase in the volume of requests due to the implementation of the new filing system and a successful outreach initiative. By cross-referencing data with the Ethics Commission, the division identified and registered 275 new filers who had previously failed to report Mr. Norfleet presented a recommendation for 30 approvals and 9 denials. Ms. Jackson-Stanley inquired about the criteria distinguishing the waivers recommended for approval from those recommended for denial, particularly regarding complaints about the new system. Mr. DeMarinis explained that unlike campaign treasurers who file frequently, Title 14 filers often only report twice a year and may lack familiarity with the process. The Board grants some leeway on technical difficulties. Mr. DeMarinis clarified that the 9 recommended denials involved filers who failed to demonstrate a good faith effort, such as waiting until after the deadline to attempt filing or blaming technical issues without validity. Mr. Bryant recused himself from Baltimore Orioles, Washington Area New Automobile Dealers, and Express Scripts due to prior professional relationships. Ms. Jackson-Stanley recused herself from Tides at River Marsh LLC. Tides at River Marsh LLC was approved with four members in favor and one recusal. Baltimore Orioles, Washington Area New Automobile Dealers, and Express Scripts were approved with four members in favor and one recusal. T he remaining items were approved unanimously. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 7 of 9 ADMINISTATIVE CLOSURES Mr. Norfleet noted that while this is not a common occurrence, the Board has the authority to administratively close committees. The case presented during the meeting involved a slate committee that had been the victim of theft. Mr. Norfleet explained that the perpetrator (Mr. McCollum) had served time for the crime, and an assigned treasurer, Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385139+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d64688f1-8179-47fa-807f-a639ff51276b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 298, "text": "Norfleet noted that while this is not a common occurrence, the Board has the authority to administratively close committees. The case presented during the meeting involved a slate committee that had been the victim of theft. Mr. Norfleet explained that the perpetrator (Mr. McCollum) had served time for the crime, and an assigned treasurer, Mr. Bloom, had successfully cleaned up the campaign's finances, which included making residual donations to charities in Baltimore County. TFIhNeA BLo aRrEdG vUoLteAdT uIOnaNnSi mously to approve the administrative closure for this committee. Ms. Dorsey presented regulations for final approval. There were no public comments. A . 33.02.03.05 (Election Judges Training): Implements law requiring service during voting to be pa id for training. Approved unanimously by the Board. B.33.04.02.01 (Confidentiality): Clarifies three categories of confidential voters. Approved unanimously by the Board. C. 33.07.04.02 (Cell Phones): Allows election judges to use cell phones for translation/interpretation services. Approved unanimously by the Board. D. 33.07.07.01–.03 (Challengers and Watchers): Updated the regulation language and requirements. Ms. Butler and Mr. Shalleck expressed concern that state parties had not been sufficiently consulted or provided feedback, as discussed in previous meetings. Ms. Jackson- Stanley and Mr. Summers noted the regulation went through the standard public comment period without objection. Ms. Jackson-Stanley moved to approve and Mr. Bryant seconded. There were three In Favor, one Opposed (Ms. Butler), and one Abstention (Mr. Shalleck). The motion failed. E. 33.07.11.02 (Compensation of Election Judges): Clarifies that students receiving credit cannot al so be compensated. Approved unanimous:ly by the Board. F.33.07.12 (Language Assistance Program) Implements the 2025 law regarding thresholds for non-English materials and translat:ion. Approved unanimously by the Board. G. 33.08.01.02 (Canvassing Board) Updates regulations to reflect pre-election canvassing schedules. Approved unanimously by the Board. H. 33.08.01.02-1 (Board Counsel): Updates regarding Board Counsel terms.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385151+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "79d19f4e-0aa9-4fec-a52c-a20dd7bd4505", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 298, "text": "Approved unanimous:ly by the Board. F.33.07.12 (Language Assistance Program) Implements the 2025 law regarding thresholds for non-English materials and translat:ion. Approved unanimously by the Board. G. 33.08.01.02 (Canvassing Board) Updates regulations to reflect pre-election canvassing schedules. Approved unanimously by the Board. H. 33.08.01.02-1 (Board Counsel): Updates regarding Board Counsel terms. Approved unanimously by the Board. I. 33.11.03.08 (Absentee Ballots): Clarifies handling of mail-in ballots received after the close of canvass (stored separately, not presented to Board). Approved unanimously by the Board. J. 33.16.05.03 (Provisional Ballots): Allows counting of provisional ballots for Board of Education contests even if the voter voted the wrong party ballot. Approved unanimously by the Board. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 8 of 9 K . 33.21.05.04 (Special Elections by Mail): Adjusts timing for mailing ballots from 14 to 30 days to mLAaNtcGhU laAwG.E A ApSpSrIoSvTeAd NuCnEan imously by the Board. The program implements a law passed by the General Assembly in 2025. The program applies to counties that meet a specific threshold regarding the number or percentage of residents who speak a language other than English as their primary language. Counties that meet these thresholds must provide online materials, translation services and bilingual election judges. The Board unanimously approved a request authorizing the State Administrator to act on behalf of Anne Arundel, Baltimore City, Baltimore County, Prince George's, and Montgomery County to aEdLdErCeTsIsO laNn PguLaAgNeS b arriers. Ms. Rhoden presented the election plans for approval for each county. Anne Arundel County: Conditional approval granted for Early Heights �irehouse location. The candidate associated with the location must ensure no campaign materials/photos are present within the 100-foot zone, and the Director must certify no other viable location exist. Approved unanimously with conditions. Baltimore City: Partial approval (Early Voting Centers and Drop Boxes only).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385162+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "957a03e6-32b2-49f3-9310-bcdd1b2f773f", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 297, "text": "Anne Arundel County: Conditional approval granted for Early Heights �irehouse location. The candidate associated with the location must ensure no campaign materials/photos are present within the 100-foot zone, and the Director must certify no other viable location exist. Approved unanimously with conditions. Baltimore City: Partial approval (Early Voting Centers and Drop Boxes only). Polling places deferred. Approved unanimously. Baltimore County: Ms. Butler and Mr. Bryant raised concerns that 50% of polling places are outside their designated precincts. Director Ruie Lavoie explained that this was due to County Council Districts going from 7 to 9, yet Board of Education Districts remaining at 7. She kept voters in their familiar districts to avoid any more confusion. Approved unanimously. Frederick County: Approved unanimously. Prince George’s County: Similar concerns raised regarding polling places outside precincts. Director Wendy Honesty-Bey con�irmed extensive surveys found no other options. Approved Policy 2025-04 (Election Judge Parity Report) unanimously. Requires election directors to provide monthly vacancy reports by party to local boards to ensure p arity is achieved where practicable. Mr. Shalleck made a motion to approve and the motion was sOeLcDon BdUedSI bNyE MSSs . Butler. Approved unanimously. Policy 2025-03 PSR Request the PSR (Public Service Request) policy addresses the public disclosure of voter registration lists and is distinct from standard Public Information Act (PIA) requests for individual records. The proposed policy aims to establish uniformity across all 24 local boards regarding costs and the specific redaction of personal identifying information (PII), such as social security numbers, driver’s license numbers, personal phone numbers, email addresses, and the specific agency where a voter registered. State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 9 of 9 Ms. Butler and Mr. Shalleck stated that the policy title created confusion between PIA (Public Information Act) and PSR (Voter List) requests.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385174+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ec8f4409-2161-4def-a0b7-2fdf4048260c", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 14, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 299, "text": "State Board of Elections, January 22, 2026 Page 9 of 9 Ms. Butler and Mr. Shalleck stated that the policy title created confusion between PIA (Public Information Act) and PSR (Voter List) requests. There were also objections to subjective language regarding identity theft Mr. Summers deferred the policy to February to allow for rewriting the title, separating fNeEdWer aBl/UsStaINteE rSeSq uirements, and removing opinionated language. Mr. Shalleck raised a concern regarding a new USPS policy where mail is \"processed\" rather than \"postmarked\" immediately upon drop-off. This creates risks for mail-in ballots which must be postmarked by Election Day. Mr. Bryant concurred, noting potential for litigation. Ms. Butler asked if the Board was going to address specific portions of the State Administrator's report that were not covered in the previous meeting, as well as the status of an audit. Ms. Butler inquired about the Office of Legislative Audits (OLA) schedule. Mr. DeMarinis clarified tPhUaBt OLILCA C sOetM tMheE sNcThe dule for the audit and granted a delay due to the election cycle. Kelly Foster, who resides in Frederick County, addressed the Board regarding Open Meetings Act compliance by local boards. She cited violations in Frederick County and requested the State Board remind local boards of their obligations under the Transparency Act (Election Law 2-208) tDoI SpCroLvOidSUe RdoEc OumF CenAtMs PbeAfIoGrNe vCoOteNsT. RIBUTIONS TSChHerEeD wUeLrEe NnoE XdTis cMloEsEuTreIsN oGf campaign contributions. TCLhOe SnEexDt MboEaErEdT mINeeGt ing is scheduled for February 19, 2026, at 2:00 PM. Mr. Summers requested a motion to move to closed session under General Provisions Article § 3- 305(b) to consult with counsel, discuss competitive bidding, and discuss potential litigation. A motion was made by Ms. Butler and seconded by Ms. Jackson-Stanley to close the public mADeeJOtiUngR aNnMdE mNoTv e into a closed session. The motion passed unanimously.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385188+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "db63eeaa-e7b3-47e1-a90c-c928ae28d3e8", "source_url": "local://2026-02/02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT.pdf", "title": "02_Open Meeting Minutes 1.22.2026 rev_DRAFT", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 15, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 87, "text": "Mr. Summers requested a motion to move to closed session under General Provisions Article § 3- 305(b) to consult with counsel, discuss competitive bidding, and discuss potential litigation. A motion was made by Ms. Butler and seconded by Ms. Jackson-Stanley to close the public mADeeJOtiUngR aNnMdE mNoTv e into a closed session. The motion passed unanimously. A motion to adjourn was moved by Mr. Shalleck, seconded by Ms. Butler, and approved unanimously. The open session was recessed at 3.:30 PM, subject to reconvening in a closed session.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.385195+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "8fadf2e9-3948-4886-b1e1-66a533ec5a89", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 274, "text": "ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL’S REPORT February 19, 2026 1. Sullivan, et al., v. Summers, et al., No. 1:24-CV-00172 (D. Md.). Katherine Strauch Sullivan and David Morsberger, two registered voters from Maryland, filed suit against SBE in the federal district court in Maryland alleging that the Board’s current regulation defining “electoral process” for voter registration list purposes violates the National Voter Registration Act and First Amendment. The complaint, filed January 18, 2024, seeks declaratory and injunctive relief restricting enforcement of the regulation. On March 8, 2024, OAG filed a motion to dismiss on behalf of SBE. The plaintiffs filed a response in opposition on March 29, 2024. OAG filed a final reply on April 12, 2024. On August 30, 2024, plaintiffs filed a motion for summary judgment on a stipulated set of facts. OAG replied in opposition to the motion on September 13, 2024. The plaintiffs filed a reply to that opposition on September 23, 2024. On March 4, 2025, the district court issued a memorandum opinion and order granting plaintiffs’ motion as to Count I; and, denying SBE’s motion as to Count I. The court ruled that the regulation is preempted by the National Voter Registration Act. The parties were also ordered to file a status report on their positions relating to the remaining counts. On March 28, 2025, the parties filed a joint status report requesting the court dismiss the outstanding claims. The Court dismissed the outstanding claims, and issued a final judgment on Count I, on October 30, 2025. On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557220+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "39b26e3e-2bed-4798-8bfd-ab9038e81607", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 286, "text": "On March 28, 2025, the parties filed a joint status report requesting the court dismiss the outstanding claims. The Court dismissed the outstanding claims, and issued a final judgment on Count I, on October 30, 2025. On November 13, 2025, plaintiffs filed a motion requesting attorneys’ fees. The parties thereafter negotiated a settlement agreement for the payment of attorneys’ fees. That agreement, which has been executed by the parties, is being considered by the Board of Public Works for final approval at their February 18, 2026 meeting. 2. Brian Della Rocca v. Susan Lee, et al., No. 25-1873 (4th Cir.)- On December 2, 2024, Brian Della Rocca filed a complaint against the Secretary of State of Maryland, Chairperson of the Maryland State Board of Elections, and Postmaster General of the United States, seeking to enjoin Maryland from using mail-in ballots in the conduct of its elections. Specifically, the complaint asserts that the United States Postal Service is so unreliable in delivering election mail to- and from voters, that its use to conduct mail-in balloting violated voters rights under the First and Fourteenth Amendments. The plaintiff served the suit on Secretary Lee and Chairperson Summers on January 16, 2025. OAG filed a motion to dismiss the suit on behalf of both state officials on February 6, 2025. Plaintiff opposed the motion; OAG filed a reply to the opposition on March 5, 2025. On April 11, 2025, the United States’ Postmaster General, through the United States Attorney’s Office, filed a motion to dismiss. Plaintiff opposed the Postmaster General’s motion on June 09, 2025. On July 15, 2025, the district court granted both motions to dismiss, ruling that the plaintiff did not possess standing to maintain the claim. Mr.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557239+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e8b897cf-9bac-4e7c-b1d6-ecf9330d552b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 299, "text": "On April 11, 2025, the United States’ Postmaster General, through the United States Attorney’s Office, filed a motion to dismiss. Plaintiff opposed the Postmaster General’s motion on June 09, 2025. On July 15, 2025, the district court granted both motions to dismiss, ruling that the plaintiff did not possess standing to maintain the claim. Mr. Della Rocca appealed the matter the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit. Appellant’s brief was filed on October 8, 2025. OAG filed a brief on behalf the appellees on November 10, 2025. Mr. Della Rocca filed a reply brief on December 1, 2025. On January 16, 2026, Mr. Della Rocca filed a letter under Federal Rule of Appellate Procedure 28(j) noting supplemental authority for the case. OAG drafted a letter in response and filed it on January 26, 2026. 3. California, et al., v. Trump, et al., 1:25-CV-010810 (D. Mass)- On April 3, 2025, nineteen states, including Maryland, filed a civil complaint in the federal district court for the District of Massachusetts against the President, the United States Attorney General, the Election Assistance Commission, and the United States Secretary of Defense. The complaint alleges, amongst seven counts, that the Executive Order imposing certain requirements on the conduct of voter registration and federal elections is unlawful. On April 23, 2025, the defendants moved to consolidate the lawsuit with a second set of lawsuits filed in Washington D.C. that challenged the executive order. The court denied that motion on May 9, 2025. On May 5, 2025, the plaintiff states asked the court for a preliminary injunction to pause implementation of provisions of the Executive Order while the lawsuit was pending. The court granted the preliminary injunction after a hearing. The defendants have appealed the grant of the preliminary injunction to the First Circuit.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557251+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c951a1fd-72b0-4b99-89ea-1f3964745038", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 296, "text": "The court denied that motion on May 9, 2025. On May 5, 2025, the plaintiff states asked the court for a preliminary injunction to pause implementation of provisions of the Executive Order while the lawsuit was pending. The court granted the preliminary injunction after a hearing. The defendants have appealed the grant of the preliminary injunction to the First Circuit. Separately, on June 13, 2025, the defendants moved to dismiss the complaint. The plaintiff states filed a response in opposition on July 11, 2025. The Court conducted a hearing on the motion on September 4, 2025. On September 17, the Court issued a memorandum opinion and order denying the motion to dismiss. February 19, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report Thereafter, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. On Friday, December 12, 2025, the parties filed cross-motions for summary judgment. OAG worked with SBE to provide a factual declaration on Maryland’s behalf in support of the plaintiff-states’ motion for summary judgment. 4. California, et al., v. Trump, et al., 1:25-CV-010810 (1st Cir.)- This is the appeal of the preliminary injunction granted in the above matter. On October 7, 2025, the Government filed appellants’ brief. However, soon after, the proceedings were stayed at the government’s request due to the lapse in appropriations for the Department of Justice. That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. The case is now fully briefed. 5. Serena Bryson, et al., v. Westley Moore, et al., No. C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557263+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6658ae79-b33c-4bc7-b9b8-2f2e5362c84d", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 286, "text": "That stay lifted when appropriations were reallocated to the Department. The case is now fully briefed. 5. Serena Bryson, et al., v. Westley Moore, et al., No. C-02-CV-25-001586 (Cir. Ct. Anne Arundel County). On June 2, 2025, five registered voters unaffiliated with a political party in Maryland served a civil action on the Governor and State Board of Elections. The suit alleges that the registered voters’ electoral rights, granted by the Maryland Constitution, are violated by their inability to vote in state-run, partisan, primary elections. The voters accordingly seek declaratory judgment and an injunction preventing the State from “conducting, organizing or funding State primary elections in which unaffiliated voters are prohibited from voting.” OAG filed a motion to dismiss the complaint on July 30, 2025. Plaintiffs filed an opposition to the motion on August 29, 2025. A hearing on the motion took place on October 20, 2025. On November 14, 2025, the Court granted the motion, dismissing the case in a memorandum opinion. Plaintiffs have appealed the case to the Appellate Court of Maryland. On January 12, 2026, the unaffiliated voters filed a petition for a writ of certiorari in the Supreme Court of Maryland. The petition asks the Supreme Court to hear the appeal now, rather than waiting to potentially entertain the appeal after a judgment by the Appellate Court of Maryland. OAG filed an answer to the petition on January 27, 2026. February 19, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report 6. The United States of America v. Jared DeMarinis, No. 1:25-CV-03934 (D.Md.) On December 3, 2025, the United States Department of Justice filed suit against the State Administrator of Elections over his response to the department’s multiple demands to disclose Maryland’s unredacted voter registration database.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557274+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "613101bd-2590-4787-b4d3-c308d2b17900", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 284, "text": "February 19, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report 6. The United States of America v. Jared DeMarinis, No. 1:25-CV-03934 (D.Md.) On December 3, 2025, the United States Department of Justice filed suit against the State Administrator of Elections over his response to the department’s multiple demands to disclose Maryland’s unredacted voter registration database. The complaint alleges that the State is in violation of a provision of the Civil Rights Act of 1960 in declining to hand over the unredacted information, as demanded. With the complaint, DOJ filed a motion to compel records pursuant to the Civil Rights Act of 1960. The motion advocates that the Court must dispense with normal process and the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure and instead must, in a summary fashion, simply compel production of the voter database. By agreement between the parties, OAG filed a motion to dismiss and an opposition to the motion to compel on January 30, 2026. Two intervening defendants also filed motions to dismiss and oppositions to the motion to compel on the same date. DOJ filed a consolidated response to all the defendants’ filings on February 13, 2026. The defendants may file a final reply on or before February 27, 2026. 7. Republican National Committee, et al. v. Jared DeMarinis, et al., No. 1:25- CV-03989 (D.Md.) On December 5, 2025, the national and state entities of the Republican Party and two Republican voters registered in Maryland sued the State Administrator of Elections, State Board, and local boards of elections for Montgomery and Howard County. The complaint alleges that election officials in Maryland are violating the National Voter Registration Act (“NVRA”) by failing to make reasonable efforts to remove eligible voters from the voter registration database.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557284+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1c002ae1-d281-44a0-86f3-9cd8c87217da", "source_url": "local://2026-02/2026-02-19 AAG report.pdf", "title": "2026-02-19 AAG report", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 7, "word_count": 201, "text": "The complaint alleges that election officials in Maryland are violating the National Voter Registration Act (“NVRA”) by failing to make reasonable efforts to remove eligible voters from the voter registration database. In support of that allegation, the complaint cites estimated population statistics and compares them to SBE’s reported voter registration totals. On January 12, 2026, The Democratic National Committee moved to intervene in the case as a defendant. The plaintiffs (RNC, Md. GOP, and voters) did not oppose the motion to intervene. OAG, on behalf of SBE, took no position on the motion. The motion remains pending before the Court. OAG filed a motion to dismiss the complaint on January 30, 2026. The local boards of elections, through their counsel, also filed motions to dismiss on the same date. On February 2, 2026, the Court granted the pending motion to intervene, permitting the DNC to participate in the case as a defendant. February 19, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report By agreement between the parties, the plaintiffs will file a consolidated response to the motions to dismiss on or before March 13, 2026. The defendants may then file replies on or before April 17, 2026. February 19, 2026 Assistant Attorney General’s Report", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:11.557293+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "4a14ab5a-1f72-4ca6-a4fe-c1b82d844481", "source_url": "local://2026-02/CCF Regs February 2026.pdf", "title": "CCF Regs February 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 27, "text": "(cid:0)(cid:8)(cid:9)(cid:10)(cid:9)(cid:11)(cid:0)(cid:12)(cid:13)(cid:10)(cid:14)(cid:15)(cid:0)(cid:13)(cid:16)(cid:0)(cid:11)(cid:17)(cid:11)(cid:18)(cid:9)(cid:19)(cid:13)(cid:20)(cid:8)(cid:0)\n(cid:0)(cid:2)(cid:3)(cid:4)(cid:5)(cid:6)(cid:0)(cid:7)(cid:7)(cid:0)\n(cid:0)(cid:8)(cid:21)(cid:22)(cid:23)(cid:24)(cid:25)(cid:26)(cid:0)(cid:27)(cid:27)(cid:0)(cid:15)(cid:19)(cid:8)(cid:18)(cid:17)(cid:13)(cid:8)(cid:28)(cid:14)(cid:11)(cid:0)(cid:12)(cid:29)(cid:0)(cid:30)(cid:11)(cid:14)(cid:8)(cid:13)(cid:20)(cid:0)(cid:11)(cid:31)(cid:30)(cid:17)(cid:13)(cid:29)(cid:19)(cid:20) (cid:0)(cid:10)(cid:0)(cid:17)(cid:13)(cid:12)(cid:12)(cid:29)(cid:19)(cid:8)(cid:9)(cid:0)\n(cid:0)(cid:18)!\"#(cid:24)(cid:26)$(cid:0)%&(cid:0)(cid:8)(cid:9)(cid:10)(cid:9)(cid:11)(cid:31)(cid:11)(cid:20)(cid:9)(cid:0)(cid:13)(cid:16)(cid:0)(cid:18)(cid:13)(cid:20)(cid:9)(cid:14)(cid:19)(cid:12)(cid:28)(cid:9)(cid:19)(cid:13)(cid:20)(cid:8)(cid:0)\n(cid:0)’()*+,-)./(cid:0)01234+5(cid:0)678(cid:0)’,43129(cid:0):;<=>;?@A?BAC(cid:0)D252,71(cid:0)E,+F-G-+5G(cid:0)’,43129(cid:0):H<I=JC(cid:0)’55+)7)2K(cid:0)L+K2(cid:0)+M(cid:0)N7,.175K(cid:0)\n(cid:0)OPQ(cid:0)(cid:0)RSTUVWXYWUZ[O(cid:0)\n(cid:0)\\](cid:0)(cid:0)^_(cid:0)‘a_abcd](cid:0)\\(cid:0)eabfg_(cid:0)ahedgij_k(cid:0)c(cid:0)dgllijfm(cid:0)(cid:0)nopq(cid:0)rstuvwxyposw(cid:0)(cid:0)sz(cid:0){|}}(cid:0)s~(cid:0)ts~v(cid:0)(cid:0)(cid:127)c_(cid:128)(cid:0)hc(cid:129)j_k(cid:0)c_(cid:0)\n(cid:0)ceedj(cid:130)clda(cid:0)(cid:130)g_mbjl(cid:131)mjg_(cid:132)(cid:0)f(cid:133)cdd(cid:0)(cid:134)jda(cid:0)(cid:135)jm(cid:133)(cid:0)m(cid:133)a(cid:0)(cid:136)mcma(cid:0)(cid:137)gcb(cid:128)(cid:0)_g(cid:0)dcmab(cid:0)m(cid:13