cid:19)(cid:21)(cid:7)(cid:7)(cid:19)(cid:18)(cid:18)(cid:24)(cid:7)(cid:0)(cid:30)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:24)(cid:6)(cid:11)(cid:6)(cid:30)(cid:21)(cid:24)(cid:6)(cid:18)(cid:17)(cid:29)(cid:0)(cid:5)(cid:3)(cid:12)(cid:0) (cid:0)!(cid:24)(cid:21)(cid:24)(cid:12)(cid:0)<(cid:18)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:23)(cid:0)(cid:7)(cid:3)(cid:18)(cid:22)(cid:20)(cid:23)(cid:0)(cid:19)(cid:12)(cid:13)(cid:6)(cid:7)(cid:12)(cid:0)(cid:24)(cid:3)(cid:12)(cid:7)(cid:12)(cid:0)(cid:21)(cid:25)(cid:12)(cid:17)(cid:23)(cid:25)(cid:12)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:7)(cid:0)(cid:24)(cid:18)(cid:0)(cid:27)(cid:21)(cid:13)(cid:18)(cid:19)(cid:0)(cid:21)(cid:11)(cid:11)(cid:12)(cid:7)(cid:7) (cid:0)(cid:11)(cid:20)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:6)(cid:24)(cid:4) (cid:0)(cid:21)(cid:17)(cid:23)(cid:0)(cid:22)(cid:17)(cid:6)(cid:27)(cid:18)(cid:19)(cid:25)(cid:6)(cid:24)(cid:4) (cid:0)(cid:17)(cid:18)(cid:24)(cid:0)(cid:19)(cid:12)(cid:7)(cid:24)(cid:19)(cid:6)(cid:11)(cid:24)(cid:6)(cid:18)(cid:17)(cid:29)(cid:0) (cid:0)=(cid:21)(cid:17)(cid:11)(cid:4)(cid:0)!(cid:3)(cid:6)(cid:3)(cid:0) (cid:0)1(cid:18)(cid:26)(cid:21)(cid:19)(cid:23)(cid:0)2(cid:18)(cid:22)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:4)(cid:0):(cid:12)(cid:30)(cid:22)(cid:28)(cid:20)(cid:6)(cid:11)(cid:21)(cid:17)(cid:0)2(cid:12)(cid:17)(cid:24)(cid:19)(cid:21)(cid:20)(cid:0)2(cid:18)(cid:25)(cid:25)(cid:6)(cid:24)(cid:24)(cid:12)(cid:12)(cid:0)", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:23.298721+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e5116d07-c102-4bf7-a5cf-c73ec5ec7a41", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Request to Provide Supplemenal Language Assistance 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Request to Provide Supplemenal Language Assistance 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 409, "text": "Maryland State Board of Elections\nRequest to Provide Supplemental Language Assistance\n1. LBE: Montgomery County Board of Elections\na. When did your Board approve plans and funding, if applicable, for supplemental assistance?\nOn February 2, 2026, the Montgomery County Board of Elections approved the plan and authorized the\nElection Director to seek the State Board of Elections' approval to provide language assistance, within the\ncounty's capacity and means, in other languages.\n2. Point of Contact: Boris Brajkovic, Election Director for Montgomery County Board of Elections\n3. Are you providing additional services in already required languages or providing already required\nservices in additional languages, or both?:\nService in an additional language\n▢\ni. Which language?: Montgomery County Board of Elections seeks approval from the State\nBoard of Elections to translate its Voter Guide (attached) and make it available on its\nwebsite in French and Korean languages for the 2026 Gubernatorial election cycle.\nServices beyond the scope of requirements in Maryland law\nii. What service(s)?: ________________________________________________________\n▢\n4. Describe the population you seek to serve:\nMontgomery County is recognized as the most diverse county in the state, with four of its local\njurisdictions ranking among the top ten most ethnically diverse jurisdictions in the nation. According to\ncensus data, there are 3,945 residents who speak Korean language and 3,483 residents who speak\nFrench language and have limited proficiency in English language.\n5. Describe or provide indicators of the need you seek to meet:\nThe census data shows that there are 3,945 Korean-speaking and 3,483 French-speaking citizens with\nlimited English language proficiency.\n6. What assistance is needed from the State Board, if any, to provide assistance?:\nNo assistance is required from the State Board of Elections to provide planned assistance to\nMontgomery County citizens who speak Korean and French languages and have limited English\nlanguage proficiency.\n7. What other information should the State Board consider when it reviews this application?:\nThe Montgomery County Government, through its Office of Community Partnerships, offers\ncomplimentary translation services for all county agencies. Consequently, the translation of the Voter\nGuide (attached to this request) will incur no additional cost to the Montgomery County Board of\nElections. Moreover, this planned effort will enhance our ongoing outreach activities to various linguistic\ngroups within the county.\nPlease attach examples of any written materials that are being proposed, as well as the English\ntranslation. The English version of the Montgomery County Voter Guide is provided together with this\nrequest.\nRevised 01.27.2025", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:23.362351+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a192d4ac-2b49-428a-8e17-6e8c86105c80", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 267, "text": "MONTGOMERY COUNTY BOARD OF ELECTIONS Official Board of Elections Voter Information Guide 2026 Gubernatorial Primary Election Look inside for information on:  Three Ways to Vote (pages 2-3)  Ballot Drop Boxes (pages 4-5)  Early Voting Centers (pages 6-7)  Phone Texting Commands (page 8)  Official Sample Ballot (pages 13-14) ¿Habla español? Su Papeleta de Muestra está en la página 15 Voting information is provided in Spanish as required by Section 203 of the Voting Rights Act of 1975. Sección 203 de la Ley del Derecho al Voto de 1975 requiere que la información sobre la votación esté disponible en español. Three Ways To Vote 1 2 3 • • MAIL-IN VOTING 1 • Registered voters can request a Mail-in ballot for this election or request to be placed on the Permanent list to vote by mail in every election. • Ballots will begin to be mailed 45 days before Election Day to voters who SCAN ME requested them by 60 days before Election Day, and will continue roughly in order requests are received. • To apply to receive a Mail-in ballot, voters can text VBM to 77788, visit 777vote.org, email absentee@montgomerycountymd.gov, or visit the Board of Elections at 18753 N. Frederick Avenue, Suite 210, Gaithersburg, Maryland 20879. • Sign and date the oath before returning the ballot. Mail-in ballots without signed oaths will NOT be counted. Sign the oath, not the ballot! Signed ballots will not be counted. Return your completed Mail-in ballot to any official Montgomery County election drop box (see pages 4 - 5 for locations) or return it via the U.S.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075196+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a758040c-787c-4e4c-a07a-1975ddf06830", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 299, "text": "Mail-in ballots without signed oaths will NOT be counted. Sign the oath, not the ballot! Signed ballots will not be counted. Return your completed Mail-in ballot to any official Montgomery County election drop box (see pages 4 - 5 for locations) or return it via the U.S. Postal Service. • If you are returning a voted Mail-in ballot via USPS in the last week before Election Day, to assure that your ballot is considered timely, please take it to the Post Office to be hand-postmarked by Election Day by a U.S. Postal Service Clerk. • Voters who have already requested a Mail-in ballot and decide to vote in person will be required to vote a Provisional ballot at the polls. Save time and hassle by returning your mail-in ballot. You can return it in person at any voting location. Mail-In Ballot REQUEST Deadlines • Mail-in ballot applications must be received by:  Tuesday, June 16, 2026: If you request your Mail-in ballot delivered by Mail/Fax.  Friday, June 19, 2026: If you request your print-your-own Mail-in ballot delivered via Internet. • If you request a print-your-own ballot that is delivered by Internet, you must print your ballot and provide your own return envelope and postage (or return your ballot to a drop box or in person). Once received at the Board of Elections, these ballots are hand- duplicated by bipartisan teams onto official ballots for scanning.  Tuesday, June 23, 2026 (by 8PM): If you pick up your Mail-in ballot in person at the Board of Elections office in Gaithersburg. Mail-In Ballot RETURN Deadlines Mail-in ballots must be returned to the Board of Elections in one of these ways: • Mailed and postmarked by Tuesday, June 23, 2026, if returned by U.S. mail (Post offices generally close by 5PM).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075215+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d4a5d015-c4cf-492b-af9a-e3bea98c6668", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 265, "text": "Mail-In Ballot RETURN Deadlines Mail-in ballots must be returned to the Board of Elections in one of these ways: • Mailed and postmarked by Tuesday, June 23, 2026, if returned by U.S. mail (Post offices generally close by 5PM). Due to U.S. Postal Service changes, we urge you to mail your ballot as early as possible to assure a timely postmark and timely delivery. • Tuesday, June 23, 2026 (by 8PM) if deposited in one of the 58 official Montgomery County drop boxes. See pages 4 - 5 for Drop Box locations. • For the location and availability of a nearby drop box and/or post office, text BOX + your location's Zip Code to 77788 (Example: BOX 20879). • Tuesday, June 23, 2026 (by 8PM), if returned in person at the Board of Elections office in Gaithersburg or at your Election Day polling place. 2 Three Ways To Vote 1 2 3 • • 2026 EARLY VOTING CENTERS 2 Thursday, June 11 - Thursday, June 18 (7AM - 8PM) Registered voters and those eligible for same-day registration may vote at any one of Montgomery County's 14 early voting centers (see pages 6 - 7 for early voting center locations). • Same-day registration will be available if you bring a current Maryland driver's or learner's permit, Maryland identification card, or other proof of SCAN ME residence (Example: change of address card, paycheck, bank statement, utility bill, or other government document that includes your name and address to prove where you live).To avoid delays, vote on Saturday or Sunday at any of the 14 early voting locations.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075225+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2e6384fb-40ee-4e9c-b881-76e773dd58eb", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 239, "text": "The longest wait times are usually on the last day of early voting. • For nearby early voting locations, driving directions, and wait times, text EV + Zip Code to 77788 (Example: EV 20879). • Wait times at each early voting center are posted on the Board of Elections website at 777vote.org. 3 2026 PRIMARY ELECTION DAY Tuesday, June 23, 2026 (7AM - 8PM) SCAN ME • Avoid delays! Vote only at your assigned polling place or else you’ll need to vote by provisional ballot. • Same-day registration will be available if you bring a current Maryland driver's license or learner's permit, Maryland identification card, or other proof of residence (for example: change of address card, paycheck, bank statement, utility bill, or other government document that includes your name and address to prove where you live). • To obtain your polling place information, text CHECK to 77788. To vote in the Democratic or Republican Party primaries, you must be registered with that party. However, all voters, including Unaffiliated voters and those registered with other parties, may vote in the primary election for Board of Education. Voters can update their party affiliation by the voter registration deadline of Tuesday, June 2, 2026. You cannot change your political party affiliation during early voting or on Election Day. To update your voter registration, text UPDATE to 77788 or visit 777vote.org. 3 Bring your Mail-In ballot to a drop box . . .", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075234+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "dcaf9a21-0852-4298-82f0-1d402bdb26ce", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 290, "text": "Voters can update their party affiliation by the voter registration deadline of Tuesday, June 2, 2026. You cannot change your political party affiliation during early voting or on Election Day. To update your voter registration, text UPDATE to 77788 or visit 777vote.org. 3 Bring your Mail-In ballot to a drop box . . . Drop Box Locations Activity Center at Bohrer Park East County Community 506 South Frederick Avenue Recreation Center Gaithersburg, MD 20877 3310 Gateshead Manor Way Silver Spring, MD 20904 Asbury Methodist Village* Rosborough Center Albert Einstein High School 409 Russell Avenue 11135 Newport Mill Road Gaithersburg, MD 20877 Kensington, MD 20895 Bauer Drive Community Rec Center Isiah “Ike” Leggett 14625 Bauer Drive Executive Office Building Rockville, MD 20853 101 Monroe Street Rockville, MD 20850 Bethesda-Chevy Chase High School Longwood Community 4301 East-West Highway Forest Knolls Elementary School Recreation Center Bethesda, MD 20814 10830 Eastwood Avenue 19300 Georgia Avenue Silver Spring, MD 20901 Brookeville, MD 20833 Montgomery Blair High School (Due to construction, the 51 University Boulevard East Northwood High School drop box Col. Zadok Magruder High School Silver Spring, MD 20901 was relocated to Forest Knolls 5939 Muncaster Mill Road Elementary School.) Rockville, MD 20855 James Hubert Blake High School 300 Norwood Road Friendship Heights Village Center Mid-County Community Silver Spring, MD 20905 4433 South Park Avenue Recreation Center Chevy Chase, MD 20815 2004 Queensguard Road Winston Churchill High School Silver Spring, MD 20906 11300 Gainsborough Road Gaithersburg High School Potomac, MD 20854 101 Education Boulevard Montgomery College - Germantown Gaithersburg, MD 20877 Bioscience Education Center Clarksburg High School 20200 Observation Drive Germantown Community 22500 Wims Road Germantown, MD 20876 Recreation Center Clarksburg, MD 20871 18905 Kingsview Road Montgomery College - Rockville Germantown, MD 20874 Gwendolyn E.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075245+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b04c7388-79dd-4038-9b9f-d9d5f34c561d", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 49, "text": "Coffield Long Nguyen and Kimmy Duong Community Recreation Center Student Services Center John F. Kennedy High School 2450 Lyttonsville Road 51 Mannakee Street 1901 Randolph Road Silver Spring, MD 20910 Rockville, MD 20850 Silver Spring, MD 20902 Nancy H. Dacek N. Potomac Montgomery College - Takoma Park Jane E.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075249+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7f52bc11-1b22-4d0c-bafd-4fafaa8e41a6", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 313, "text": "Coffield Long Nguyen and Kimmy Duong Community Recreation Center Student Services Center John F. Kennedy High School 2450 Lyttonsville Road 51 Mannakee Street 1901 Randolph Road Silver Spring, MD 20910 Rockville, MD 20850 Silver Spring, MD 20902 Nancy H. Dacek N. Potomac Montgomery College - Takoma Park Jane E. Lawton Community Community Recreation Center Charlene Nunley Recreation Center 13850 Travilah Road Student Services Center 4301 Willow Lane Rockville, MD 20850 7625 Fenton Street Chevy Chase, MD 20815 Takoma Park, MD 20912 Damascus Community Rec Center Leisure World* 25520 Oak Drive Montgomery County Clubhouse I Damascus, MD 20872 Board of Elections 3700 Rossmoor Boulevard 18753 North Frederick Avenue Silver Spring, MD 20906 Damascus High School Gaithersburg, MD 20879 25921 Ridge Road (Drive-up Box) Damascus, MD 20872 * Located in a limited access community. 4 Available until Election Day, June 23, 2026, at 8PM Drop Box Locations Montgomery County Robertson Park Youth Center Bette Carol Thompson Scotland Conference Center 801 Rabbitt Road Neighborhood Recreation Center Marriott Bethesda North Gaithersburg, MD 20878 7700 Scotland Drive 5939 Executive Boulevard Potomac, MD 20854 North Bethesda, MD 20852 Rockville City Hall 111 Maryland Avenue Upper County Community Northwest High School Rockville, MD 20850 Recreation Center 13501 Richter Farm Road (Drive-up Box) 8201 Emory Grove Road Germantown, MD 20874 Gaithersburg, MD 20877 Rockville High School Paint Branch High School 2100 Baltimore Road Walt Whitman High School 14121 Old Columbia Pike Rockville, MD 20851 7100 Whittier Boulevard Burtonsville, MD 20866 Bethesda, MD 20817 Sandy Spring Volunteer Poolesville High School Fire Department Walter Johnson High School 17501 West Willard Road 17921 Brooke Road 6400 Rock Spring Drive Poolesville, MD 20837 Sandy Spring, MD 20860 Bethesda, MD 20814 Potomac Community Rec Center Margaret Schweinhaut Senior Ctr Watkins Mill High School 11315 Falls Road 1000 Forest Glen Road 10301 Apple Ridge Road Potomac, MD 20854 Silver Spring, MD 20901 Gaithersburg, MD 20879 Marilyn J.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075259+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "e7a23ad7-443f-4a39-ba87-c1a3567669aa", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 298, "text": "Praisner Community Seneca Valley High School Wheaton High School Recreation Center 19401 Crystal Rock Drive 12401 Dalewood Drive 14906 Old Columbia Pike Germantown, MD 20874 Silver Spring, MD 20906 Burtonsville, MD 20866 Sherwood High School Wheaton Library and Quince Orchard High School 300 Olney-Sandy Spring Road Community Recreation Center 15800 Quince Orchard Road Sandy Spring, MD 20860 11701 Georgia Avenue Gaithersburg, MD 20878 Wheaton, MD 20902 Silver Spring Civic Building Richard Montgomery High School 1 Veterans Place White Oak Community Rec Center 250 Richard Montgomery Drive Silver Spring, MD 20910 1700 April Lane Rockville, MD 20852 Silver Spring, MD 20904 Springbrook High School Riderwood Village* 201 Valley Brook Drive Wilson Wims Elementary School Montgomery Station Silver Spring, MD 20904 12520 Blue Sky Drive Clubhouse Clarksburg, MD 20871 3120 Gracefield Road Takoma Park Library Silver Spring 20904 101 Philadelphia Avenue Thomas S. Wootton High School Takoma Park, MD 20912 2100 Wootton Parkway Rockville, MD 20850 * Located in a limited access community. For the location and availability of a nearby drop box, and/or post office locator, text BOX + ZIP CODE to 77788 (example: BOX 20879) or visit 777VOTE.ORG 5 Early Voting Centers During the early voting period, you may vote in person at any one of Montgomery County's 14 early voting centers. Each site also will have a drop box for receiving Mail-in ballots. The shortest lines are usually during the weekend. The longest lines are usually at the start of the first day and the last three hours on the last day. Before you are given a ballot, an Election Judge will ask you, \"Would you prefer to mark your ballot using the ballot marking device, or by hand using a pen?\" Either way, you’ll have a paper ballot with your choices to insert into the scanner.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075269+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6f5021d5-2798-48e8-964e-5bb6c5b339a3", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 278, "text": "Before you are given a ballot, an Election Judge will ask you, \"Would you prefer to mark your ballot using the ballot marking device, or by hand using a pen?\" Either way, you’ll have a paper ballot with your choices to insert into the scanner. Activity Center at Bohrer Park 1 506 S. Frederick Avenue, Gaithersburg, MD 20877 PARKING: Free on site Bauer Drive Community Recreation Center 2 14625 Bauer Drive, Rockville, MD 20853 PARKING: Free on site Damascus Community Recreation Center 3 25520 Oak Drive, Damascus, MD 20872 PARKING: Free on site Germantown Community Recreation Center 4 18905 Kingsview Road, Germantown, MD 20874 PARKING: Free on site Jane E. Lawton Community Recreation Center 5 4301 Willow Lane, Chevy Chase, MD 20815 PARKING: Free on site; street parking restrictions suspended on Willow, Elm, and 44th Streets from 10 am to 6 pm Marilyn J. Praisner Community Recreation Center 6 14906 Old Columbia Pike, Burtonsville, MD 20866 PARKING: Free on site Mid-County Community Recreation Center 7 2004 Queensguard Road, Silver Spring, MD 20906 PARKING: Free on site Isiah “Ike” Leggett Montgomery County 8 Executive Office Building RideOn 101 Monroe Street, Rockville, MD 20850 Trip Planner PARKING: One hour free at East Jefferson & Monroe Street garage with an \"I Voted/Yo Voté\" sticker; metered street parking available. 6 Thursday, June 11 - Thursday, June 18 7AM - 8PM To vote in Montgomery County in the primary election, you must be a U.S. citizen, a Montgomery County resident, register to vote by Tuesday, June 2, 2026, or in person during early voting or on Election Day, and be 18 years of age by General Election Day on Tuesday, November 3, 2026.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075279+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "779b7a71-8ebe-4348-beed-ac3447a0f23b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 222, "text": "Same-day registration is available during both early voting and on Election Day if you bring a Maryland driver's license, or learner's permit, Maryland identification card, or other proof of residence (for example: change of address card, paycheck, bank statement, utility bill, or other government document that includes your name and address to prove where you live). If you moved, you can update your address during early voting. An election official will help you change your address and give you the ballot for your new address. Nancy H. Dacek North Potomac Community 9 Recreation Center 13850 Travilah Road, Rockville, MD 20850 PARKING: Free on site Potomac Community Recreation Center 10 11315 Falls Road, Potomac, MD 20854 PARKING: Free on site Sandy Spring Volunteer Fire Department Station 4 11 17921 Brooke Road, Sandy Spring, MD 20860 PARKING: Free on site Silver Spring Civic Building 12 1 Veterans Place, Silver Spring, MD 20910 PARKING: Public garage or metered street parking (Free on Sundays) Wheaton Community Recreation Center 13 11701 Georgia Avenue, Wheaton, MD 20902 PARKING: Free on site White Oak Community Recreation Center 14 1700 April Lane, Silver Spring, MD 20904 PARKING: Free on site For nearby early voting locations, driving directions, and wait times, text EV + Zip Code to 77788 (Example: EV 20879) or visit 777VOTE.ORG. 7 Get What You Need To Vote!", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075286+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "542af358-f813-4ebc-b9c4-8e724e728282", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 290, "text": "Phone Texting Commands VOTE HELP Text to 77788 Text to 77788  Register To Vote  Additional information CHECK Text to 77788  Review your voter registration  Find out where to vote  Find out your voting districts  See the status of your mail-in or provisional ballot UPDATE Text to 77788  Update your voter registration VBM Text to 77788  Apply To Vote By Mail BOX Zip Code Text + to 77788 Example: BOX 20879  Ballot Drop Box and Post Office Locator EV Zip Code Text + to 77788 Example: EV 20879  Three Nearby Early Voting Centers  Wait Times SERVE to 77788 Text  Serve as an Election Worker 8 Choose How To Vote In Person 9 Election Security Our Voting Equipment Is Not Connected to the Internet Neither the Ballot Marking Device nor the digital scanner that counts your ballot is ever connected to the Internet. Each scanner has been certified by the U.S. Election Assistance Commission. The Montgomery County Board of Elections tests every scanner to ensure that it counts accurately before sending it to the polls. The Montgomery County Board of Elections follows strict chain of custody procedures and audit results after Election Day to make sure the scanners were not tampered with. Backup Systems Are in Place to Protect Election Results There are several backup systems in place to protect the validity of election results. First, results are printed on paper at each polling place. Second, a digital scan of each ballot is stored on a removable drive. Third, the paper ballots themselves can be used in an audit or recount, as they were during the 2018 and 2022 primary election recounts. There are also backup systems for voter registration.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075296+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "216b4221-37d6-4a1c-9cca-c1dd3b8c7eb9", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 271, "text": "First, results are printed on paper at each polling place. Second, a digital scan of each ballot is stored on a removable drive. Third, the paper ballots themselves can be used in an audit or recount, as they were during the 2018 and 2022 primary election recounts. There are also backup systems for voter registration. Notices are mailed to voters for every change, and each polling place has a paper list of voters. Anyone who disputes his or her voter registration can cast a provisional ballot, and election officials will determine after Election Day whether the ballot was validly cast and will be counted. We Are Looking Out for Your Safety A dedicated bipartisan team of professionals and volunteers preserve the chain of custody of your ballot and the voting data. All employees receive background checks and thousands of election workers of different party affiliations work together to ensure election integrity. The safety and security of election workers and voters is our priority, and we continuously work and coordinate with the Montgomery County Police Department and other relevant agencies to provide a safe voting experience. Election Results Are Not Confirmed Immediately, and Mail-In Ballots and Provisional Ballots Continue to Be Counted AFTER Election Day The results reported and any races called by the media on election night are not final. The election will not be certified for at least two weeks after Election Day, until all Mail-in and provisional ballots are counted. This allows time to audit the results, investigate complaints, and make sure every vote counts. If you return your Mail-in ballot early, it will be processed before Election Day.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075305+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7e56b390-dfde-4a0c-a1f9-b107ab509d95", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 280, "text": "The election will not be certified for at least two weeks after Election Day, until all Mail-in and provisional ballots are counted. This allows time to audit the results, investigate complaints, and make sure every vote counts. If you return your Mail-in ballot early, it will be processed before Election Day. Election officials will start processing received ballots on Monday, June 1, but no results are tabulated before the polls open at 7AM on Election Day, Tuesday June 23, nor released until the polls close that day at 8PM. Montgomery County may take longer to announce final results because there are more voters, usually more Mail-in ballots, and more print-your-own Internet-delivered ballots (that must be hand-duplicated) than in other Maryland counties. All validly cast ballots will be counted, whether or not they will change the results. VOTING ACCESSIBILITY Voters With Disabilities Maryland’s Voting System offers features for accessibility. Please ask the Election Judge about your options. At least one touchscreen ballot marking device will be available at each site with large-print, high contrast, Braille, and audio capabilities. Each ballot marking device has a keypad that allows voters with visual impairments to navigate the ballot and make their choices. Language Access As required by Section 203 of the Voting Rights Act of 1975, the ballot marking device offers both visual and audio instructions in either English or Spanish as selected by the voter. Interpreters are available to assist voters who speak Spanish while additional language assistance options are available to speakers of other languages determined by the State Board of Elections through the new Language Assistance Program. The Board of Elections is committed to serving all County voters, including limited-English speakers.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075316+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "02ecc935-3ef9-4601-a7e0-1ab976498a04", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 279, "text": "Interpreters are available to assist voters who speak Spanish while additional language assistance options are available to speakers of other languages determined by the State Board of Elections through the new Language Assistance Program. The Board of Elections is committed to serving all County voters, including limited-English speakers. Poll workers are trained and have access to various language resources to assist with communication in Amharic, Chinese, French, Korean, Spanish, Vietnamese, and other languages. 10 ELECTION WORKERS NEEDED! An election judge is a registered Maryland voter who volunteers to assist voters during early voting and on Election Day. The role of an election judge is to ensure fair and accessible elections for all voters and to protect the integrity of the election process. • Due to the diversity of languages spoken within the County, bilingual election judges are needed. • Election Judges who are fluent in both English and Spanish are especially needed in each polling place to meet the requirements of Section 203 of the 1975 Voting Rights Act and the Language Assistance Program established by Title 15.5 of the Maryland Election Law Article. • Election workers will earn a minimum $250 stipend per full day. If you served at a polling place during the 2024 elections, you also will receive a $100 bonus per full day served ($50 for half-day shifts) during early voting and on Election Day. To apply, text SERVE to 77788, or visit the Board of Elections website, 777vote.org. HIGH SCHOOL ELECTION We WORKER PROGRAM need you! (16 & OLDER) The Montgomery County Board of Elections Future Vote Student Election Worker Program provides high school students with an exciting opportunity to serve as election workers.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075326+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "261a8089-e7fa-46e4-8522-3ba29084eedc", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 14, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 299, "text": "To apply, text SERVE to 77788, or visit the Board of Elections website, 777vote.org. HIGH SCHOOL ELECTION We WORKER PROGRAM need you! (16 & OLDER) The Montgomery County Board of Elections Future Vote Student Election Worker Program provides high school students with an exciting opportunity to serve as election workers. Volunteers may choose to earn up to 25 Student Service Learning (SSL) hours or a minimum $250 stipend per day of service along with a great hands-on introduction to the democratic process. REQUIREMENTS: Interested students must meet the following requirements to participate as a student election worker: • Must be at least 16 years of age by the time of registration • U.S. Citizen • Registered to vote in Maryland • Must have parental consent for workers under 18 years old For additional information: visit 777vote.org, then select “Election Workers”, then select “High School Poll Workers”. To apply, text SERVE to 77788, or visit the Board of Elections website, 777vote.org. FUTURE VOTE AMBASSADORS! Students, grades 6-12, are needed to serve as student aides (election pages) at polling places. Serving gives students their first exposure to the actual logistics of the democratic process in the United States. Students leave the program with experience in how elections are set up and a greater understanding of and trust in American democracy. Student election aides are not offered any monetary compensation, but they are given Student Service Learning (SSL) hours for every hour they work. To apply, visit 777vote.org, then select “Future Vote”, then select “How to Sign-up\", (Deadline: May 26, 2026) or text FV to 77788. 11 Key dates, Election Officials, and contacts Key dates for the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary Election Update your voter registration information regarding your party affiliation, residential / mailing address, or your name by Tuesday, June 2, 2026.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075337+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5b06f83b-5f64-436b-bf52-ad0d5e0364a2", "source_url": "local://2026-02/MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026.pdf", "title": "MoCo Voter Guide for the 2026 Primary Election 02062026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 15, "chunk_total": 16, "word_count": 274, "text": "To apply, visit 777vote.org, then select “Future Vote”, then select “How to Sign-up\", (Deadline: May 26, 2026) or text FV to 77788. 11 Key dates, Election Officials, and contacts Key dates for the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary Election Update your voter registration information regarding your party affiliation, residential / mailing address, or your name by Tuesday, June 2, 2026. Request a Mail-in ballot at any time but no later than Tuesday, June 16, 2026; request print- your-own emailed ballot by Friday, June 19, 2026, or come to our office to receive a Mail-in ballot in-person by Tuesday, June 23, 2026, at 8pm. Return your voted Mail-in ballot by 8pm on Tuesday, June 23, 2026, to any drop box (see pages 4-5 for locations) or have it postmarked and mailed via USPS before 8pm on Tuesday, June 23, 2026. Early Voting: Thursday, June 11, 2026, to Thursday, June 18, 2026, from 7am to 8pm at any of the 14 Early Voting Centers (please see pages 6 and 7 for locations). Election Day: Tuesday, June 23, 2026, from 7am to 8pm at your assigned polling place Montgomery County Board of Elections Board Members 18753 N. Frederick Avenue, Suite 210 David A. Naimon, President Gaithersburg, Maryland 20879 Daniel A. Koroma, Vice President Hours: 8:30AM - 5PM Amoretta M. (Amie) Hoeber, Secretary 240-777-VOTE (8683) Keyna I. Anyiam, Member 777vote.org Alexander C. Vincent, Member Margie C. Delao, Substitute Member mocovotesmd.bsky.social Lawrence L. Halloran, Substitute Member facebook.com/777vote instagram.com/777vote Election Director x.com/777vote Boris Brajkovic Maryland State Board of Elections 1-800-222-VOTE (8683) elections.maryland.gov info.sbe@maryland.gov facebook.com/MarylandStateBoardofElections x.com/md_sbe instagram.com/md_sbe This Sample Ballot is available in PDF or large type. Email elections@montgomerycountymd.gov or call 240-777-8500 12", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.075347+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9e0d4370-af0f-4ec3-9662-28d94b331bb3", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Policy 2025-03 Individual Voting Record  (PIA) and Voter Registration List (PSR) Request Policy .pdf", "title": "Policy 2025-03 Individual Voting Record  (PIA) and Voter Registration List (PSR) Request Policy ", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 245, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman\nState Administrator\nJim Shalleck, Vice Chairman\nDiane Butler\nKatherine Berry\nDeputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley\nEric Bryant\nPolicy 2025-03 Individual Voting Record (PIA) and Voter Registration List (PSR)\nRequest Policy\nIt is the policy of the State Board of Elections to administer and maintain voter registration\nrecords accurately and transparently. In doing so, the State Board must balance the requirements\nfor public disclosure of registration records imposed by laws like Election Law § 3-505 against\nvoters’ privacy interest in the personally identifying information they provide for registration.\nPIA\nThe State Board of Elections provides the following PIA policy for disclosure of voter\nregistration applications pursuant to public information requests.\nThe following information should be redacted from disclosure before any registration\napplication is released publicly:\n1. Maryland Driver’s License Number\n2. Social Security Number\n3. Personal Phone Number\n4. Personal Email Address\n5. Agency through which the Voter Registered or Updated their Registration\nAll other information contained in the voter registration application may be disclosed without\nredaction. Individual records requests will be charged as a countywide request after 2 hours of data\ngathering has occurred for a requester that sends multiple requests (see PSR below).\nPSR\nAll PSR requests that are countywide or by district requests will be charged $75 and statewide\ndata requests will be charged $125.\nFAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200\nMD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:24.117422+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f83e4920-3c66-404f-a816-7756270ecbe4", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 285, "text": "2/13/26, 12:13 PM State of Maryland Mail - Fwd: Do Not Pass the proposed deadlines for Poll Watchers to register ba | Maryland Fwd: Do Not Pass the proposed deadlines for Poll Watchers to register 1 message , MARYLAND STATE BOARD OF ELECTIONS ---------- Forwarded message --------- From: Mary Nelson Date: Mon, Feb 2, 2 at 4: Subject: Do Not Pass the proposed deadlines for Poll Watchers to register To: <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce: Changing a deadline for registering unduly handicaps the poll watchers and challengers from both parties. It is hard enough to get people committed day by day. To insist that everything be lined up prior to Day 1 of Early Voting poses an undue hardship on candidates, parties and Central Committees and really serves no purpose. As a Chief Judge, | have no problem working with the current regulations and would prefer not to add one more problem when people appear with the paperwork and get turned away. You don't want to disenfranchise voters and these poll watchers and challengers are part of the election process and are working within the system in an orderly fashion. Please leave the regs as they stand. Thank You, Mary Nelson Chief Judge, Cecil County https://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/7ik=badd2b88 12&view=pt&search=all&permthid=thread-f: 185 7030803488626353&simpl=msg-f- 185 7030803488626353 = 1/1 2/13/26, 4:27 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035783516/thread/8666990000357835207?action.... Message ID <CAOLH-LEeDRmBN-VovMi4bS-YG4R7-y6RNhYcfs8BHd87JkSUeg@mail.gmail.com> From \"Mary Nelson iy To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 04:14 PM(Incoming) Subject Do Not Pass the proposed deadlines for Poll Watchers to register Thread Subject Do Not Pass the proposed deadlines for Poll Watchers to register Return-Path TY Changing a deadline for registering unduly handicaps the poll watchers and challengers from both parties. It is hard enough to get people committed day by day.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202704+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "cf1b8bff-cab2-4068-95ae-d5bab962ab9e", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 259, "text": "It is hard enough to get people committed day by day. To insist that everything be lined up prior to Day 1 of Early Voting poses an undue hardship on candidates, parties and Central Committees and really serves no purpose. As a Chief Judge, | have no problem working with the current regulations and would prefer not to add one more problem when people appear with the paperwork and get turned away. You don't want to disenfranchise voters and these poll watchers and challengers are part of the election process and are working within the system in an orderly fashion. Please leave the regs as they stand. Thank You, Mary Nelson Chief Judge, Cecil County https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000357 835 16/thread/8666990000357835207?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:28 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782260/thread/866699000035 782264 ?action. .. Message ID <543632960.876490.1770066309535@connect.xfinity.com> From \"RONALD Calabrese To \"mailto:info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 04:05 PM(Incoming) Subject Election Watcher Thread Subject Election Watcher Return-Path Subject:Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectfully submitted, Ronald J. Calabrese Baltimore County Central Committee https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782260/thread/866699000035782264?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:29 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003578 1340/thread/866699000035781344?action....", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202729+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6f27b87a-6661-4e36-8d75-2cefe9dcb1be", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 296, "text": "This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectfully submitted, Ronald J. Calabrese Baltimore County Central Committee https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782260/thread/866699000035782264?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:29 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003578 1340/thread/866699000035781344?action.... Message ID <CALOXsH3FRysFUF6uHXJGegZHV8WSoTgk+eoFnykUL-KKcpbSYg@mail.gmail.com> From \"Pamela Ciliberti_ To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 03:55 PM (Incoming) Subject Opposition to SBE CHANGES to Challenger and Watcher regulations Thread Subject Opposition to SBE CHANGES to Challenger and Watcher regulations Return-Path | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectfully submitted, Pamela Ciliberti Chair Frederick County Republican Central Committee https://kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035781340/thread/866699000035781344?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:32 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035785160/thread/866699000035785164 ?action. .. Message ID <ul5wmvAQSVxC6XkOJRR3zRusdD OrOMOOaaxpgZYqkawnPpocZluc7aeN1Xr493R- _It7_FF72tPeppUVaOE_zrHUty7DztYzYoVDrRi-izw=@proton.me> From \"Ray Smith To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 03:49 PM (Incoming) Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Thread Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Return-Path | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202745+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "dc27dbec-15e8-4e60-9ae2-890da405fe5e", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 70, "text": "The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Thank you for your attention to this matter.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202753+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7c68c376-efcb-4cfa-9b53-840dd9671a25", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 606, "text": "Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Thank you for your attention to this matter. Sincerely, Raymond Smith Baltimore County Republican Central Committee Election Law § 10-311 (a) (1) The following persons or entities have the right to designate a registered voter as a challenger or a watcher at each place of registration and election: (i) the State Board for any polling place in the State; (ii) a local board for any polling place located in the county of the local board; (iii) a candidate; (iv) a political party; and (v) any other group of voters supporting or opposing a candidate, principle, or proposition on the ballot. (2) A person who appoints a challenger or watcher may remove the challenger or watcher at any time. (b) Except as provided in § 10-303(d)(2) of this subtitle and subsection (d) of this section, a challenger or watcher has the right to: (1) enter the polling place one-half hour before the polls open; (2) enter or be present at the polling place at any time when the polls are open; (3) remain in the polling place until the completion of all tasks associated with the close of the polls under § 10-314 of this subtitle and the election judges leave the polling place; (4) maintain a list of registered voters who have voted, or individuals who have cast provisional ballots, and take the list outside of the polling place; and (5) enter and leave a polling place for the purpose of taking outside of the polling place information that identifies registered voters who have cast ballots or individuals who have cast provisional ballots. https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035785160/thread/866699000035785164?action=print 1/2 2/13/26, 4:32 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035785160/thread/866699000035785164 ?action. .. c) (1) (i (ii) The State Board shall prescribe a form that shall be supplied to the challenger or watcher by the person or entity designating the challenger or watcher. (2) A challenger or watcher shall be positioned near the election judges and inside the voting room so that the challenger or watcher may see and hear each person as the person offers to vote. (d) (1) A challenger or watcher may not attempt to: (i) ascertain how a voter voted or intends to vote; (ii) converse in the polling place with any voter; (iii) assist any voter in voting; or (iv) physically handle an original election document. (2) An election judge may eject a challenger or watcher who violates the prohibitions under paragraph (1) of this subsection. (e) (1) Except as provided in paragraphs (2) and (3) of this subsection, an election judge shall permit an individual other than an accredited challenger or watcher who desires to challenge the right to vote of any other individual to enter the polling place for that purpose. (2) A majority of the election judges may limit the number of nonaccredited challengers and watchers allowed in the polling place at any one time for the purpose of challenging the right of an individual to vote. (3) A nonaccredited challenger or watcher shall leave the polling place as soon as a majority of the election judges decides the right to vote of the individual challenged by the challenger or watcher. (4) In addition to restrictions provided under this subsection, all restrictions on the actions of an accredited challenger or watcher provided under this subtitle apply to a nonaccredited challenger or watcher. https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035785160/thread/866699000035785164?action=print 2/13/26, 4:34 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782092/thread/866699000035782096?action. ..", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202776+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "76c658e7-cd06-4a36-8891-fde900f91d99", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 267, "text": "Message ID <CAAJubE4zu33Wn 3nrp8=Ac2 X9F pdF bHqd GU-ZF5NH5pO0OF+_WcTg@mail.gmail.com> From Jean 2 aT To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce pe a Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 03:05 PM (Incoming) Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Thread Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Return-Path Pt Subject: Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process All SBE, I serve as an Election Judge/Provisional Judge each election and I strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectfully submitted, Jean Benhoff Baltimore County, MD https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782092/thread/8666990000357820967?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:36 PM kb.vote.md_.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035 7 79952/thread/8666990000357799567?action. .. Message ID <rnQhiL_rZY1C-2TlInC5fpbhaVGtFfGebP8qszcBlxeRNWZ_A-xkUfpiq_N1jlqjm8tvp4- AK8rslqvbtqxHhc4L9hj8kMOj6TbG5SLYmwmU=@protonmail.com> From \"Kate Taylo To \"info.sbe@maryland.gov\"<info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 06:23 PM (Incoming) Subject Maintain current poll watcher process Thread Subject Maintain current poll watcher process Return-Path es To whom it may concern; | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202789+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5b695a53-361d-45f2-86f4-70826f9cd23b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 274, "text": "The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline Sincerely, Kate Taylor cell/text \"| do my best proofreading after | hit send.\" Sent from Proton Mailfor iOS. https://kb.vote.md_gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000357 79952/thread/8666990000357799567?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:37 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035787 11 7/thread/866699000035787 121 ?action... Message ID <2F4142AE-DF65-4402-BF4F-249COBOCABE2@gmail.com> From Janice Grim iii To <info.sbe@maryland. 9° Pe Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 07:18 PM (Incoming) Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Thread Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Return-Path FP | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. Observation is an important part of a free and fair democratic process. We should have MORE transparency, not less! You allow same day voter registration, but require poll watchers a limited window? For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. It is truly unconscionable. Respectfully submitted, Janice Grim Baltimore County Central Committee Sent from my iPhone https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003578711 7/thread/866699000035 787121 ?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:38 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782865/thread/8666990000357828697?action. ..", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202803+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "58e0df21-6ae3-4da3-91ca-dd7739f9b3a3", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 300, "text": "We should have MORE transparency, not less! You allow same day voter registration, but require poll watchers a limited window? For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. It is truly unconscionable. Respectfully submitted, Janice Grim Baltimore County Central Committee Sent from my iPhone https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003578711 7/thread/866699000035 787121 ?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:38 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782865/thread/8666990000357828697?action. .. Message ID <CADQkgMdCAfSjMQx+8d YsY=Gdg+VfHt+tpbd+mDgwtOQ QbT=rj38 A@mail.gmail.com> From \"Neil Posner iy To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 07:00 PM (Incoming) Subject Request to Maintain Existing Process for Poll Watchers and Challengers Thread Subject Request to Maintain Existing Process for Poll Watchers and Challengers Return-Path Pe Hello, lam writing to request the State Board of Elections maintain the current process for Election Challengers and Watchers and refrain from passing the recently proposed COMAR amendments to these rules. The amendments being discussed would be detrimental to the election process by unduly restricting the rights of designated entities to appoint Poll Watchers/Challengers and by causing significant confusion and discord on Election Day. Maryland Election Law states that candidates, parties, and other specified entities have \"the right to designate a registered voter as a challenger or a watcher at each place of registration and election....\" Further, the law states that \"[a¢rtificate signed by any party or candidate shall be sufficient evidence of the right of a challenger or watcher to be present in the voting room.\" Simply, designated entities have the right to select their volunteers, and the only validation required is a certificate they sign. To impose additional requirements on this process - namely, requirements to submit pre-selected names and locations to the local Boards of Elections by a set deadline - would be a significant infringement, and seemingly a direct violation, of the Law and of the various entities’ rights recognized under said law.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202817+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c60eb385-ca16-4fd9-8176-240e58671ef6", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 286, "text": "To impose additional requirements on this process - namely, requirements to submit pre-selected names and locations to the local Boards of Elections by a set deadline - would be a significant infringement, and seemingly a direct violation, of the Law and of the various entities’ rights recognized under said law. In addition to the lack of justification for these rule changes being considered, making the changes at this point in the middle of an election season would cause significant confusion during the election. Many candidates, party organizations, etc., are accustomed to the current rules. It is easy to forsee how many of them could be unaware of the change, attempt to designate watchers past the proposed deadline, and then find their organizations denied access at the polls, past the point by which this could be remedied, leading to significant arguments at the polls and a very messy election. For these and other reasons, I strongly ask that the Board oppose the changes to the procedures being discussed, in the name of the rights of all involved and a smooth, fair election. Sincerely, Neil Posner Baltimore County Republican Central Committee https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782865/thread/866699000035782869?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:40 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782700/thread/866699000035782704?action.... Message ID <CALHvPPPFJajkh29SmDKX9RQ8hU8tdpRoCTrnTJdDkn3V-r+c+A@mail.gmail.com> From “Barry Chodak qn To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce a a Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 06:16 PM (Incoming) Subject Change for Challengers and Watchers Thread Subject Change for Challengers and Watchers Return-Path Pe I've been a Poll Watcher and an Election Judge. The proposed change is wholly unnecessary. Elections are for citizens to participate, and this change is just another way to discourage participation. When | visited various polling places in Baltimore County, | was welcomed by the chief judges, and the process was both simple and routine.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202830+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6c2c8c87-3220-4288-86ad-503ea08efe57", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 294, "text": "The proposed change is wholly unnecessary. Elections are for citizens to participate, and this change is just another way to discourage participation. When | visited various polling places in Baltimore County, | was welcomed by the chief judges, and the process was both simple and routine. As a judge, | was inspired by the people who were willing to take time out of their day to visit and observe the process. If you add the proposed requirement, you will be adding an additional burden on the chief judges and creating an upset with those concerned citizens who jump in at the last minute to witness and be a part of our election process. | urge you to leave things as they are and withdraw the proposed change. Sincerely, Barry Chodak Baltimore County Resident and Concerned Citizen https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782700/thread/866699000035782704?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:41 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782536/thread/8666990000357825407?action.... Message ID <CACWKPG9mb_aASMFEYByUU7YFYOimAP9=0J-xFjzbRsxghE8HbQ@mail.gmail.com> From Ciciogvep | To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Cc Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 06:04 PM (Incoming) Subject Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Thread Subject Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Return-Path ee Members of the State Board of Elections, | am writing to urge the State Board of Elections to maintain the existing Challenger and Watcher process. The current form from a designated entity is adequate and should remain in place. Similarly, the existing deadline works well. As such, | am requesting, do not impose a new deadline or change the required form. Anew deadline during an election year will cause undue hardship for Election Judges who already have a difficult role. To create additional confusion and possible conflict at polling places when citizens are denied entry will only complicate existing challenges. For these reasons, the SBE should not change the process for watchers and challengers. Sincerely, Shelley M.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202843+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0f1e2f0a-fb6e-4962-9af9-7322bebd83be", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 297, "text": "Anew deadline during an election year will cause undue hardship for Election Judges who already have a difficult role. To create additional confusion and possible conflict at polling places when citizens are denied entry will only complicate existing challenges. For these reasons, the SBE should not change the process for watchers and challengers. Sincerely, Shelley M. Aloi Frederick Republican County Central Committee https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035782536/thread/866699000035782540?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:42 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035783822/thread/866699000035783826?action.... Message ID <CAKgMu33CRh6YTX60LG5PHK3g-zGG+XQ7af2r GSMQxDWthvh8tA@mail.gmail.com> From \"Kelly McGuire ee To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Ce a a Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 05:54 PM (Incoming) Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Thread Subject Request to Maintain Existing Challenger and Watcher Process Return-Path eS | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectfully, Kelly McGuire, Treasurer Frederick County Republican Central Committee https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035783822/thread/8666990000357838267?action=print 1/1 2/13/26, 4:43 PM Message ID From To Bcc Date Subject Thread Subject Return-Path kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/86669900003578237 1/thread/866699000035782375?action.... <CAKFGegfBzF MTctvncBw=+DX-L45VpnMgOOuuRweDYvkRKoTJKw@mail.gmail.com> \"Kate Strauch Sullivan <undisclosed-recipients:> <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 04:49 PM (Incoming) No to Challengers and Watchers regulation change No to Challengers and Watchers regulation change State Board Of Elections Members- | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new regulations to the designation process. Among many issue | have with the regulation change, the most egregious the new imposed deadline.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202856+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6d64948b-baf5-4aa9-8bf7-7fd37ac7b656", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 299, "text": "Among many issue | have with the regulation change, the most egregious the new imposed deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not pass this regulation change. Respectfully submitted, Kate Strauch Sullivan Baltimore County Central Committee “The truth is like a lion; you don’t have to defend it. Set it free; it will defend itself.” This electronic mail message is intended exclusively for the individual or entity to which it is addressed. This message, together with any attachment, may contain confidential and privileged information. Any unauthorized review, use, printing, copying, retention, disclosure or distribution is strictly prohibited. If you have received this message in error, please immediately advise the sender by reply email message to the sender and delete all copies of this message. https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035 78237 1/thread/866699000035782375?action=print 11 2/13/26, 4:44 PM kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/866699000035779857/thread/866699000035779861 ?action.... Message ID <019701dc947f$8bfccbfO0 $a3f663d0$@gmail.com> From \"Dennis Evans To <info.sbe@maryland.gov> Date Mon, Feb 2, 2026 at 03:07 PM(Incoming) Subject Challenger and Watcher process Thread Subject Challenger and Watcher process Return-Path Po | strongly request that the State Board of Elections keep the Challenger and Watcher process unchanged and not impose any new deadline. The signed form from a designated entity should remain sufficient. Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202870+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d1dff4ca-982d-4a1b-aec0-2e2ca996a0ff", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Public Comments Challenger Watcher.pdf", "title": "Public Comments Challenger Watcher", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 13, "word_count": 60, "text": "Adding a new deadline would be unreasonable, create confusion, and lead to unnecessary conflict at polling places when citizens—unaware of the change—are denied entry despite having valid documentation. This would place an unfair burden on Election Judges and disrupt orderly election administration. For these reasons, the SBE should not add this deadline. Respectively: Dennis W Evans https://kb.vote.md.gov/agent/marylandstateboardofelections/all/tickets/details/8666990000357 7985 7/thread/866699000035779861?action=print 11", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.202874+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b5842d50-05fe-4d41-93a0-dd1fb23e3f3d", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Request for Public Comment on 2_19_Redacted.pdf", "title": "Request for Public Comment on 2_19_Redacted", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 1, "word_count": 168, "text": "2/17/26, 9:05 AM State of Maryland Mail - Re: Request for Public Comment on 2/19\ninactive voters continue to be labeled “active,” especially when voter status is central to the list-\nmaintenance process.\nI respectfully ask the Board to explain how its current list maintenance practices allow clearly inactive\nand ineligible registrations to remain active for years, and to outline what specific corrective actions will\nbe implemented to ensure timely, lawful voter roll maintenance going forward.\nThank you.\n“The truth is like a lion; you don’t have to defend it. Set it free; it will defend itself.”\nThis electronic mail message is intended exclusively for the individual or entity to which it is addressed. This\nmessage, together with any attachment, may contain confidential and privileged information. Any unauthorized\nreview, use, printing, copying, retention, disclosure or distribution is strictly prohibited. If you have received this\nmessage in error, please immediately advise the sender by reply email message to the sender and delete all copies\nof this message.\nhttps://mail.google.com/mail/u/0/?ik=aa30e29583&view=pt&search=inbox&permmsgid=msg-a:r7462786473785386152&dsqt=1&simpl=%23msg-f:185… 2/2", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.223901+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "10494eed-13ea-4098-8a59-0e42768bbf23", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM.pdf", "title": "Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 4, "word_count": 282, "text": "Maryland Association of Election Officials Representing the Local Election Boards of the State of Maryland February 12, 2026 Michael Summers, Chairman, State Board of Elections Dear Chairman Summers and Board Members, The Maryland Association of Election Officials (MAEO) is the professional membership organization representing local election officials and election boards across all 24 jurisdictions in the state. Our members are dedicated to ensuring the smooth and effective administration of elections, and we are deeply committed to making Maryland elections accessible, secure, and fair. The MAEO Board and membership met to discuss the proposed regulations that govern Challengers and Watchers, COMAR 33.07.07.01 through .03. During the meeting, the MAEO Board in attendance voted in support of submitting these comments. In addition, all local boards in attendance (21) voted in support of submitting these comments. Currently, COMAR 33.07.07.01D(4) states: “A designating entity shall provide the local board with: (a) Copies of completed certificates; or (b) A list of individuals designated as challengers and watchers.” This regulation has always been in place, but it has not been followed consistently. In our experience, it is very rare that designating entities provide the local board with copies of completed certificates or a list of individuals who have been designated as challengers and watchers. In the proposed regulation, a deadline was imposed to make sure that designating entities comply with this regulation. The deadline would allow election offices to notify the chief judges assigned to the voting location to expect a challenger and watcher specifically by their name. It also allows the election office to keep a copy of the challenger and watcher form for our records in the case that an emergency happens at a voting location.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.431376+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "f88d24e9-e25c-47a6-b4ab-7bd19910db5b", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM.pdf", "title": "Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 4, "word_count": 229, "text": "The deadline would allow election offices to notify the chief judges assigned to the voting location to expect a challenger and watcher specifically by their name. It also allows the election office to keep a copy of the challenger and watcher form for our records in the case that an emergency happens at a voting location. This is not an effort to prevent or hinder Challengers and Watchers from being at voting locations, but an effort to make sure that we can prepare and accommodate their presence so that they can best further their mission. The safety and security of election judges, staff, voters, equipment, and voting locations is one of our highest priorities. Election judges are identified, assigned, and trained in advance. Voters and candidates are subject to rules and regulations of proper decorum. In addition, board members and staff members are identified in the voting location upon entry. We believe that challengers and watchers should be included in our process to ensure a safe and efficient voting area. Passage of the proposed deadline in the final regulation will provide us with proper documentation and confidence that whoever is in the voting location is safe. We would like to thank the State Board for accepting our comments on these proposed final regulations. On behalf of MAEO, we kindly ask that you reconsider the passage of these final regulations.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.431392+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "88730e54-032e-4061-9104-890da5687ad1", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM.pdf", "title": "Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 4, "word_count": 210, "text": "Passage of the proposed deadline in the final regulation will provide us with proper documentation and confidence that whoever is in the voting location is safe. We would like to thank the State Board for accepting our comments on these proposed final regulations. On behalf of MAEO, we kindly ask that you reconsider the passage of these final regulations. Sincerely, Erin Perrone Stephanie Taylor MAEO, President MAEO, Vice-President Carroll County Harford County Abigail Goldman Tae Frere MAEO, Secretary MAEO, Treasurer Baltimore City Saint Mary’s County Barry Jackson Jessica Noranbrock MAEO, Board Member MAEO, Board Member Washington County Dorchester County Reider White Teresa Womble MAEO, Board Member MAEO, Board Member Anne Arundel County Howard County cc: Jared DeMarinis, State Administrator Katherine Berry, Deputy State Administrator Audit trail Details Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2/12/26, FILE NAME 9:06 PM STATUS Signed 2026/02/13 STATUS TIMESTAMP 16:04:21 UTC Activity erine.perrone@maryland.gov sent a signature request to: Stephanie L. Taylor (stephanie.taylor@maryland.gov) Barry E. Jackson (barry.jackson@maryland.gov) Abigail Goldman (abigail.goldman@baltimorecity.gov) 2026/02/13 Erin Perrone (erine.perrone@maryland.gov) SENT 02:07:40 UTC Reider White II (reider.white@maryland.gov) Tae M Frere (tae.frere@maryland.gov) Jessica Noranbrock (jessica.noranbrock@maryland.gov) Teresa Womble (teresa.womble@maryland.gov) 2026/02/13 Signed by Erin Perrone (erine.perrone@maryland.gov) 02:09:13 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Stephanie L. Taylor (stephanie.taylor@maryland.gov) 16:04:21 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Barry E.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.431403+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "973b9eac-6347-410e-a8b1-ff282f8646a8", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM.pdf", "title": "Signed Letter to SBE_COMAR 33.07.07_Challengers & Watchers_MAEO Considerations.docx - 2_12_26, 9_06 PM", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 4, "word_count": 152, "text": "Taylor (stephanie.taylor@maryland.gov) Barry E. Jackson (barry.jackson@maryland.gov) Abigail Goldman (abigail.goldman@baltimorecity.gov) 2026/02/13 Erin Perrone (erine.perrone@maryland.gov) SENT 02:07:40 UTC Reider White II (reider.white@maryland.gov) Tae M Frere (tae.frere@maryland.gov) Jessica Noranbrock (jessica.noranbrock@maryland.gov) Teresa Womble (teresa.womble@maryland.gov) 2026/02/13 Signed by Erin Perrone (erine.perrone@maryland.gov) 02:09:13 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Stephanie L. Taylor (stephanie.taylor@maryland.gov) 16:04:21 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Barry E. Jackson (barry.jackson@maryland.gov) 13:01:06 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Reider White II (reider.white@maryland.gov) 13:09:52 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Teresa Womble (teresa.womble@maryland.gov) 16:02:28 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Abigail Goldman (abigail.goldman@baltimorecity.gov) 12:43:10 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Tae M Frere (tae.frere@maryland.gov) 13:49:16 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 Signed by Jessica Noranbrock (jessica.noranbrock@maryland.gov) 13:25:05 UTC SIGNED 2026/02/13 This document has been signed by all signers and is complete 16:04:21 UTC COMPLETED The email address indicated above for each signer may be associated with a Google account, and may either be the primary email address or secondary email address associated with that account.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:40.431410+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "6a66dafa-4310-4a57-8adf-50c65313c0f2", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 271, "text": "State Administrator's Report February 19, 2026 Important Announcements and Meetings On February 4, Jared DeMarinis attended the opening of the exhibition “Ballot Boxes of the Americas: History and Symbols of Democracy” at the headquarters of the Organization of American States (OAS). Maryland provided three pieces for the exhibit. One was a 1800’s glass ballot box from Carroll County. The NASED (National Association of State Election Directors) Winter Conference occurred on February 1-3. There were public presentations on the use of AI by election offices, VVSG updates, USPS plans for the election year, and a SAVE system briefing. On February 11, Jared DeMarinis spoke at a League of Women Voters of Anne Arundel County regarding the upcoming election, tactics to combat disinformation and increase voter participation. Legislation All of the departmental bills have been heard by the respective committees. Additionally, Jared DeMarinis and Katie Berry did an election briefing in front of the Labor, Elections, and Government committee. It was well received. Candidacy and Campaign Finance (CCF) Division Between January 14 and February 13, 2026, 174 new committees were established. 2026 Gubernatorial Elections SBE has filed 410 candidates so far. The candidate filing deadline is February 24, 2026, at 9:00 PM. Every candidate who filed in 2025 must file another financial disclosure statement in 2026 in order to appear on the ballot. SBE has continued to send notices to those candidates. The candidates have until the withdrawal deadline, February 26, 2026 to file a current financial disclosure statement. MD CRIS The first report in the new MD CRIS system was the Annual 2026 Report, due on Wednesday, January 21, at 11:59 PM.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127719+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a959bbe8-fb2b-4b61-876b-bd38bfd596a1", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 297, "text": "SBE has continued to send notices to those candidates. The candidates have until the withdrawal deadline, February 26, 2026 to file a current financial disclosure statement. MD CRIS The first report in the new MD CRIS system was the Annual 2026 Report, due on Wednesday, January 21, at 11:59 PM. Annual 2026 Filing Performance: ● Total Filers Assigned the 2026 Annual Report: 2,119 ● On-Time Filing: 86% ● Total Filed: 93% (1,814 on-time filers, 109 late filers, 196 non-filers Page 1 From January 15th to January 22nd, 2026, leading up to the filing of the Annual 2026 Report, the CCF Division hosted a virtual Filing Hotline Support via Zoom for filers. The feedback for this open Filing Hotline Support was overwhelmingly positive. Filing Hotline Support: ● Total Callers: 1,044 ● Total Assistance Time: 2,798 minutes, equivalent to 46 hours and 38 minutes of direct support ● Peake Activity: Jan 21st with 340 callers and 887 minutes of support Public Outreach The CCF Division conducted 32 training sessions, offering hands-on instruction to 541 participants. These sessions were designed to ensure that every user is well-prepared for their submissions by covering essential aspects of the reporting process and providing direct technical support. On Monday, February 10, the candidacy staff was invited to speak at the African American Democratic Leadership Council. The presentation focused on the requirements for filing for office in the 2026 gubernatorial election, aimed at candidates or potential candidates. Starting on November 10, CCF executed a proactive outreach campaign to ensure compliance among businesses employing lobbyists. A notice was sent to entities registered with the Maryland State Ethics Commission but not yet with the State Board of Elections. Due to this outreach, 245 businesses have since successfully registered on the Maryland Business Contribution Disclosure System (MD BCDS).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127745+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c6661b32-4160-49d9-8be6-35bb5e602ac8", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 296, "text": "Starting on November 10, CCF executed a proactive outreach campaign to ensure compliance among businesses employing lobbyists. A notice was sent to entities registered with the Maryland State Ethics Commission but not yet with the State Board of Elections. Due to this outreach, 245 businesses have since successfully registered on the Maryland Business Contribution Disclosure System (MD BCDS). On January 12, 2026, we hosted a focused press event and showcase for members of the media, university representatives, and independent researchers to demonstrate the capabilities of the newly upgraded MD CRIS and MD BCDS. This event has a strong turnout with 23 attendees participating both in person and virtually. On February 9, 2026, Allen Norfleet was a featured speaker at a journalism class at the Philip Merrill School of Journalism at the University of Maryland. The main topic of discussion was Maryland Campaign Finance law. Public Financing (PF) Currently, there are 52 active public financed (PF) committees. As of February 12, twenty-three public financed candidates are Certified/Qualified. One even maxed out on the initial Matching Fund Report (MFR). Currently, there is $10,587,985.42 in the State Fair Campaign Fund. This means the program can fully fund 2 Primary candidates and 1 candidate in the General. No additional funding is needed. Enforcement Listed below are the civil penalties paid in January 2025. The total amount collected is $675: a. Carmody, Jack for District 8 - $125 - Failure to include authority line or authority line is Page 2 insufficient. b. LeCole, Kenisha for Wicomico County Board Of Education (District 7)-Civil penalty - $50 - Failure to include authority line or authority line is insufficient. c. Finoh, Sia Friends of - $500 - Failure to maintain account books and records, Failure to include authority line or authority line is insufficient.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127761+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9efac7b4-7014-41a0-976a-7f8bdd808ffb", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 295, "text": "LeCole, Kenisha for Wicomico County Board Of Education (District 7)-Civil penalty - $50 - Failure to include authority line or authority line is insufficient. c. Finoh, Sia Friends of - $500 - Failure to maintain account books and records, Failure to include authority line or authority line is insufficient. Communications Social Media presence remains a priority to ensure votes know their elected officials are their trusted sources for election information. We are in the final stages of planning for an aggressive voter outreach campaign focusing on voter education. The campaign includes a landing page that allows people to upload the election calendar to an individual’s phone and provides information on how to register to vote, become an election judge and methods of voting. There will continue to be paid outreach focusing on election judge recruitment and voter education leading up to the 2026 Primary and General elections. There will also be resources provided to local boards for the use of organic social media content to supplement the outreach campaign. Internally, we will utilize their own organic social media content to target mis/disinformation through voter education materials and videos. LBEs had the opportunity to order a new 2026 “Maryland Voter” sticker celebrating the 250th anniversary of the Declaration of Independence in place of the typical “I Voted” sticker. The SBE Official Voter Resource app is in testing stages. We are hoping to launch the app later this month which will be a wonderful resource for voters during the 2026 elections. Social Media Highlights So far in 2026, we have seen a large increase in social media engagement. We have gained 558 new Facebook followers and 343 new Instagram followers. Our Instagram posts have reached almost 600 interactions, while our Facebook page has seen almost 1.3M interactions.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127772+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "5c2cff87-3095-4583-9352-5085d90caf0f", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 282, "text": "Social Media Highlights So far in 2026, we have seen a large increase in social media engagement. We have gained 558 new Facebook followers and 343 new Instagram followers. Our Instagram posts have reached almost 600 interactions, while our Facebook page has seen almost 1.3M interactions. One organic Facebook post featuring Election Dates was viewed over 200,000 times, liked by almost 3,000 people, and shared over 1,000 times. Our ads have also been drawing in a noteworthy number of engagements. The current ads we have running feature information encouraging voters to sign up to be election judges. Election Management & Compliance Correctional Facility Voting Per Election Law Article, §1-303.2(e), we submitted the annual JCR report to the Senate Education, Energy, and the Environment (EEE) Committee and the House Ways and Means Committee regarding the registration and voting activities in the detention centers of Maryland for 2025. Preparations for the 2026 election cycle are currently underway. We have been meeting with the Page 3 Department of Public Safety and Correctional Services (DPSCS) and local detention center staff to discuss our previous successes and how we can improve our efforts this election cycle. One of the ideas discussed was to explore options for providing candidate information through non-partisan sources. Lastly, we recently received a list of resource fairs that will be held this year. We are making plans to attend some of these to increase our voter outreach and education efforts. We are preparing the materials for the voting packet procurement. Our voting packets include a Hello Voter Letter, Voter Registration Application, Mail-in Ballot Application, and a postage paid return envelope. Prince George’s and Montgomery Counties will receive bilingual voting information and materials (English/Spanish).", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127784+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "7caad112-9409-44e0-b584-f377bf49eadb", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 291, "text": "We are making plans to attend some of these to increase our voter outreach and education efforts. We are preparing the materials for the voting packet procurement. Our voting packets include a Hello Voter Letter, Voter Registration Application, Mail-in Ballot Application, and a postage paid return envelope. Prince George’s and Montgomery Counties will receive bilingual voting information and materials (English/Spanish). As we did last election cycle, we will be using a SmartSheet that contains all of the information we use and share with the centers and local boards. This workspace includes a facility list with contact information, voting packet amounts, and all peripherals including forms, informational resources, and video. This was a helpful improvement for everyone involved. Contractual Staffing for Election The SBE Election Judge train the trainer program has been reviewed by core SBE staff, who provided valuable feedback in effort to continue to improve and evolve the program. The first round of train the trainer classes is scheduled to begin on February 17. After successfully completing the program, trainers will be deployed to Baltimore County, Charles & Frederick. This will be followed by the next round of local board training sessions. Transportation for Election SBE has conducted the kickoff meeting with the transportation team. Local board transportation meetings will begin in late March. The meetings will cover logistical details of the voting system transportation to polling places and early voting locations. Language Assistance Implementation: Anne Arundel County, Baltimore City, Baltimore County, Montgomery County, and Prince George’s County will provide language assistance in 2026 under the new state law. These counties will have signage and an interpretation service, Language Line Solutions, available at polling places, and translated voter registration and absentee ballot applications and voter guides posted to their websites.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127796+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c7e09808-8530-441e-8ca9-bce4bf66697d", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 297, "text": "Language Assistance Implementation: Anne Arundel County, Baltimore City, Baltimore County, Montgomery County, and Prince George’s County will provide language assistance in 2026 under the new state law. These counties will have signage and an interpretation service, Language Line Solutions, available at polling places, and translated voter registration and absentee ballot applications and voter guides posted to their websites. The counties are also making efforts to recruit bilingual Election Judges. If LBEs want to provide additional documents or help in more languages, they will submit a request using a form prescribed by SBE. Information Systems & Technology Applications Release 10.6 of MDVOTERS will be deployed on April 10, 2026. This release will be the last release before the primary election. The release will focus on enhancements of web delivery reporting and cure letter updates. Page 4 Information Security Cyber Awareness As the election nears, the cybersecurity team wants to put more emphasis on phishing attempts from bad actors as the phishing emails and texts are becoming more frequent and complex. Mail-In Voting (MIV) To address the ongoing concerns regarding postmarks, SBE is drafting enhanced language for Mail-In Voter materials that will 1.) emphasize that voters should return their ballot as early as possible; 2.) Alert voters that some USPS postmarks may be dated 1-2 days after the day the voter mails their ballot. To ensure their ballot is postmarked on or before Election Day, voters should request a hand-stamped postmark at a USPS facility and; 3.) recommend that voters in Maryland can use official ballot drop boxes. Ballots in drop boxes are retrieved by election officials at least daily, and drop boxes remain open until 8pm on Election Day Personnel Management Human Resources passed their DBM recruitment audit. Charles County is interviewing to fill their Deputy Director position.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127807+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "d1a7ee0f-2aa3-4c90-b2de-6d07fe0fb1e3", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 297, "text": "Ballots in drop boxes are retrieved by election officials at least daily, and drop boxes remain open until 8pm on Election Day Personnel Management Human Resources passed their DBM recruitment audit. Charles County is interviewing to fill their Deputy Director position. Many recruitments are being posted on behalf of the LBEs. Procurement Active Procurements 1.) The New Voting System (NVS): RFP D38B5600001 was published on June 27, 2025. On September 5, 2025 SBE received 4 offers in response to the solicitation. One Offeror withdrew their proposal. The RFP is planned for deployment in the 2028 election. The BAFO process will be underway this week. SBE sent an intent to award on February 3, 2026 to the Offeror unanimously selected by the evaluation committee as the top choice for Maryland. State Certification and debriefings of non-selected Offerors are currently underway. 2.) Managed Security Services: RFP D38B6600005- The MSS RFP for a managed security services provider was submitted for DoIT review in late January, 2026. The purpose of this solicitation is to procure a security contract that strengthens the agency's cybersecurity operations, engineering capabilities, and enterprise risk management. The selected contractor will provide managed security operations, specialized engineering expertise, and strategic advisory services to support the continued maturation of the agency's Cyber Security Division. 3.) MDVOTERS V Data Center: RFP D38B6600007- The MDVOTERS V hosting services RFP is in the final development stages. The purpose of this contract is for Data Center (DC) hosting services, including hardware for several election systems, network administration, and DC migration/relocation services, and a managed services provider for the data centers. The primary DC is also referred to as the Voter Registration Operations Center (VROC) throughout this document. Additionally requirements include the management and operations of the Continuity of Operations Center (COOP) data center.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127817+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "ba1f9c03-19b0-4ca1-b78a-02e661a20a85", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 283, "text": "The primary DC is also referred to as the Voter Registration Operations Center (VROC) throughout this document. Additionally requirements include the management and operations of the Continuity of Operations Center (COOP) data center. Page 5 Recent Awards: 1.) Statewide Pollbook Modernization: BPW approved SBE’s pollbook award on 12/3/2025. The contract started on January 15, 2026. 2.) Voter Registration Applications: It has come to SBE’s attention that the printing of the VRAs (by Taylor Print and Visual Impressions) does not meet the required standards provided in the solicitation. SBE is in the process of remedying the issue so please be on the lookout for new VRAs. 3.) Other supply orders made through MCE for you to please confirm delivery receipt: a.) I Voted Stickers b.) Orange Provisional Bags c.) Yellow Absentee Bags Requests for Information: 1.) Automated Ballot Tabulation Audit Contract: An RFI for information on additional vendor options for the Clear Ballot contract to determine if this is still a sole source or if other vendors have entered the marketplace was posted on eMMA on January 15, 2026. Responses are due on February 13, 2026. Voter Registration, Audits & Operations Internal Audits Monthly audits of voter registration data continue to verify compliance with federal and state laws governing voter registration. Critical Data Oversight Review Two virtual sessions were held to review and train local board staff members on the requirements and procedures for this required review of their peers. Both sessions were well attended and seemed to be well received and useful. Election Operations The Election Operations has finalized the Election Judge, Same Day Registration, and Address Change Manuals for the 2026 election cycle and provided these to all local boards of elections.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127828+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0349039d-23bb-4371-9995-ce29cbc12c5e", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 175, "text": "Both sessions were well attended and seemed to be well received and useful. Election Operations The Election Operations has finalized the Election Judge, Same Day Registration, and Address Change Manuals for the 2026 election cycle and provided these to all local boards of elections. We are in the process of reviewing each LBE’s customization of manuals and forms so that they are compliant and standardized. We continue to track election judge recruitment, pay rates, and training schedules in preparation for the 2026 Gubernatorial Primary. We continue to work with Voting Works and LBE stakeholders to implement Risk Limiting Audits for the upcoming 2026 election cycle in congruence with SB 313 (2025). This audit will replace the manual audit and complement our automated tabulation audit by verifying the outcome of the election prior to state certification. Registration & Election Management Database (MDVOTERS) ERIC Reports for January were received. NCOA (National Change of Address): 114,657 Page 6 Cross State: 13,085 In State Update: 44,879 The current total active registered voters as of February 9, 2026 was 4,308,629.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127836+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "9c4f5b2c-ef0c-4df0-8755-1b4e1f36401c", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026.pdf", "title": "State Administrator's Report- February 19, 2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 11, "word_count": 213, "text": "Registration & Election Management Database (MDVOTERS) ERIC Reports for January were received. NCOA (National Change of Address): 114,657 Page 6 Cross State: 13,085 In State Update: 44,879 The current total active registered voters as of February 9, 2026 was 4,308,629. Motor Vehicle Administration (MVA) Transactions During January 2026, MVA collected the following voter registration transactions: New Registration - 9,201 Residential Address Changes - 16,735 Last Name Changes - 2,651 Political Party - 5,935 Last 13 Months of MVA Transactions - Chart: MVA Transactions - Monthly Changes: New Address Last Political Registrations Changes Name Party Dec-2025 Month-to-Month - Change 26.2% 16.8% 30.4% 24.2% Same Month - Previous Year Change 1.3% 4.1% -24.3% -12.9% Same Month - 2 Years Ago Change 8.7% 25.3% 14.4% 30.2% Jan-2026 Month-to-Month - Change 9.4% 6.4% 10.5% 14.9% Page 7 Same Month - Previous Year Change -15.2% -17.7% -63.2% 62.1% Same Month - 2 Years Ago Change 4.8% -29.2% -24.0% 8.3% MSR- Removals Exercise of Delegated Duties We will use this section of the State Administrator’s Report to report when we have exercised duties assigned to the State Board of Elections but delegated to the State Administrator and staff. Since the last State Administrator’s Report: __x___ No delegated duties were exercised. _____ The following delegated duties were exercised. Page 8", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.127845+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "c7adf3d0-37aa-4d9c-8c4e-90bfd9e21efe", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 245, "text": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems Maryland State Board of Elections F ebruary 2026 V2.0 State Certification Testing for Voting Systems Introduction This document describes the requirements for vendors seeking state certification for voting systems. The State Board of Elections (SBE) will conduct all testing independently of the voting system vendor. The testing results, along with a recommendation by the State Administrator, will be presented to the members of the State Board of Elections who, during a publicly held State Board Meeting, will formally vote on whether the voting system will be certified. If the voting system includes a certified network component and an uncertified election night network component, only the portion of the voting system related to the certified network will be considered by the members of the State Board of Elections for certification. However, SBE will make a recommendation to the board based on the viability of the entire system which would include all components certified and otherwise. Please note, if SBE is going through an active procurement for a new voting system, only the system selected will go through the Maryland State Certification testing. Overview 1) Timeline – 60 days 2) Application a) Identification of System. The application shall specify clearly the specific equipment, hardware, firmware, and software for which certification is sought. b) Overview of System. The application shall provide a brief overview description of the voting system. Marketing brochures may be used for this purpose. c) Use in Other Jurisdictions.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.725978+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "529dc0ef-362e-4911-81c7-e1be0ac54e6e", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 272, "text": "Overview 1) Timeline – 60 days 2) Application a) Identification of System. The application shall specify clearly the specific equipment, hardware, firmware, and software for which certification is sought. b) Overview of System. The application shall provide a brief overview description of the voting system. Marketing brochures may be used for this purpose. c) Use in Other Jurisdictions. The application shall include a list of all jurisdictions where: i) The exact system for which certification is being sought, or a model or version of the system is in use, together with: (1) The price that was charged to the jurisdiction for the system, and (2) A breakdown of the per unit price and the price of any components. ii) The exact system for which certification is being sought, or a model or version of the system has been but is no longer in use; and iii) A request has been made for approval of the exact system for which certification is being sought or a model or version of the system, together with a statement of whether the request was granted, was denied, was withdrawn, or is pending. d) Qualification Certificate. If an independent testing laboratory has completed qualification testing on the voting system, the application shall be accompanied by a: i) Copy of the qualification certificate issued for the voting system; and ii) Signed authorization directing the independent testing laboratory that performed the qualification testing to: (1) Submit the results of its testing directly to the evaluation agent; and (2) Allow the evaluation agent or his or her designee full access to all test records and data. e) Certification Testing Transfer.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.725999+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "1a21ea46-31ee-4d08-90a2-c2916e4c526a", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 2, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 89, "text": "If the voting system has successfully completed certification testing by or for another state, the application shall be accompanied by a: i) Copy of the certification issued for the voting system; and ii) Signed authorization directing the agency that performed the certification testing to: (1) Submit the results of its testing directly to the evaluation agent, and (2) Allow the evaluation agent or his or her designee full access to all test records and data. 2 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems f) Application Fee.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726005+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b0df5908-dde8-4ae4-9ca6-274f1e4a6abf", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 3, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 357, "text": "The application shall be accompanied by a non-refundable application fee of $5,000. (COMAR 33.09.03.02(G)) 3) Submission:  Technical Data Package – This package must include:  Hardware schematic diagrams  Hardware theory of operation  Customer maintenance documentation  Operations manual  Software source code (both in the form of a listing and on a USB Flash Drive)  Hardening scripts (both in the form of a listing and on a USB Flash Drive)  Software system design  Customer documentation  A detailed list of any known and unresolved system problems, including those that have occurred in other jurisdictions.  When available, the report and test results of the independent testing laboratory  A full sample set of import files that demonstrate all features  Hashing Procedures  Authentic Hash Values for Comparison  A list of all third-party hardware, including make and model if applicable, required to utilize all components of the system (servers, workstations, etc.), a list of all third-party software required to install or operate the system and the terms of the license agreements for each identified software and the terms of the license agreements for all hardware and software identified in this bullet.  Two precinct-based scanners  One high speed central count scanner  Two accessible voting devices  Assistive devices for use by voters with disabilities with the accessible voting device  Ballot receptacle  Paper ballots for testing (see Functional Testing section below)  Audio ballots for testing  Election management software installed on appropriate computers and/or servers as recommended for use in the state  A list of all ancillary equipment (switches, routers, cables, etc.) and the terms of the license agreements for each  Completed Questionnaire – Parts 1 and 2  Business Information Package (see instructions below)  Affidavit of Anti-Bribery and Anti-Debarment (see instructions below)  Risk Limiting Audits (RLA) Documentation/Procedures (if available) Note: If SBE owns, leases or has in its possession hardware that can be upgraded, either third-party or from the voting system vendor, SBE may grant the vendor permission to use the equipment on site instead of supplying new or additional equipment.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726016+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "bec2045a-e22a-4334-bbff-f09e40a18a3f", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 4, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 176, "text": "SBE may also request that the equipment be reset by the vendor prior to the start of testing. 4) SBE Evaluation (which is anticipated to take 60 days): 3 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems a. Technical Review – SBE (or its agent) will conduct a technical analysis of the voting system by reviewing the technical data package and voting system software source code provided by the vendor with its certification package. b. Functional Review – SBE (or its agent) will conduct a functionality review of the voting system by: i. Conducting election testing on equipment and ballots provided by the vendor (see Functional Testing section below); and ii. Reviewing responses provided by the vendor on the questionnaire regarding functional capabilities of the voting system (part 2). 5) SBE will contact the applicant: a. When the application for certification has been received. b. When SBE is ready to begin the evaluation. c. If SBE discovers a deficiency in the application or submission; and d. If SBE has any questions during the evaluation.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726025+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b2ed367a-c08a-468b-b65c-97c8c75b6beb", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 5, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 253, "text": "When the application for certification has been received. b. When SBE is ready to begin the evaluation. c. If SBE discovers a deficiency in the application or submission; and d. If SBE has any questions during the evaluation. Standards a) State Standards – Established under § 9-102(d)(1) and (3) of the Election Law Article of the Annotated Code of Maryland. b) Federal Standards – i) If testing is complete, the vendor shall provide: (1) a qualification certificate from the EAC; (2) signed authorization directing the laboratory to submit the results to SBE and allow SBE full access to the laboratory’s test records; and (3) a copy of the report issued by the independent testing laboratory showing that the system meets the standards as set out in the applicable Voluntary Voting System Guidelines. ii) If testing is not complete, the vendor shall provide documentation from the EAC that the voting system has been submitted for qualification testing and a statement from the vendor that it reasonably anticipates that testing will be completed on or before September 5, 2026. c) Additional Considerations - Section 9-102(e) of the Election Law Article provides a list of additional factors that SBE shall consider during the certification process. Using the attached questionnaire (part 1), a vendor seeking certification shall indicate how its system addresses each of the additional factors for consideration. d) Accessibility Standards – i) The vendor shall certify that the system provides access to voters with disabilities as provided in § 9-102(f) of the Election Law Article.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726035+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "a1b9a449-f64b-478c-ac87-85e6c61c697a", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 6, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 297, "text": "Using the attached questionnaire (part 1), a vendor seeking certification shall indicate how its system addresses each of the additional factors for consideration. d) Accessibility Standards – i) The vendor shall certify that the system provides access to voters with disabilities as provided in § 9-102(f) of the Election Law Article. Using the attached questionnaire (part 1, #20), a vendor seeking certification shall indicate how its system meets the accessibility standards. ii) If the vendor has performed a usability and/or accessibility evaluation on the voting system version submitted for certification, the vendor shall provide a detailed description of the type of evaluation, the outcome of the evaluation and the agent who conducted the evaluation along with their credentials. If a usability and/or accessibility evaluation was not conducted, please explain why in the attached questionnaire (part 1, #21). 4 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems iii) In addition to the above referenced accessibility standards, SBE must also conduct an accessibility and usability evaluation of the voting system of the system and an evaluation by individuals representing a cross-section of voters with disabilities. See Election Law Article, § 9-102(h)(2). Voting system equipment provided by the vendor will be used for this evaluation and public demonstration. Public Demonstration A public demonstration, using the databases provided for this testing, will be held within the 60 days allotted for certification. The applicant is required to provide all equipment, software and resources required for the demonstration at no cost to SBE. These demonstrations will take place virtually while allowing for live public comments and responses from the voting system vendor, and in person at three neutral venues designated by the vendor and approved by SBE, starting on a Tuesday and concluding on a Thursday unless otherwise specified by SBE.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726046+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0dffb678-3375-4057-8370-7c2fc19358e7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 7, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 295, "text": "These demonstrations will take place virtually while allowing for live public comments and responses from the voting system vendor, and in person at three neutral venues designated by the vendor and approved by SBE, starting on a Tuesday and concluding on a Thursday unless otherwise specified by SBE. Specific details regarding dates and times will be provided within 30 days of SBE’s receipt of the application for certification. At least 2 weeks prior to the public demonstration, a walkthrough of the virtual public demonstration must be presented to SBE for review and approval. Please ensure that the paper ballot layout and the accessible ballot layout reflect your best practices as they will not be altered by SBE for the demonstration. For the public demonstration ballots, please use generic terms, phrases and selections. Functional Testing Please review these instructions carefully as failure to follow them may result in delayed testing and certification. In addition to the required equipment and supplies listed herein, the applicant is responsible for ensuring that any other supplies or equipment necessary for conducting a mock election are provided. Additionally, please ensure that any equipment and ancillary items provided to SBE for the purpose of this testing are labelled so that they can be distinguished from the other equipment and items that SBE may have on site. Instructions – Election Management System (EMS) 1. The applicant shall provide all equipment, including ancillary items, required to use the voting system’s EMS. 2. The applicant shall have the voting system EMS components installed and the system hardened and configured in the way that is recommended for use. 3. The applicant shall provide a list of settings included that are not recommended for use and include an explanation of why those settings are not recommended. 4.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726057+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "b2ebde18-9393-4d8e-8184-11eba14d4bbf", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 8, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 286, "text": "The applicant shall have the voting system EMS components installed and the system hardened and configured in the way that is recommended for use. 3. The applicant shall provide a list of settings included that are not recommended for use and include an explanation of why those settings are not recommended. 4. The EMS provided to SBE shall include a primary election database and a general election database. Below are the general configurations for the databases. For detailed configurations, refer to the 2022 ballots provided and the information below. a. Primary Election Database that includes: i. Configured for all voting equipment being proposed ii. English Only iii. Ballot Styles by Precinct iv. Ballot Sets for Republican, Democratic, Non-Partisan v. Include Reporting Groups – Early Voting, Election Day, Mail-In Ballots 1, Provisional, Mail-In Ballots 2 5 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems vi. “Official Ballot” must be printed on the header of the printed BMD ballot vii. All ballot Images captured if the system allows a. General Election Database that includes: viii. Configured for all voting equipment being proposed ix. Both English and Spanish – Within the contests, with the exception of the contest headers, English text must be separated by <space>/<space> Spanish text on the regular paper ballots but English Only and Spanish Only on the BMD ballots. Examples will be provided upon request. x. Ballot Styles by Precinct xi. Ballot Set for All Parties xii. Include Reporting Groups – Early Voting, Election Day, Mail-In Ballots 1, Provisional, Mail-In Ballots 2 xiii. “Official Ballot\\Papeleta Electoral Oficial” must be printed on the header of the printed BMD ballot xiv. All Images captured if the system allows Instructions – Ballots and Test Decks 1.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726068+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "49ebd416-dd42-4bbc-b673-98fddd5826fd", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 9, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 293, "text": "Ballot Styles by Precinct xi. Ballot Set for All Parties xii. Include Reporting Groups – Early Voting, Election Day, Mail-In Ballots 1, Provisional, Mail-In Ballots 2 xiii. “Official Ballot\\Papeleta Electoral Oficial” must be printed on the header of the printed BMD ballot xiv. All Images captured if the system allows Instructions – Ballots and Test Decks 1. A regular ballot test deck for each election database submitted and the settings used 2. An EVC-1 BMD test deck for each election database submitted, the instructions for creating them and the settings used 3. 20 blank ballots per style for the primary election database submitted 4. 5 blanks ballots per style for the general election database submitted 5. Screenshots of a BMD screen showing 1 column, 2 columns, 3 columns and 4 column layouts for a single contest 6. Any additional supplies required Instructions – Tabulators and Media 1. Provide 2 precinct tabulators configured for the primary election (1 from the EVC-1 poll and the other from the first poll listed) 2. Provide 1 high speed scanner configured for the primary election (Mail-In Ballots 1 poll) 3. Provide 25 blank and formatted media required for the system 4. Provide 2 media devices from the Mail-In Ballots 1 poll with results and that is ready to upload (at least 500 ballots with images) for each election being provided 5. Provide 2 media devices from the EVC-1 poll with results and that is ready to upload (at least 500 ballot images) for each election being provided Instructions – Accessible Equipment 1. Provide 2 accessible voting machines configured for the primary election (1 from the EVC-1 poll and the other from the first poll listed) 2. Provide 10 blank and formatted media devices Instructions – Public Demonstration 1.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726079+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "2430875b-088a-492c-8a58-3b97b9472ebc", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 10, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 295, "text": "Provide 2 accessible voting machines configured for the primary election (1 from the EVC-1 poll and the other from the first poll listed) 2. Provide 10 blank and formatted media devices Instructions – Public Demonstration 1. Virtual Demonstration - 6 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems a. Must provide SBE with a virtual walkthrough of the demonstration at least 2 weeks prior to the start of the demonstrations b. All public demonstrations must be recorded and those recordings must be provided to SBE in MP4 format within 48 hours c. Must allow for public comments/questions and vendor/SBE responses all of which should be provided to SBE in writing within 48 hours d. Demonstrate the functionality of at least 1 precinct based scanner e. Demonstrate the functionality of at least 1 accessible voting machine f. Must provide all ancillary items required g. Will take place from Tuesday to Thursday at a time specified by SBE 2. In-Person Demonstration – a. Vendor must locate and secure a central, ADA compliant, and neutral location b. The location must be near a public transportation route c. Must provide the ability for the public to schedule appointments d. Must provide at least 3 precinct based scanners e. Must provide at least 3 accessible voting machines f. Must provide all ancillary items required including but not limited to ballots, blank paper and supplies g. Will take place from Tuesday to Thursday, unless otherwise specified, at times specified by SBE Business Information Package The business information package shall include the information listed below. Each section must be clearly labeled and identified. If the vendor is not the manufacturer of the system, two business information packets – one for the vendor and one for the manufacturer – are required.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726090+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "4be5fcb4-37c3-4e4a-af0e-84350c10d329", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 11, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 300, "text": "Each section must be clearly labeled and identified. If the vendor is not the manufacturer of the system, two business information packets – one for the vendor and one for the manufacturer – are required. Additionally, please provide a redacted business information package for any requests under the Public Information Act. 1. History and description of the business, including: a. Year established, b. Products and services offered, c. Areas served, d. Branch offices, e. Subsidiaries, f. Parent companies, and g. Subsidiaries of parent companies; 2. Management and staff organization, including: a. Number of full-time employees by category, and b. Number of part-time employees by category; 3. The name and address of each: a. Officer of the business, and b. Person who is known to have a beneficial interest (as defined in State Finance and Procurement Article, §13-221, Annotated Code of Maryland) in the business or any parent company; 7 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 4. Financial history of business, including: a. A financial statement for each of the past 3 fiscal years, b. Gross sales in voting products and voting services for the past 3 fiscal years, and c. The percentage that those sales represent of all sales of the business and its subsidiaries; 5. Location and manufacturing capability of each facility that is or will be used to fabricate and assemble all or any part of the voting system for which certification is being sought; 6. Location and servicing capability, including any service limitations, of each facility that is or will be used to serve the voting system for which certification is being sought; 7. The legal and financial relationship among all vendors and manufacturers of the voting system and its various components; and 8. Anti-Bribery and Anti-Debarment Affidavit (attached) signed by the vendor.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726101+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "fb604a40-be46-4750-9384-c7d02df910e4", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 12, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 284, "text": "Location and servicing capability, including any service limitations, of each facility that is or will be used to serve the voting system for which certification is being sought; 7. The legal and financial relationship among all vendors and manufacturers of the voting system and its various components; and 8. Anti-Bribery and Anti-Debarment Affidavit (attached) signed by the vendor. If the vendor does not manufacture the voting system, an affidavit from the manufacturer must also be submitted. 8 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems Affidavit of Anti-Bribery and Anti-Debarment I am the (title) ________________________________________ and the duly authorized representative of (vendor/manufacturer) __________________________________________ and that I possess the legal authority to make this Affidavit on behalf of myself and the business for which I am acting. For purposes of this affidavit, the following terms have the meanings indicated. 1. \"Controlling stockholder\" means a stockholder who: a. Owns more than 25 percent of the voting stock of a corporation; or b. Notwithstanding the number of shares that the stockholder owns, has the power to direct or control the direction of the management or policies of a corporation. 2. \"Convicted\" includes an accepted plea of nolo contendere. 3. \"Principal\" means: a. A sole proprietor; b. A partner, officer, director, or controlling stockholder; or c. An employee directly involved in the process of obtaining contracts with public bodies. 4. \"Public body\" means: a. The United States; b. Any state, territory, or possession of the United States; or c. Any political subdivision of a state, territory, or possession of the United States. To the best of the vendor's/manufacturer's knowledge, this affidavit provides complete information on whether the vendor/manufacturer or any principal in the vendor's/manufacturer's business: 1.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726111+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0575e4d7-c770-4bd9-be08-8b377b02ebb7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 13, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 300, "text": "The United States; b. Any state, territory, or possession of the United States; or c. Any political subdivision of a state, territory, or possession of the United States. To the best of the vendor's/manufacturer's knowledge, this affidavit provides complete information on whether the vendor/manufacturer or any principal in the vendor's/manufacturer's business: 1. Has been convicted under the laws of any public body of bribery, attempted bribery, or conspiracy to bribe; 2. Has been convicted under the laws of any public body of: a. A criminal offense incident to obtaining, attempting to obtain, or performing a public or private contract, or b. Fraud, embezzlement, theft, forgery, falsification or destruction of records, or receiving stolen property; 3. Has been convicted under the laws of any public body of a criminal violation of an antitrust statute; 4. Has been convicted of a violation of the Racketeer Influenced and Corrupt Organization Act or of the Mail Fraud Act for acts in connection with the submission of bids or proposals for a public or private contract; 5. Has been convicted of conspiracy to commit any act or omission that would constitute grounds for conviction under any of the laws or statutes described in §C(1)—(4) of COMAR 33.09.03.07. 6. During the course of an official investigation or other proceeding, has admitted, in writing or under oath, an act or omission that would constitute bribery, attempted bribery, or conspiracy to bribe under the laws of any public body; 7. Has been found civilly liable under an antitrust statute of any public body for acts or omissions in connection with the submission of bids or proposals for a public or private contract; 8. Has ever been debarred or suspended from doing business with any public body or any agency, instrumentality, or unit of a public body; or 9.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726122+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "dd253434-57e1-4840-98f3-20b9da0e9d1f", "source_url": "local://2026-02/State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026.pdf", "title": "State Certification Testing for Voting Systems 2026- 2.9.2026", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 14, "chunk_total": 15, "word_count": 197, "text": "Has been found civilly liable under an antitrust statute of any public body for acts or omissions in connection with the submission of bids or proposals for a public or private contract; 8. Has ever been debarred or suspended from doing business with any public body or any agency, instrumentality, or unit of a public body; or 9. Is a principal, successor, assignee, subsidiary, or affiliate of any other person who has: a. Been convicted under any of the laws or statutes described in items 1 through 5 above, b. Made an admission described in item 6 above, 9 | P a ge State Certification Testing for Voting Systems c. Been found civilly liable for an act or omission described in item 7 above, or d. Been debarred or suspended as described in item 8 above. Provide information of convictions, admissions, civil liabilities, or debarments, or indicate that there is no information to report. I DO SOLEMNLY DECLARE AND AFFIRM UNDER THE PENALTIES OF PERJURY THAT THE CONTENTS OF THIS AFFIDAVIT ARE TRUE AND CORRECT TO THE BEST OF MY KNOWLEDGE, INFORMATION, AND BELIEF. Date: ____________ By: ____________________________________________ (Authorized Representative and Affiant) 10 | P a ge", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:41.726130+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "04b269d0-ba5b-4d76-bc0e-f708d437ced0", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 0, "chunk_total": 24, "word_count": 295, "text": "Jared DeMarinis Michael Summers, Chairman State Administrator Jim Shalleck, Vice Chairman Diane Butler Katherine Berry Deputy Administrator Victoria Jackson-Stanley Eric Bryant Memorandum TO: State Board Members FROM: Allen Norfleet, Director Division of Candidacy and Campaign Finance DATE: February 19, 2026 SUBJECT: Title 13 Waiver of late filing fees standards Enclosed are the waiver requests, which were submitted by campaign committees that have been assessed late filing fees. The attached Waiver Request Information Page contains an overview of each committee as well as the Administrator’s recommendation for Board approval on granting a waiver request. In the past the Board has considered the following facts in determining whether just cause exists to grant a waiver.  Administrative error of any kind on the part of the Division.  The lateness is due to extenuating circumstances, i.e. physical illness or death in the family.  The late report is the first late report and allows the committee to close, or contains minimal financial activity.  The fee will cause undue financial hardship, if the liability of the fine is the personal responsibility of the officers.  Computer problems occurred which made timely filing impossible. However, the filer still must have demonstrated a good faith effort to timely file. Prior to the meeting please review each waiver request. Note the recommendations that you may disagree with or have questions on that you would like to discuss. Pursuant to Election Law Article §13-337 (b) (3), the State Administrator has denied three waiver request. No Board action is required on the denials. As of February 13, 2026 SBE has collected $14,210.00 in late fees year to date. SBE collected $9,857.00 for the month of February in late fees. Please feel free to contact me at 410-269-2856 if you have any questions.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:43.996404+00:00"}
{"chunk_id": "0fb28e4c-9120-40c6-989b-9614353e03f7", "source_url": "local://2026-02/Title 13 Late Fee Waivers.pdf", "title": "Title 13 Late Fee Waivers", "section_hierarchy": ["2026-02"], "page_classification": "local_document", "chunking_strategy": "local_pdf", "chunk_index": 1, "chunk_total": 24, "word_count": 262, "text": "No Board action is required on the denials. As of February 13, 2026 SBE has collected $14,210.00 in late fees year to date. SBE collected $9,857.00 for the month of February in late fees. Please feel free to contact me at 410-269-2856 if you have any questions. FAX (410) 974- 2019 Toll Free Phone Number (800) 222-8683 151 West Street Suite 200 MD Relay Service (800) 735-2258 https://elections.maryland.gov Annapolis, Maryland 21401 1. Baker, Donald for Sheriff 2. Ball, Calvin Team, The 3. Blount, Danny M. Friends of 4. Branch, Chanel A. Friends of 5. Building Industry, Political Action Committee Maryland 6. Chase, Chris Friends of 7. Crawford, Jim Friends of 8. Crawford, Joe Citizens For 9. Danos(Sentman), Maureen Committee to Elect for ROW 10. Dedicated to Students Slate 11. Dudley, Chris (Charles) Committee to Elect 12. Dumais, Kathleen M. Friends Of 13. Fink, Derek Citizens To Elect 14. Flaim, Jeanette Friends of 15. Foltz, Nathan Citizens for Special Education Compliance 16. Handley, James Friends of 17. Heath, Dianne Committee for Board of Education 18. Hospital Association PAC, Maryland 19. Jones, Rachel R. Friends of 20. Martinez, Ashanti Friends of 21. Milburn, Sheila 4 St. Mary's 22. Mortgage Bankers and Brokers Association, Inc. PAC, Maryland 23. Planned Parenthood Advocates for DC, Maryland & NoVa PAC 24. Stephensbailey, Iyo Maryland for 25. Strickland Lucas, (JoWanda) for Register of Wills 26. Western Maryland Democratic PAC 27. Whiting, Derrick Neighbors of 28. Wiggins, David Anthony Citizens For 29. Young, Caylin Friends of Denied 1. Association of Health Underwriters PAC Maryland 2. Smith, Leslie Friends of 3.", "date_extracted": "2026-04-17T18:09:43.996426+00:00"}
